WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.440
Tim Ferriss: Hello, ladies and germs.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.650
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.660
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.710
I'm going to keep my preamble short because
I would like to get into the meat and potatoes

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.790
of this conversation that I've been looking
forward to.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.430
My guest today is John Vervaeke on Twitter.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.000
You can find him @Vervaeke_John.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.990
He is a professor of psychology and cognitive
science at the University of Toronto.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.780
He currently teaches courses on thinking and
reasoning with an emphasis on cognitive development,

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.070
intelligence, rationality, mindfulness, and
the psychology of wisdom.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:37.270
Vervaeke is the director of UToronto's Consciousness
and Wisdom Studies Laboratory and its Cognitive

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.450
Science program where he teaches Introduction
to Cognitive Science and the Cognitive Science

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.259
of Consciousness, emphasizing the 4E model,
which I'm sure we will get into, which contends

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:51.600
that cognition and consciousness are embodied,
embedded, enacted, and extended beyond the

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.600
brain.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.170
Vervaeke has taught courses on Buddhism and
cognitive science in the Buddhism, Psychology,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:58.229
and Mental Health program for 15 years.

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:03.830
He is the author and presenter of the outstanding
— that's what I'm adding — YouTube series

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:07.840
— I highly recommend — Awakening from
the Meaning Crisis and his brand new series,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:08.880
After Socrates.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.300
You can find all things John Vervaeke at johnvervaeke.com.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.330
That is spelled V-E-R-V-A-E-K-E, johnvervaeke.com.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:20.430
John, thank you so much for taking the time
today.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.060
John Vervaeke: Great pleasure to be here,
Tim.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.140
Great pleasure, indeed.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:28.819
Tim Ferriss: Could you please elaborate on
the four ways of knowing?

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:30.590
John Vervaeke: Sure.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.090
The idea about a taxonomy that has to be principled.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:38.560
And so what it is, is every one of these kinds
of knowing has a particular vehicle, a particular

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.250
result, a particular standard, and a particular
kind of memory.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.640
I'm not going through all of these things
to be pedantic.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.950
I'm going through to these to show that this
is a rigorous taxonomy, that this is like,

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:51.050
because you can cut up the world however you
want.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.920
But in the scientific taxonomy, you want it
to be very systematic and rigorous.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.720
The kind of knowing we're most familiar with,
and our culture is very fixated on, is propositional

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.720
knowing.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.190
This is knowing that something is the case.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:07.090
This is knowing that cats are mammals, knowing
that two plus two equals four.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.050
The standard of realness for that is truth.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:14.840
You're convinced that something is true and
it results in beliefs.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:21.120
You get true beliefs, and perhaps they become
true justified beliefs, et cetera.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.069
The kind of memory that's associated with
that is called semantic memory.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:27.830
For example, if I ask you, "Do you remember
learning that cats are mammals?

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.531
Can you tell me the day that that really dropped
for you?"

00:03:30.531 --> 00:03:32.000
And you go, "I don't know."

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.580
So that's semantic memory.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:37.280
The reason why I'm saying this is all of these
memories are distinct from each other, such

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.930
that it's possible to damage one without the
others being completely damaged.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.750
The next is procedural knowing.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.190
This is knowing how, this is knowing how to
kiss someone.

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:50.790
This is knowing how to kick a ball.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.180
This is knowing how to swim.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.370
This doesn't result in beliefs.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.519
This results in skills, and skills aren't
true or false.

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:01.299
Tim, do you know how to swim?

00:04:01.299 --> 00:04:02.319
Tim Ferriss: I do.

00:04:02.319 --> 00:04:03.319
John Vervaeke: Right.

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:05.390
Now, is swimming true or false right now?

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.790
No, you're misunderstanding.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:08.790
Skills aren't true or false.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:12.810
They apply or they don't, and they apply powerfully
or they don't.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.030
And so it's standard of realness is powered.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:19.480
Does our know-how empower us?

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.380
Then prosaically enough, psychologists called
a form of memory associated with this procedural

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.350
memory.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:30.470
Now each one of these, I'm going in an order
of dependence.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:33.780
The propositional depends on the procedural,
and we can get into that in a minute, but

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.840
I'll just go through the four first.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.690
The next is perspectival knowing.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.910
This is knowing what it's like to be.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:45.540
So, you know what it's like to be you now
in your state of mind in this situation.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.870
You know what you're foregrounding, what you're
backgrounding, you know what is salient for

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:50.870
you.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:52.389
You can sense how the salience is shifting
around.

00:04:52.389 --> 00:04:57.500
You got a sense of how you're fitted to this
situation.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:00.080
This doesn't result in skills or beliefs.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.940
It results in perspectives, and you know how
to take perspectives and you know what the

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:09.449
difference between perspectives are and how
valuable it is to have multi perspectives

00:05:09.449 --> 00:05:11.669
on a situation.

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:15.620
Sometimes in order to solve a problem, you
shift to a different perspective.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:19.890
And so perspectives aren't true or false,
they're not powerful or not, their standard

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.729
of realness is presence.

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:25.580
How present are you in this situation?

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.199
In fact, when people do VR work, they look
for this.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:32.330
How much do people feel like they're in the
game?

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:33.330
There's that.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:37.880
That's the perspectival knowing, that they're
really situated in it, that they really belong

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.910
to it, et cetera, et cetera.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.450
This has its own kind of memory called episodic
memory.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:47.199
So I can ask you perhaps, do you remember
what you did on your last birthday?

00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:48.590
Tim Ferriss: And the answer's yes.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:52.320
John Vervaeke: What you'll do is you'll remember
a particular perspective you had on a particular

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.260
situation and your state of mind in that situation.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.820
That's your episodic memory.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:02.980
Now below that, and this gets to what we were
talking about earlier, is participatory knowing.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.920
This is knowing by being.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.919
This is the kind of knowing that results from
how you and reality have been co-shaped to

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:13.380
fit together to generate those affordances.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.669
For example, the water bottle and I have both
been shaped by gravity.

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:23.390
We've both been shaped by electromagnetism
so that we can — there's an affordance there.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:28.750
We've both been shaped by a biological history,
which is niche construction.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.020
My ancestors evolved.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:35.120
They made tools that shaped the environment
that shaped my ancestors.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.790
Culture has shaped me in this technology,
made this and taught me how to drink out of

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:42.100
a water bottle.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:46.510
There's been all this co-shaping of me and
parts of the environment.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:51.290
So we fit together, we're niche together,
we belong together.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:52.660
Affordances are available to me.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:57.090
The idea, and then that's carried in this
very weird sense of memory that you probably

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:02.699
don't even recognize is it's your sense of
self, the sense of all of the identities you've

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:08.419
taken up and how they're somehow historically
narratively linked together to certain narrative

00:07:08.419 --> 00:07:11.000
histories of certain situations.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.740
Here's the proposal.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.229
You have participatory knowing.

00:07:15.229 --> 00:07:16.910
It's laying out affordances.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.650
When that's missing, you know it.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:22.860
Culture shock or when you're homesick or when
you're lonely, that participatory knowing.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:25.680
Tim Ferriss: John, could you do just define
affordances

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:34.270
John Vervaeke: The affordances are real relations
for how you can interact with the object and

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:37.220
how that object is available to you.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:40.789
This affords graspability, the water bottle
is graspable.

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:46.680
That's an affordance, a real relation of fittedness,
so me and the water bottle belong together.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:52.190
It makes itself available for certain interactions
and I can shape myself to interact with it

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:53.610
powerfully and appropriately.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:54.740
Did that work as an explanation?

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:55.919
Tim Ferriss: It did work.

00:07:55.919 --> 00:08:00.580
Could you perhaps give an example of an affordance
that would come into play with culture shock?

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:04.900
John Vervaeke: An affordance would come into
play with culture shock is there are certain

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:11.129
affordances about how you and other people
relate that tell you when conversation is

00:08:11.129 --> 00:08:14.590
available to you, when there's an affordance
for conversation.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.340
Notice that most of this is unconscious for
you.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.110
Notice even within your own culture.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:20.509
So Tim, how close should you stand to somebody
at a funeral?

00:08:20.509 --> 00:08:26.280
Tim Ferriss: I was just thinking about the
body spacing because I was just in Japan where

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:28.560
it's very, very different.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:33.130
I couldn't give you a discreet number, but
I was probably a feeling associated with it

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:35.030
where it goes from comfortable to uncomfortable.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.030
John Vervaeke: Right.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:39.279
You've got that sense of — and it wasn't
working in Japan.

00:08:39.279 --> 00:08:40.719
Tim Ferriss: Very different.

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:46.370
John Vervaeke: And so that way of connecting
to people, by shaping the literal personal

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.529
space between you and them was not available
to you.

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:50.770
That affordance wasn't there.

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:52.779
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that makes sense.

00:08:52.779 --> 00:08:54.970
John Vervaeke: Okay.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:09:02.070
The affordances are here, and then the perspectival
knowing makes certain affordances salient

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.440
to me, grabbing my attention, arousing my
metabolism.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.910
The floor is walkable for me right now, but
I don't need to walk right now.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:12.010
So it's not something that I'm paying attention
to, right?

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:17.530
But I bring it into my situational awareness
with the perspectival knowing, that situational

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:22.220
awareness tells me which skills I should activate,
which is procedural knowing.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:26.661
Then the procedural knowing puts me into the
causal relationships with the world that give

00:09:26.661 --> 00:09:28.380
me the evidence for my beliefs.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.520
So that's all the kinds of knowing.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:37.760
The point is, outside of the propositional,
these other kinds of knowing are built on

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:44.130
the way you are dynamically coupled to the
environment, and therefore, they harken back

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:47.890
to that participation, that contact we were
talking about.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:53.800
Tim Ferriss: I suppose it's this coupling
and interaction with the environment on a

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.850
few different levels in the sense that you
have your sort of skin encapsulated ego and

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:03.029
its interaction with what we perceive as external
elements.

00:10:03.029 --> 00:10:10.020
Then you have perhaps those who think of mind
and body in this Cartesian dualistic way.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:15.710
You have the interaction of the body with
the mind, which in some respects is the entire

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.930
reason for which the brain evolved in some
respects.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.370
Let me just come back to semantic memory.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:25.280
Could you give just another example of semantic
memory, because I don't think I grasped that?

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:26.480
John Vervaeke: Oh.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.040
So if I ask you, "Are dogs fishes?"

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:30.140
and what do you say to me?

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:31.450
Tim Ferriss: I say no.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:32.970
John Vervaeke: That's semantic memory.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.380
Did anybody ever sit you down, you remember
it?

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:41.000
Do you have an episodic memory of, "I remember
when I learned that dogs weren't fishes?"

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:46.040
It's not bound up with your particular perspective
on a situation or the state of mind you are

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.040
in.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:50.180
It is this abstracted fact, and that's what
propositions are about.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:52.060
They're about these abstracted facts.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:53.709
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:10:53.709 --> 00:10:57.720
I've read somewhere, and I can't believe everything
I read on the internet, so I'd love for you

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:00.279
to fact check this.

00:11:00.279 --> 00:11:09.480
Is there some interplay between what people
experience as flow states and the activation

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:13.570
or use of multiple forms of knowing?

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:15.160
Or did I just pull that out of the air?

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:16.209
John Vervaeke: No.

00:11:16.209 --> 00:11:19.170
I have argued for that thesis.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:23.710
I don't know if that makes it a fact, but
I would definitely argue on its behalf.

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:29.260
I think, well, I've published and done work
on the flow state, and I think the flow state

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:36.100
is an example of, in our experience, a prioritization
of the procedural, the perspectival, and the

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.529
participatory over the propositional.

00:11:38.529 --> 00:11:42.620
In fact, one of the things people reliably
report is that whole propositional processing

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.120
drops out.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.550
Even that narrative nanny, the thing in your
head that's like, "How am I doing?

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:48.550
How do I look?

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:49.550
Do people like me?

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:50.740
How am I doing?

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:51.740
Maybe I should lose some weight.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:52.740
How am I doing?

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:54.270
How's my hair," that all falls away.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:56.270
Because you're so — well, this goes to what
I was saying a few minutes ago.

00:11:56.270 --> 00:12:01.130
There's this enlivening, enriching of a sense
of contact.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:05.290
One of the defining features of the flow state
is people feel at one with their environment.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:08.900
They feel dynamically coupled to it.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.000
So that's participatory.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.209
They feel at one with it.

00:12:11.209 --> 00:12:14.660
And the perspectival, everything is super
salient.

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:20.050
There's an ongoing sense of discovery of even
almost like insight in — well, I've argued

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:25.089
in insight cascade, you're getting even more
like the perspective, you're getting super

00:12:25.089 --> 00:12:26.089
present.

00:12:26.089 --> 00:12:30.890
Then of course the procedurality, is you just
know how to do it.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.300
Hence the word, it just flows from you.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:39.640
What's really important, Tim, is that the
flow state is optimal for people.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:43.120
They think they're at their best with good
reason.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.041
They'll say it's one of the best experiences
they had, one of the most rewarding, even

00:12:47.041 --> 00:12:48.240
more rewarding than pleasure.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:54.330
And so much so that asking people how frequently
they get into the flow state is a good predictor

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:57.320
of their sense of wellbeing.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.790
And so here's the point I want to make about
that.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:04.790
This is a clear example of that non-propositional
connectedness.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:11.540
Flow is about really realizing the transjectivity,
that connectedness, and how much it contributes

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:15.490
to a sense of agency and meaning for people.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:19.800
Tim Ferriss: I think we're going to jump in
the deep end of the meaning pool shortly.

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:26.910
Let me ask you first though, given what you
just said, how highly do you prioritize getting

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:28.750
into flow states yourself?

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:36.500
And if the answer is reasonably high or higher,
how do you personally like to get into flow

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:37.500
states?

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:41.040
John Vervaeke: I've said this, and I mean
this deeply, and I don't mean it as a trivial

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:42.170
commodification.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:46.470
Daoism is the religion, philosophy of flow.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:57.870
I've been practicing Daoist practices, Tai
Chi Chuan, Zhan Zhuang, Yi Chuan, Qigong.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:02.440
I got professional training and practice for
10 years as a Shiatsu therapist.

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:04.600
I've done it all.

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:06.410
Well, not at all, that's pretentious.

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:07.870
But I've done a lot.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:09.699
Okay, that's fair.

00:14:09.699 --> 00:14:14.399
What those things do, and this is something
I want to talk to you about at some point,

00:14:14.399 --> 00:14:18.290
they get me into a flow state, but in a particular
way that I think is really important.

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:20.019
And this goes to a story I've told.

00:14:20.019 --> 00:14:24.440
I was in grad school and I'd been doing Tai
Chi Chuan for about three or four years.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:28.090
I was doing it really religiously, both in
the sense of devotedly, and I was doing it

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:33.230
two or three hours a day and going to the
dojo three or four times a week kind of thing.

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:36.329
Some of my fellow students came up to me and
said, "What's going on with you?

00:14:36.329 --> 00:14:37.329
What are you doing differently?"

00:14:37.329 --> 00:14:41.660
I'm like, "Oh, you're worried in grad school
that people are going to find you out."

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.589
And so I said, "I'm doing Tai Chi Chuan."

00:14:43.589 --> 00:14:46.360
Well, before I said that, and I said, "Well,
why are you saying that?"

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:51.310
And they said, "Well, because you're just
— in conversations, you're much more flowing

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:55.320
and you're much more flexible and you're much
more balanced and you're much more receptive.

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:56.320
And you seem to..."

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:03.750
And it was like, "Oh, the flow state that
I'm getting into in Tai Chi is permeating

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:08.500
many different domains of my life and many
different levels of my mind."

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:12.820
I thought, ah, and this is a question that
has become a question to me.

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:19.120
What are the contexts that generate flow that
transfer like that powerfully, versus the

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.449
context where the flow stays locked into that
particular context?

00:15:22.449 --> 00:15:24.500
Tim Ferriss: That is a great question.

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:25.570
That is such a good question.

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:27.680
So where have you gone with that?

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:32.880
John Vervaeke: Well, I've gone with that,
well, in several different directions, and

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.220
it's very much a work in progress.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.579
I asked for some charity on the part of listeners.

00:15:36.579 --> 00:15:39.430
This is something I'm very much working on
right now.

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:44.460
I'm glad you think it's a great question because
even if you get a good question that then

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:48.839
you're a good scientist, then you're doing
something.

00:15:48.839 --> 00:15:56.940
One of the things I think has to do with whether
or not the skill or the skillset that's being

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:06.420
trained in the particular practice pick is
relatively indispensable in domains other

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:08.600
than the situation you're in.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:13.930
Now, ideas about sensory motor movement and
balance and coordination and getting the cerebellum

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:18.319
and the frontal cortex to talk better to each
other, that transfers broadly.

00:16:18.319 --> 00:16:23.800
We've already got a lot of evidence about
that, especially the cerebellum cortex loop.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:28.290
Even when people are meditating, the cerebellum
is firing like crazy because of the cerebellum

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:29.680
cortex loop.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:33.279
And so if it does that, then it could even
shows up in the metaphors.

00:16:33.279 --> 00:16:38.790
We talk about balancing equations and justice
being the balancing of things and scales.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:46.050
And so you want to find a set of skills that
has that inbuilt potential.

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:52.759
Then what I would want to say is the degree
to which there has been a framework built

00:16:52.759 --> 00:17:02.410
around the situation, a ritual philosophical
framework that helps to afford the translation.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:07.699
So around the Tai Chi Chuan, you have Daoism,
which gives you this way of framing reality

00:17:07.699 --> 00:17:13.819
as yin and yang that you can see instantiated
in the Tai Chi, but you can also see it prevalent

00:17:13.819 --> 00:17:14.819
in the world.

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:18.410
And so you set up the possibility with this
framework for the resonance.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:22.820
And so people who get trapped, and I'm not
saying that all video games trap people, but

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:28.559
the ones that suffer from video game addiction,
they get the problem that they flow in a world

00:17:28.559 --> 00:17:34.530
that does not have a framework around it,
it does not tap into those generally transferrable

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:35.530
skills.

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:40.190
And so they find the external world depressing
because of its absence of flow potential that

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:42.710
drives them into the game where they flow.

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:45.950
And then you get the vicious cycle that gets
you into the addiction.

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:50.720
I think there's something about what are the
kinds of skills that are being brought into

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:51.720
the flow state.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:54.410
Do they have this domain general power?

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:59.679
Is there a framework around it that helps
people set up these patterns of resonance

00:17:59.679 --> 00:18:04.770
between what they're doing in the practice
and how they're seeing the world and encountering

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:05.770
it?

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.480
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it makes a lot of sense
to me.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:16.270
If there is some overarching philosophical
framework or religious framework for that

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:21.080
matter within which you're practicing a given
skill, there's a broader context that then

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:26.929
extends whatever you're honing in this specific
situation to other domains.

00:18:26.929 --> 00:18:30.010
It makes me think actually of a friend of
mine, his name is Joshua Waitzkin.

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:34.460
He was the basis for Searching for Bobby Fischer,
the book and the movie, of course, very, very

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:35.690
high-level chess player.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:37.100
But he talks a lot.

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:38.630
He no longer plays chess.

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:39.630
He does a million other things.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:42.820
He was actually world champion in Tai Chi
Push Hands.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:45.820
And what I find most, I shouldn't say most
interesting.

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:48.550
He's a lovely, deeply soulful guy.

00:18:48.550 --> 00:18:52.500
It's not that I'm looking at him as a sort
of instrument of learning mastery, but he

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:58.630
is able to take concepts from all these different
disciplines and apply them and transfers to

00:18:58.630 --> 00:18:59.630
all these other things.

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:06.860
For instance, in chess, he talks a lot about
practicing the macro in the micro, or learning

00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:07.990
the macro from the micro.

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:13.520
He'll say, "Yeah, chess people, often novices,
will, say, practice openings, but that's kind

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:18.059
of like copying from the teacher's answer
book."

00:19:18.059 --> 00:19:21.860
You're memorizing a lot in terms of declarative
knowledge, but you're not learning principles.

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:27.360
So he'll say, "Why don't you start with — let's
just say three or four or five pieces on the

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:32.230
board and you're going to practice manipulating
empty space or pulling into a vacuum."

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:38.450
Lo and behold, a lot of those same principles
transferred over to Tai Chi, and then he took

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:42.290
principles from Tai Chi and moved them over
into foil boarding.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:50.410
Is Tai Chi your go-to break-glass-in-case-of-emergency
tool in the department of flow states, or

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:53.530
do you have other approaches in the quiver?

00:19:53.530 --> 00:20:00.299
John Vervaeke: Yeah, and this comes from the
Socratic part of my framework, if you want

00:20:00.299 --> 00:20:01.299
to put it that way.

00:20:01.299 --> 00:20:03.049
That sounds very pretentious.

00:20:03.049 --> 00:20:06.140
Tim Ferriss: I'm into it.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:11.430
John Vervaeke: I have learned how to get into
the flow state when lecturing.

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:15.970
And that inevitably affords really good learning.

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:19.840
Of course, not for every student, that would
be claiming something that's impossible.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:23.570
No teacher is a great teacher to every student.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:29.470
But I mean I've won teaching awards, et cetera,
et cetera, and that's because of this.

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:36.309
Very often, students get drawn into it because
I encourage a lot of Socratic dialogue in

00:20:36.309 --> 00:20:37.309
the lecture.

00:20:37.309 --> 00:20:40.580
And so it very much often becomes a shared
flow state.

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:47.200
I become very interested in shared flow states
and what they mean for how we can unpack the

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:49.970
capacity for insight and reflection.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:51.820
So that's one of my other go-to areas.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:53.770
Tim Ferriss: So let's unpack that a little
bit.

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:58.710
So in lecturing, did you do that naturally
from the outset?

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:02.760
Kind of like Usain Bolt having just different
tendon attachments and so on, you can just

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:03.840
run fast.

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:12.190
So did that come out of the box for you or
was that shared flow state in the form of

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:14.850
lecturing a learned skill?

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:16.870
If so, how did you develop that?

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:20.960
John Vervaeke: So very much a cultivated thing,
and deliberately so.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:29.370
By nature, I'm almost pathologically shy and
so I didn't realize it was a lesson from Aristotle

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:36.150
at the time, but you cultivate your character
to compensate for your personality defects.

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:40.770
And so I adopted — I've read my report cards
from grade three, and they were calling me

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:42.500
mini teacher.

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:47.420
So taking on the role of the teacher, and
I was fortunate to have some really wonderful

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:52.059
teachers that allowed this, and to have classmates
that enjoyed it.

00:21:52.059 --> 00:21:54.659
Think of all the conditions of how that could
have gone wrong.

00:21:54.659 --> 00:21:59.580
So very fortunate, and that was how I started
to manage that situation.

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:00.950
So Tim —
Tim Ferriss: And manage that situation, you

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:02.640
mean manage your shyness by —
John Vervaeke: Yes.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:03.960
Tim Ferriss: Assuming the role of teacher?

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:04.960
John Vervaeke: Yes.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:05.960
Right.

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:07.260
The full persona and the role.

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:11.720
So Tim, this is close enough to teaching,
and I don't mean to put you in the role of

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:13.490
a student, I'm not saying that, but it's close
enough.

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:15.169
It's in proximity for transfer.

00:22:15.169 --> 00:22:17.260
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I'm a student.

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:18.260
John Vervaeke: Yep.

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:23.130
Well, anyways, it's comfortable, but if you
were to meet me if at a social party, I'm

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:26.400
at times indistinguishable from potted plants,
right?

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:32.400
Because when I don't have that role available
to me, my natural constitution comes out.

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:36.960
So it is very deliberately cultivated from
very, very early on.

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:41.900
And then as I started doing Tai Chi Chuan,
when I started in grad school hearing about

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:46.980
the bleed, that's when I started to teach
and lecture and I looked for it and I looked

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:52.320
for what broke it and what enhanced it, and
I slowly cultivated it more and more over

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:53.320
time.

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:55.450
Yes, it was very much a deliberate long-term
cultivation.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:56.960
Tim Ferriss: So what enhances it?

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:01.039
Because you take, let's just grab an example
out of the year, Harvard Business School.

00:23:01.039 --> 00:23:07.929
They have these case study methods and there
is ostensibly supposed to be a fair amount

00:23:07.929 --> 00:23:14.799
of interplay between lecture and student,
but I would imagine that not all professors

00:23:14.799 --> 00:23:20.870
or grad students, or whoever is doing the
teaching at the time would say they achieve

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:24.330
a flow state, even if they've experienced
it in other places like surfing.

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:28.600
So what have you found to be things that enhance
that for yourself and for the students?

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:32.660
John Vervaeke: So part of this, I'm going
to declare a bias because I have a published

00:23:32.660 --> 00:23:34.940
theory about what I think the flow state is.

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:38.840
I think it's an insight cascade and the enhancement
of implicit learning.

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:45.029
So in enhance, we cultivate better insight
capacities, we cultivate better intuition

00:23:45.029 --> 00:23:46.730
in an integrated manner.

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:51.880
Now, that ghost, so I'm stating the bias upfront,
but I think it's a good theory and I think

00:23:51.880 --> 00:24:00.880
it's well argued and well evidenced, but what
I mean by that is more and more I crafted

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:08.730
giving priority to the emergence of insight
or intuition in me as I was lecturing and

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:12.810
not sticking to the script so strongly, but
willing to bend.

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:13.810
So I use the jazz analogy.

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:19.559
I may have the script of the lecture, but
I can riff on it and I look and I hunger and

00:24:19.559 --> 00:24:21.870
sensitize myself for that.

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:28.650
And then when the students start to even demonstrate
proto insight, I will really call that out

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:33.341
in a positive manner and draw it out and they
get — and they start priming each other

00:24:33.341 --> 00:24:36.630
with insights and they start priming me and
I start priming them.

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:41.520
And so it's an easy thing to state and it's
a hard thing to do because when you're lecturing

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:47.179
and when I teach people how to lecture, I
actually try to say, I try to get them to

00:24:47.179 --> 00:24:51.860
make space for the jazz because what you do
when you're anxious, and one of the things

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:55.180
that really kills it a lot, it's PowerPoint,
right?

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:58.679
Because what you want is, "I need to say all
of these things and I need to be clear and

00:24:58.679 --> 00:25:00.880
I need to have everything follow."

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:08.780
And so you get really, you try to constrain
your anxiety with the tightness of this structure

00:25:08.780 --> 00:25:09.780
that you built.

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:16.000
And what that actually does is that undermines
the possibility of flow.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:20.220
And so it's like Tai Chi Chuan, right?

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:23.070
You want to be pung, you don't want to be
too yin, too yang.

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:28.710
You want loosen the structure enough so that
insight and intuition flow, but not so much

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:32.610
that the lecture becomes incoherent and you
find the sweet spot.

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:38.659
Tim Ferriss: How did you find Tai Chi, or
how did Tai Chi find you?

00:25:38.659 --> 00:25:47.309
John Vervaeke: I was brought up in a fundamentalist
Christian extended family, not just the nuclear

00:25:47.309 --> 00:25:51.070
family, extended family.

00:25:51.070 --> 00:25:55.799
And it's only later that I've come to realize
how traumatizing that was for me.

00:25:55.799 --> 00:26:01.820
And so when I was 15, I read a couple of books,
The Lord of Light and Siddhartha, Lord of

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:07.260
Light by Roger Zelazny, Siddhartha by Hermann
Hesse, Fifth Business by Robertson Davies,

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:08.960
and it really blew me open.

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:10.760
It really blew me open.

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:18.720
And so I left the Christianity, and like I
said, I've done some therapy and other things,

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:24.500
but religion, your mother religion is like
your mother tongue.

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:28.200
It shapes you in a way that nothing else ever
can, or your first love.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:32.440
You don't necessarily stay with that person,
but it shapes you in very interesting ways

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:35.039
in your relationships.

00:26:35.039 --> 00:26:41.350
And so if you'll allow me a metaphor, it left
a taste for the transcendent in my mouth.

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:48.690
And then I got to university and I took a
first year course in philosophy and I encountered

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:53.700
the figure of Socrates and the project of
wisdom and it was like, that's it.

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:55.429
And of course, that is still with me now.

00:26:55.429 --> 00:26:58.010
That's how profound it hit me, how profoundly.

00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:00.420
But what happened — and then I went into
philosophy.

00:27:00.420 --> 00:27:04.620
What happened in philosophy, academic philosophy,
now it's changing now, but we're talking about

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:10.250
a long time ago, the '80s, the topic of wisdom,
self-transcendence, all these kinds of things

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:16.539
that are rich in the Platonic corpus and the
right and the Neoplatonic, it drops off the

00:27:16.539 --> 00:27:22.540
table and you talk about skepticism and epistemology
and these are valuable metascientific and

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:26.871
metacultural skills and I value them, but
that hunger for the cultivation of wisdom

00:27:26.871 --> 00:27:28.309
was not being met.

00:27:28.309 --> 00:27:34.620
And I happened to live in a place where literally
down the road there was the Tai Chi and Meditation

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:35.620
Center.

00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:36.620
So I thought, "You know what?

00:27:36.620 --> 00:27:42.919
I'm going to give the Eastern philosophies
a try and maybe they will give me what I need

00:27:42.919 --> 00:27:46.450
for satisfying this hunger for the cultivation
of wisdom."

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:50.210
And I was very lucky again because first of
all, wonderful people.

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:54.750
And secondly, this is where I got what I would
now call an ecology of practices.

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:57.260
They didn't teach me one thing.

00:27:57.260 --> 00:28:01.340
They taught me a meditative practice, Vipassana.

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:03.720
They taught me a contemplative practice, metta.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:08.140
And then they taught me Tai Chi Chuan to get
how to flow between them in a regular and

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:10.679
reliable ecology of practices.

00:28:10.679 --> 00:28:14.570
And that's how it happened and that has been
with me ever since.

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:16.160
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:24.429
Vipassana, metta and Tai Chi, that is a very
potent and uncommon trinity there.

00:28:24.429 --> 00:28:30.799
That's a very formidable combination of things
and I do want to revisit at some point meditation

00:28:30.799 --> 00:28:32.380
as contrasted with contemplation.

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:33.690
John Vervaeke: Yes.

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:41.519
Tim Ferriss: But first, I'd like to ask you,
how old were you when you decided to leave

00:28:41.519 --> 00:28:45.010
Christianity and what was that experience
like?

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:46.670
John Vervaeke: 15.

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:48.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:56.409
John Vervaeke: And my life became bifurcated
and fragmented in a way that I came to deeply

00:28:56.409 --> 00:28:58.860
find distasteful.

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:05.710
And so trying to deal with fragmentation has
also become a pervasive theme, whether it's

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:09.620
fragmentation within a person or fragmentation
between the different disciplines that study

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:10.620
the mind.

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:17.410
That's why I'm in cognitive scientists trying
to integrate and solve the fragmentation problem.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:23.780
It was very challenging for me because I couldn't
move out on my own or anything.

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:24.780
I'm 15.

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:25.780
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, right.

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:29.740
John Vervaeke: I'm still in my family and
they knew that something was going on, and

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:33.309
there was this weird don't ask, don't tell
thing.

00:29:33.309 --> 00:29:34.419
It was very challenging me.

00:29:34.419 --> 00:29:40.600
There were times when I went to church and
the minister would be getting everybody to

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:44.409
bow their head and raise their hand as if
they agreed with this.

00:29:44.409 --> 00:29:47.980
And of course, everybody's looking around
surreptitiously and everybody's raising their

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:50.269
hand and I'm the one person not raising my
hand.

00:29:50.269 --> 00:29:52.659
And just very, very, very difficult.

00:29:52.659 --> 00:29:56.380
And then of course there was a group of friends,
close friends at high school that were — they

00:29:56.380 --> 00:30:01.679
became very much invested in this project
that I was engaged in of wrestling with meaning.

00:30:01.679 --> 00:30:07.039
And so they became very supportive and that
was very happy for me but that was — and

00:30:07.039 --> 00:30:17.539
it caused things and it caused me to get married
too young because marrying was a way of escaping

00:30:17.539 --> 00:30:24.740
that fundamentalist family and setting up
a new one.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:28.670
And I'm on good terms with my ex, and I'm
not making any criticisms of her.

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:33.480
I'm just saying we got married, I think too
young because we were both escaping that and

00:30:33.480 --> 00:30:39.130
we both — and that's a good reason for forming
a company together for a while, like the fellowship

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:40.130
of the ring or something.

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:43.929
We're both going to leave fundamentalism together,
we're going to help — but it's not the basis

00:30:43.929 --> 00:30:45.980
for a long-term marriage.

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:46.980
It's not.

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:52.080
And of course, you don't know this at the
time, the ways in which I was foolish around

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:55.269
this are significant, but I was wrestling
with a lot of stuff.

00:30:55.269 --> 00:30:58.080
I was wrestling with a profound kind of depression.

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:02.950
I used to call it the black burning in my
chest because of that hole.

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:05.100
Like I said —
Tim Ferriss: I can imagine.

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:09.240
John Vervaeke: But also, like I said, there
was trauma.

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:12.980
There's instances from when I was still in
it as a kid that were absolutely traumatic

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:13.980
to me.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:16.570
I came home one day and I lived within walking
distance of the school.

00:31:16.570 --> 00:31:21.660
And there was always somebody in the home
just because of the way it was a small home.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:22.840
We were a poor family.

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:30.799
There was kids, my mom was mostly a housewife,
and I came home once and there was nobody

00:31:30.799 --> 00:31:31.799
there.

00:31:31.799 --> 00:31:38.550
And I was absolutely convinced to my bones
that the Rapture had occurred, all my family

00:31:38.550 --> 00:31:39.769
had been taken away.

00:31:39.769 --> 00:31:40.789
I was too sinful.

00:31:40.789 --> 00:31:45.520
I was left behind and the Antichrist and his
minions were coming for me.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:51.120
This is, I'm 10.

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:55.320
And then another one, I used to read the Bible
every day, and I'm glad I have, by the way.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:58.659
Biblical literacy is really important part
of cultural literacy.

00:31:58.659 --> 00:32:04.159
I don't begrudge that and the Bible still
is deeply meaningful to me, but I read this

00:32:04.159 --> 00:32:10.049
passage in the New Testament and in multiple
places they talk about the unforgivable sin,

00:32:10.049 --> 00:32:14.769
the sin that can never be forgiven no matter
how much you pray about it, and I was terrified.

00:32:14.769 --> 00:32:16.960
And I thought, "Have I committed...?"

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:18.650
Again, I think I was like 12.

00:32:18.650 --> 00:32:22.179
"Have I committed the unforgivable sin?

00:32:22.179 --> 00:32:25.400
And is thinking that the unforgivable sin?"

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:33.169
And my mother could tell I was getting deeply
distressed, and she took me to the minister

00:32:33.169 --> 00:32:39.049
who was supposed to help me, and he just gave
me empty platitudes that even as a 12-year-old

00:32:39.049 --> 00:32:45.150
I recognized were completely useless, and
I realized he doesn't have an answer to this.

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:50.620
He pretended he had — he spoke with the
confidence of some — but there was no answer

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:56.630
forthcoming, and it was just like that kind
of trauma.

00:32:56.630 --> 00:33:00.310
And these are just a couple incidences of
many.

00:33:00.310 --> 00:33:05.649
And again, I'm not villianizing my mom or
my dad or my family.

00:33:05.649 --> 00:33:07.820
I love them.

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:09.320
My mom and my dad are both dead.

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:18.409
I love my extended family but this is when
I stepped out of that, that now came to the

00:33:18.409 --> 00:33:20.800
fore like, "Well, you've got to address this.

00:33:20.800 --> 00:33:23.580
What is the world such that?

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:25.150
You shouldn't have these fears.

00:33:25.150 --> 00:33:26.549
You shouldn't have these terrors.

00:33:26.549 --> 00:33:31.880
What do you do with the way that has made
you afraid of things?"

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:39.590
So yeah, it took work, it took therapy, it
took meditation, contemplation, Tai Chi Chuan,

00:33:39.590 --> 00:33:45.400
a lot of good education, especially in the
Socratic, Platonic tradition.

00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:46.909
So that's what it was like.

00:33:46.909 --> 00:33:51.679
I wouldn't recommend it for anybody but the
thing I have to tell you, Tim, is I meet a

00:33:51.679 --> 00:33:55.840
lot of people who are attracted to my work
because of something similar like this happening

00:33:55.840 --> 00:33:56.840
to them.

00:33:56.840 --> 00:34:01.950
They have left a religious home and they do
not find any religious homes viable, yet they

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:06.470
still hunger for that deep connectedness,
that meaning, that space in which one can

00:34:06.470 --> 00:34:07.770
cultivate wisdom.

00:34:07.770 --> 00:34:15.359
Tim Ferriss: Let's segue to this word "wisdom,"
because this might sound strange, but the

00:34:15.359 --> 00:34:25.390
self transcendence and the experience of that,
I have some, I suppose, experience that lends

00:34:25.390 --> 00:34:29.730
itself to understanding what that means.

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:36.700
Wisdom though, I would love for you to define
for us, just so everybody including myself,

00:34:36.700 --> 00:34:37.700
is on the same page?

00:34:37.700 --> 00:34:40.560
John Vervaeke: Knowledge is about overcoming
ignorance.

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:42.750
Wisdom is about overcoming foolishness.

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:47.599
So you understand wisdom by understanding
foolishness, and you understand foolishness

00:34:47.599 --> 00:34:49.520
as not identical to ignorance.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:52.970
These are the moods you have to make to get
the chess pieces on the board if you want

00:34:52.970 --> 00:34:55.700
to play the game we're going to play.

00:34:55.700 --> 00:34:58.230
And so foolishness, what is it?

00:34:58.230 --> 00:35:00.560
What do we mean by this?

00:35:00.560 --> 00:35:05.589
And this is an idea that's born in the Axial
Revolution, this specific notion of foolishness.

00:35:05.589 --> 00:35:11.480
But this idea, and I think 4E cogsci bears
it out, I argue for it, I publish about it.

00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:20.080
The very processes that make us intelligent
problem solvers, make us so adaptive, are

00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:24.460
the very same processes that make us prone
to self-deceptive, self-destructive behavior,

00:35:24.460 --> 00:35:25.460
right?

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:27.650
That you don't get one without the other.

00:35:27.650 --> 00:35:32.380
So you use your intelligence to solve problems,
but you use rationality and wisdom to deal

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:36.290
with the self-deception that emerges as you're
trying to solve your problems, and that's

00:35:36.290 --> 00:35:37.490
a very different thing.

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:42.100
And the thing about these self deceptions
is they don't come as sort of isolated little

00:35:42.100 --> 00:35:43.190
things.

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:48.920
They can constellate into complex patterns
of self-organization.

00:35:48.920 --> 00:35:56.980
The confirmation bias can strengthen egocentrism,
which can strengthen the degree to which you

00:35:56.980 --> 00:36:04.390
engage in a kind of narrow framing of situations,
which can then feed back into the — and

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:08.460
we've published on this, you get these massively
complex.

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:17.230
So the dynamical complex self-organization
of your cognition is hijacked by self-deceptive

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:22.970
processes, and they form these systems that
are also complex and adaptive and hard to

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:24.500
intervene on.

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:27.850
We call it parasitic processing in the work
that Leo and I published.

00:36:27.850 --> 00:36:28.850
Right?

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:31.430
Tim Ferriss: John, would you mind giving an
example, because there are people listening,

00:36:31.430 --> 00:36:35.280
I'm sure a lot of people listening who pride
themselves on being good problem solvers,

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:39.270
having strong analytical minds, and they're
like, "But I don't see how that automatically

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:47.130
begets — not automatically, but how that
would beget delusion, self-deception."

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.380
Would you mind giving an example or a hypothetical?

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:51.160
John Vervaeke: Let's use a chess example.

00:36:51.160 --> 00:36:52.160
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:54.470
John Vervaeke: Here's the problem facing you
in chess.

00:36:54.470 --> 00:37:00.369
You can't actually check all the possible
alternative pathways for playing a chess game.

00:37:00.369 --> 00:37:04.230
The number of alternative pathways is greater
than the number of atomic particles in the

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:05.310
universe.

00:37:05.310 --> 00:37:11.740
So even the super fastest chess game can't
check all of the possible alternative sequences

00:37:11.740 --> 00:37:13.940
of actions.

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:18.480
That's why, by the way, you get a chess game
that beats any human being, or let's do it

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:19.480
with go.

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:23.819
You get AlphaGo beats any human being, but
then you can make a machine that beats AlphaGo

00:37:23.819 --> 00:37:27.920
because no game is — no AI can algorithmically
play and search.

00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:33.200
So what we have to do is we have to bias what
information we check.

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:34.200
This is the paradox.

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:36.090
It sounds like a Zen koan, right?

00:37:36.090 --> 00:37:41.520
You're intelligent because of your ability
to ignore so much information in a way that

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:46.460
makes obvious to you the relevant information
enough of the time that you're a very good

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:49.140
problem solver in very many, many domains.

00:37:49.140 --> 00:37:50.190
So you're playing chess.

00:37:50.190 --> 00:37:51.550
What's a good heuristic?

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:52.550
Control the center board.

00:37:52.550 --> 00:37:56.130
I've played with somebody who was controlling
the center board, I realized that they were

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:58.400
biasing their attention that way.

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:03.550
And so I played a peripheral game and beat
them because they focused on — now, should

00:38:03.550 --> 00:38:05.250
people not focus on the center board?

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:08.730
Well, no, it's a very good heuristic, but
it's heuristic.

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:10.550
It's not an algorithm.

00:38:10.550 --> 00:38:11.550
This is a formal thing.

00:38:11.550 --> 00:38:13.190
It's called the No Free Lunch Theorem.

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:17.150
There is no problem solving method that will
universally solve all your problems.

00:38:17.150 --> 00:38:22.270
In fact, the area above the line of average
problem solving where it's improving your

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:26.700
performance is equal to an area under the
line where it degrades your performance.

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:31.450
Every bias is just as heuristic, misfiring,
and every heuristic is just a bias that happens

00:38:31.450 --> 00:38:34.190
to be working for us in a lot of context.

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:36.240
They're interchangeable together.

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:38.920
And so even when you're doing analytic stuff,
right?

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:44.220
You're confronting this, you often realize
the way you've framed the problem is actually

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:45.819
what's preventing you from solving the problem.

00:38:45.819 --> 00:38:49.599
You have to have that aha moment that breaks
you out of it.

00:38:49.599 --> 00:38:53.760
But when you're trapped in it, that's a form
of self-deception.

00:38:53.760 --> 00:38:54.950
You can't really lie to yourself.

00:38:54.950 --> 00:38:59.761
What you do is you bias your attention to
find the wrong thing salient in a way that

00:38:59.761 --> 00:39:03.020
binds you to trying to solve it in a particular
manner.

00:39:03.020 --> 00:39:04.859
This shows up in interpersonal context.

00:39:04.859 --> 00:39:09.510
I'm sure you've had something like this as
an experience.

00:39:09.510 --> 00:39:14.240
"Oh, I thought she was angry, but she's afraid.

00:39:14.240 --> 00:39:15.970
She's afraid, not angry.

00:39:15.970 --> 00:39:18.970
I've been going at this all wrong."

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:19.970
Right?

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:20.970
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:39:20.970 --> 00:39:24.460
John Vervaeke: That doesn't mean that sometimes
people aren't angry at you and you shouldn't

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:26.970
see them as angry at you, right?

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:35.170
And so even the most analytic thing you're
doing is dependent on — pure logic, you're

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:36.730
going in to do a logic class.

00:39:36.730 --> 00:39:40.420
This is from my history, and a lot of people
will back me up on this.

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:44.430
For a while, what you're doing is you're practicing
doing all the machinery of the transformations

00:39:44.430 --> 00:39:48.250
and the logic trees and all the derivations,
and you just got to practice that.

00:39:48.250 --> 00:39:52.890
And then you realize, "But I'm still not getting
really — I'm still only getting Bs and lower

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:54.339
As, what's going on?"

00:39:54.339 --> 00:40:00.099
And you keep getting docked, not on the logic,
but how you translated natural language into

00:40:00.099 --> 00:40:04.310
the logic, for which there is no logical procedure.

00:40:04.310 --> 00:40:11.310
And so that translation process is needed
in order to get the logical machinery running,

00:40:11.310 --> 00:40:14.590
but it is not itself governed in a purely
logical manner.

00:40:14.590 --> 00:40:20.820
It requires this flexibility, this reframing,
this insight, this kind of stuff.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:28.650
Tim Ferriss: I wanted to invoke a word you
mentioned in passing with respect to flow

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:31.540
states in lectures, which was intuition.

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:32.540
John Vervaeke: Sure.

00:40:32.540 --> 00:40:39.810
Tim Ferriss: And so I wanted to loop that
in to ask — well, you can pick and choose.

00:40:39.810 --> 00:40:41.880
This is interviewee's choice.

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:47.770
So one would be how do you think about intuition
versus logic when it comes to problem solving

00:40:47.770 --> 00:40:52.790
or searching for meaning or finding meaning?

00:40:52.790 --> 00:41:00.250
Alternative question would be what kinds of
problems is logic poorly suited to solving?

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:01.250
John Vervaeke: Sure.

00:41:01.250 --> 00:41:04.410
And so we'll do that and then I'll tell you
what I think intuition is and what the powers

00:41:04.410 --> 00:41:05.530
and perils of it are.

00:41:05.530 --> 00:41:06.760
Tim Ferriss: Great.

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:11.490
John Vervaeke: So the search space, the search
space for very, very many problems, like the

00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:14.150
chess example, is combinatorially explosive.

00:41:14.150 --> 00:41:19.810
It's computationally intractable and this
is the seminal work of Newell and Simon and

00:41:19.810 --> 00:41:25.560
then Simon and his notion of bounded rationality,
and this is the idea that you can't be comprehensively

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:26.560
logical.

00:41:26.560 --> 00:41:27.560
Logic and math are algorithmic.

00:41:27.560 --> 00:41:29.480
They work in terms of certainty.

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:34.130
Certainty requires that you search sometimes
all or at least most of the space, and if

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:36.530
you tried to do that, you would've committed
cognitive suicide.

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:41.900
That would be the last thing you do and that
can't be a rational way to behave.

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:47.680
So bounded rationality is something more like
knowing where, when, how, and to what degree?

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:53.850
Using all the non-propositional kinds of knowing
to tell you where, when, how, and what degree

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:55.300
you should be logical?

00:41:55.300 --> 00:41:59.339
You have to cultivate these virtues for when
you use logic.

00:41:59.339 --> 00:42:02.950
So that's what rationality is and that's a
much harder problem.

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:05.130
"When should I be logical?"

00:42:05.130 --> 00:42:12.130
And so you have to ask yourself, "If I formulate
the problem such that the search space could

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:18.250
be searched in a relatively exhaustive fashion,
and there are algorithms that work that, how

00:42:18.250 --> 00:42:19.980
many people are present in this conversation?"

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:23.270
There's an algorithm counting, "Two, one,
two, we're done.

00:42:23.270 --> 00:42:24.270
There, that worked."

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:26.950
There was nothing wrong with doing that, right?

00:42:26.950 --> 00:42:32.490
But if you try to say, "I want to count all
the numbers of possible conversations we could

00:42:32.490 --> 00:42:37.619
have and thereby try to figure out invariant
principles through all conversations," it's

00:42:37.619 --> 00:42:41.270
like, well, what's a conversation?

00:42:41.270 --> 00:42:45.910
And I've been in many ones and there's good
ones and there's bad ones, but the good ones

00:42:45.910 --> 00:42:50.631
are aren't — they're sort of the same, they're
similar to each other.

00:42:50.631 --> 00:42:57.380
And the problem with similarity, and this
is now the main argument, is if you try to

00:42:57.380 --> 00:42:59.540
be logical about similarity, you're doomed
because —

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:00.601
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by that?

00:43:00.601 --> 00:43:01.601
What do you mean by logical about similarity?

00:43:01.601 --> 00:43:02.601
John Vervaeke: Yeah, I do.

00:43:02.601 --> 00:43:06.720
So this is an argument made by Nelson Goodman,
what a great name for a philosopher, Nelson

00:43:06.720 --> 00:43:07.720
Goodman.

00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:08.720
Tim Ferriss: Oh, outstanding.

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:11.910
John Vervaeke: 1972, and the argument goes
like this.

00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:14.160
So identity is to share all properties.

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:18.160
Similarity is to share a lot of properties,
but not share all properties, kind of the

00:43:18.160 --> 00:43:20.339
same as Sesame Street used to say.

00:43:20.339 --> 00:43:27.309
So he said, "Pick up any two objects and you'll
find that there's an indefinitely large number

00:43:27.309 --> 00:43:29.430
of properties they share."

00:43:29.430 --> 00:43:34.220
So a plum and a lawnmower, they both have
curved surfaces, they're both shiny, they're

00:43:34.220 --> 00:43:38.250
both found in North America, both contain
carbon, both weigh more than a paperclip.

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:40.410
Neither one is a particularly good weapon.

00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:43.430
How many true things can I say that apply
to both?

00:43:43.430 --> 00:43:46.690
Tim Ferriss: Those —
John Vervaeke: Indefinitely large.

00:43:46.690 --> 00:43:47.859
Tim Ferriss: Indefinitely large.

00:43:47.859 --> 00:43:48.859
John Vervaeke: Right.

00:43:48.859 --> 00:43:50.079
And that's the case for anything.

00:43:50.079 --> 00:43:55.750
So if you try to have a logic of similarity,
it will not pick out — it won't help you

00:43:55.750 --> 00:43:57.530
sort the world at all.

00:43:57.530 --> 00:44:03.060
So what people rely on is psychological similarity,
which is they say to you things like, "But

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:04.319
none of those properties..."

00:44:04.319 --> 00:44:08.800
And here's the key term, "None of those properties
are the relevant properties.

00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:11.160
Those aren't the important ones."

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:16.730
And of course, relevance and importance are
not features of a proposition.

00:44:16.730 --> 00:44:21.450
The same proposition can be relevant one minute
and irrelevant the next.

00:44:21.450 --> 00:44:25.020
This is actually a formal argument by Jerry
Fodor and others.

00:44:25.020 --> 00:44:27.190
Relevance is not a logical property.

00:44:27.190 --> 00:44:32.160
If what we mean by logical properties are
syntactic relations within propositions and

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:38.020
implication relations between them, you can't
capture relevance with those syntactic relations

00:44:38.020 --> 00:44:43.099
and therefore you can't do a logic of similarity.

00:44:43.099 --> 00:44:48.559
Tim Ferriss: Maybe this is a good place, you
can chastise me if it isn't, but to bring

00:44:48.559 --> 00:44:55.200
in and define intuition and the powers and
perils thereof.

00:44:55.200 --> 00:45:03.030
And to the extent that you could maybe share
how you harness the powers and avoid the perils

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:04.030
personally.

00:45:04.030 --> 00:45:08.230
I think that would make it easy to grasp for
folks, myself included.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:09.950
John Vervaeke: Excellent, excellent.

00:45:09.950 --> 00:45:11.240
Oh, wonderful, wonderful.

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:22.300
So I'm deeply influenced by two things: Csikszentmihalyi's
work on Flow and Hogarth's work, Educating

00:45:22.300 --> 00:45:27.300
Intuition, and he makes a proposal, and I
think this is the best proposal I've heard.

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:31.630
So if we're doing inferences to the best explanation,
it's the best explanation.

00:45:31.630 --> 00:45:38.069
So we've had a robust research program from
the late '60s, starting with Arthur Reber

00:45:38.069 --> 00:45:40.430
for what's called implicit learning.

00:45:40.430 --> 00:45:43.999
So I'm sorry, I need a minute to describe
the experimental paradigm for this because

00:45:43.999 --> 00:45:45.000
—
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

00:45:45.000 --> 00:45:48.550
John Vervaeke: So what you do is you create
an artificial grammar for how you're allowed

00:45:48.550 --> 00:45:51.160
to string numbers and letters together.

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:54.849
The grammar might have the rule, you can't
have two odd numbers beside each other.

00:45:54.849 --> 00:45:56.800
You can't have two vowels beside each other.

00:45:56.800 --> 00:46:00.310
You can't have more than four consonants in
a row or something like this, and you make

00:46:00.310 --> 00:46:03.730
these by completely arbitrary artificial grammar.

00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:09.060
You make these number-letter strings that
are like nine or 12 long.

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:12.330
So you can't hold them easily in working memory
or anything like that.

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:17.690
And so what you do is you give people and
you say, "This is one, this is one," not quite

00:46:17.690 --> 00:46:19.589
this fast, but I'm just doing it.

00:46:19.589 --> 00:46:25.290
You hold up these strings and you get people
to look at them and there's various modifications.

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:28.220
Sometimes you give feedback, but that's just
the basic paradigm.

00:46:28.220 --> 00:46:29.540
Now, here's what you do.

00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:32.300
Tim Ferriss: So you're not giving them the
rules in advance.

00:46:32.300 --> 00:46:33.300
Is that correct?

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:34.300
John Vervaeke: No.

00:46:34.300 --> 00:46:35.300
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:46:35.300 --> 00:46:36.300
So this is purely deductive.

00:46:36.300 --> 00:46:37.300
You're just giving them examples, since I'm
—

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:38.760
John Vervaeke: Well, I'm going to say it's
not deductive but —

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:39.760
Tim Ferriss: Oh, it's not.

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:40.760
Oh, okay.

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:41.990
John Vervaeke: They're purely just doing — they're
doing this.

00:46:41.990 --> 00:46:42.990
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:44.280
John Vervaeke: I'll say what I think it is
in a minute.

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:45.280
Okay.

00:46:45.280 --> 00:46:48.160
And I'll say why it's not deductive because
of an experimental variation.

00:46:48.160 --> 00:46:49.160
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:46:49.160 --> 00:46:50.690
John Vervaeke: So I'm not just going to swipe
that aside.

00:46:50.690 --> 00:46:52.690
I'm going to address your point.

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:55.839
Okay, so you do this and you do this, and
you do this and you do this.

00:46:55.839 --> 00:47:00.359
And then what you do is you say, "Now I'm
going to give you a bunch of strings."

00:47:00.359 --> 00:47:04.420
And what you do is some of those strings are
new, they haven't appeared yet, but they're

00:47:04.420 --> 00:47:07.160
generated by the same artificial grammar.

00:47:07.160 --> 00:47:11.369
And then some are generated by a completely
alternative or even randomly generated.

00:47:11.369 --> 00:47:15.040
And then you present them to people and you
say, "Which of these new strings belongs with

00:47:15.040 --> 00:47:17.380
the old ones?"

00:47:17.380 --> 00:47:23.050
And people reliably score well above chance
on this, well above chance.

00:47:23.050 --> 00:47:24.130
Okay.

00:47:24.130 --> 00:47:31.310
So what people have is the capacity without
explicit awareness or deliberate effort, they

00:47:31.310 --> 00:47:34.140
can pick up on very complex patterns.

00:47:34.140 --> 00:47:36.130
Now, why isn't it deduction?

00:47:36.130 --> 00:47:39.550
Because if you do the following and you say,
"I'm going to give you the strings, and I

00:47:39.550 --> 00:47:43.619
want you to figure out the rules that are
generating the strings," their performance

00:47:43.619 --> 00:47:47.010
degrades and they actually fall below chance,
they get really worse.

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:49.190
Tim Ferriss: They get all tangled up in themselves.

00:47:49.190 --> 00:47:50.569
John Vervaeke: Yes.

00:47:50.569 --> 00:47:57.480
So what Hogarth proposes is that intuition
is a result of implicit learning.

00:47:57.480 --> 00:48:02.130
You're picking up on very complex patterns
without explicit awareness or deliberate effort.

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:09.300
You know how close to stand to somebody at
a funeral, but it depends on their status,

00:48:09.300 --> 00:48:13.930
their closeness to the person, their emotional
state, how you are feeling.

00:48:13.930 --> 00:48:17.480
It depends on all of these variables, but
it depends on what part of the funeral it

00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:18.480
is.

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:24.010
It depends on potential religious, there's
all these variables and they're interacting

00:48:24.010 --> 00:48:25.760
in complex dynamic ways.

00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:30.000
And you've picked that up and you don't know
how you've picked it up.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.710
You don't know where and when.

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:38.050
So you just get the intuition, "I should stand
this close," and you do it, and you're right.

00:48:38.050 --> 00:48:40.230
Now, what's the peril of this?

00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:41.510
So that's its power.

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:46.580
You pick up on complex patterns and you don't
do it deductively.

00:48:46.580 --> 00:48:50.460
And I gave you the evidence for that, right?

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:52.650
So what's the peril with this?

00:48:52.650 --> 00:48:55.180
And this is Hogarth's work, and I think it's
powerful.

00:48:55.180 --> 00:48:58.790
Implicit learning doesn't care what patterns
it picks up.

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:00.109
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:49:00.109 --> 00:49:06.329
John Vervaeke: It doesn't distinguish real
causal patterns from correlational patterns,

00:49:06.329 --> 00:49:10.560
and there's a lot more complex correlational
patterns that aren't real causal patterns.

00:49:10.560 --> 00:49:16.550
And so we pick up on all kinds of complex
patterns that are not real and we form intuitions

00:49:16.550 --> 00:49:20.780
around them, but when we don't like our intuition,
we don't call it intuition, we call it bias

00:49:20.780 --> 00:49:25.960
or prejudice or racism or sexism or a whole
bunch of other things.

00:49:25.960 --> 00:49:26.960
Right?

00:49:26.960 --> 00:49:32.010
And so Hogarth says, "Well, what should we
do?"

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:34.900
We can't replace implicit learning with explicit
learning.

00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:36.170
What can we do explicitly?

00:49:36.170 --> 00:49:41.930
And he says, "Well, what we can do is set,
we can explicitly set up the situation in

00:49:41.930 --> 00:49:48.890
which we're doing implicit learning, that
replicates how science distinguishes cause

00:49:48.890 --> 00:49:51.080
from correlation," because that's what science
is.

00:49:51.080 --> 00:49:53.809
It's a practice for distinguishing cause from
correlation.

00:49:53.809 --> 00:49:55.020
So what are those?

00:49:55.020 --> 00:50:00.819
Well, you need clear feedback, you need clear
information.

00:50:00.819 --> 00:50:03.830
You need clear and tightly coupled feedback,
error has to matter.

00:50:03.830 --> 00:50:07.690
You can falsify the experiment and you set
that up.

00:50:07.690 --> 00:50:13.660
And if you're learning in those situations,
chances are your implicit learning will track

00:50:13.660 --> 00:50:17.280
causal patterns rather than correlational.

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:19.940
And then you ask me, "How do you do that,
John?"

00:50:19.940 --> 00:50:21.540
Well, here's John's answer.

00:50:21.540 --> 00:50:25.900
Csikszentmihalyi says, "How do you get into
the flow state?

00:50:25.900 --> 00:50:27.650
What are the informational constraints?

00:50:27.650 --> 00:50:32.910
The information has to be clear, it has to
be tightly coupled, and error has to matter."

00:50:32.910 --> 00:50:40.380
The exact three conditions that Hogarth proposes
for making sure you're doing good implicit

00:50:40.380 --> 00:50:47.960
learning are exactly the conditions that get
people reliably into the flow state.

00:50:47.960 --> 00:50:52.819
And so we've published on that what flow state
is, it's an evolutionary marker that you're

00:50:52.819 --> 00:50:57.349
getting a lot of insight and you're getting
a lot of insight within implicit learning

00:50:57.349 --> 00:51:00.030
that is probably tracking real causal relationships.

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:04.920
So whatever you're doing, keep doing it because
chances are you're really figuring out the

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:06.829
environment in a powerful way.

00:51:06.829 --> 00:51:09.619
Tim Ferriss: What have you done with this
knowledge?

00:51:09.619 --> 00:51:15.359
How has that informed your behaviors or beliefs
as you operate in the world?

00:51:15.359 --> 00:51:22.109
John Vervaeke: I try to make sure that we're
setting up the explicit circumstance for flow

00:51:22.109 --> 00:51:25.740
states that have a lot of those conditions
in them.

00:51:25.740 --> 00:51:31.000
This goes towards work I'm doing on shared
flow states when people are getting into dialogically

00:51:31.000 --> 00:51:35.410
shared flow states, dia-logos, and what are
the contexts we can build around that so that

00:51:35.410 --> 00:51:42.990
we can get people more reliably having insight
cascades and tracking real patterns of sense-making

00:51:42.990 --> 00:51:45.819
as opposed to weird, funky correlational things?

00:51:45.819 --> 00:51:52.140
Tim Ferriss: So I am going to double click
on dia-logos because I've mentioned dialogue

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:55.950
and wanting to get into the etymology of that
just because I'm a nerd about all that stuff.

00:51:55.950 --> 00:52:07.480
But before we get there, could you could give
a personal example of how you have used this

00:52:07.480 --> 00:52:12.609
insight and knowledge based on the studies
and the work of these various people you've

00:52:12.609 --> 00:52:20.380
mentioned, and of course your own publications,
how has that informed how you operate in the

00:52:20.380 --> 00:52:22.180
world or make decisions?

00:52:22.180 --> 00:52:24.498
John Vervaeke: So it's made me a lot more
careful about where I might be.

00:52:24.498 --> 00:52:27.930
So first of all, it's given me a way of challenging
a certain kind of decadent romanticism in

00:52:27.930 --> 00:52:33.410
the culture, which says that you should always
trust your intuition.

00:52:33.410 --> 00:52:39.839
There is no panacea faculty, there is no panacea
practice, no free lunch theorem.

00:52:39.839 --> 00:52:42.829
And I have become too — I've become — I
don't know what — almost like a preacher

00:52:42.829 --> 00:52:43.829
or something.

00:52:43.829 --> 00:52:49.470
I'd be almost evangelical about trying to
say stop demonizing any faculty and stop deifying

00:52:49.470 --> 00:52:50.589
any faculty.

00:52:50.589 --> 00:52:56.849
Your intuition will lead you as much wrong
as your reason, as much wrong as your emotions,

00:52:56.849 --> 00:53:02.300
as much wrong as whatever, as much wrong as
your logic, right?

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:07.839
Look, logic does not tell you how to go from
a weaker logic to a stronger logic.

00:53:07.839 --> 00:53:12.440
I can do all the possible manipulations within
predicate logic and it won't get me to motor

00:53:12.440 --> 00:53:13.440
logic.

00:53:13.440 --> 00:53:17.880
I have to do something outside of that to
actually increase my logical competence.

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:18.980
So there's no panacea.

00:53:18.980 --> 00:53:20.790
There's no panacea.

00:53:20.790 --> 00:53:24.590
So that has become like I have taken that
up as a theme and I do it.

00:53:24.590 --> 00:53:25.970
I do it for myself.

00:53:25.970 --> 00:53:32.550
This is why I advocate very strongly for,
and I was fortunate to be taught, an ecology

00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:34.140
of practices.

00:53:34.140 --> 00:53:35.760
I do not practice.

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:40.960
I practice a bunch of practices that have
complimentary strengths and weaknesses that

00:53:40.960 --> 00:53:46.420
are constantly checking each other and calling
each other out and constraining each other

00:53:46.420 --> 00:53:50.900
and pointing out errors in each other like
an ecology, how you have the checks and balances

00:53:50.900 --> 00:53:53.530
in a biological ecosystem.

00:53:53.530 --> 00:53:59.180
That is how it had a huge impact on how I
try to cultivate wisdom and virtue and how

00:53:59.180 --> 00:54:01.480
I recommend to others how they should do it.

00:54:01.480 --> 00:54:07.460
I strongly advocate for practicing an ecology
of practices that is well-designed and what

00:54:07.460 --> 00:54:11.220
are some of the design principles for a good
ecology of practices.

00:54:11.220 --> 00:54:15.559
And then I feed that back into my own practices
and how I try to teach other people.

00:54:15.559 --> 00:54:16.559
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:54:16.559 --> 00:54:18.690
So I would love to know how you have improved,
maybe let me rephrase that.

00:54:18.690 --> 00:54:27.630
What questions you have asked yourself to
improve your ability to spot spurious or at

00:54:27.630 --> 00:54:31.420
the very least unhelpful forms of pattern
recognition.

00:54:31.420 --> 00:54:32.420
Right?

00:54:32.420 --> 00:54:36.839
So the intuition we want to avoid and how
you've done that, because I've observed certainly,

00:54:36.839 --> 00:54:40.369
and I'm sure you have, and I'm sure I do it
in my own way.

00:54:40.369 --> 00:54:49.200
People who are very methodical about testing
in some scientific empirical fashion, causality

00:54:49.200 --> 00:54:50.200
and, say, engineering.

00:54:50.200 --> 00:54:55.450
So they're doing split testing in their job
as an engineer, and then as soon as they jump

00:54:55.450 --> 00:55:02.450
into the world of, say, nutrition or fill
in the blank, it all falls apart.

00:55:02.450 --> 00:55:10.299
And they just seem to founder in spotting
all these sort of correlations that they turn

00:55:10.299 --> 00:55:13.349
into causation in a really kind of sloppy
way.

00:55:13.349 --> 00:55:14.600
And I'm sure I do it as well.

00:55:14.600 --> 00:55:19.280
So how have you improved your ability to spot
that for yourself?

00:55:19.280 --> 00:55:24.930
John Vervaeke: Well, I don't want to in any
way try to convey that I don't fall prey to

00:55:24.930 --> 00:55:25.930
this.

00:55:25.930 --> 00:55:29.599
How have I tried to address it is how I'll
frame the question you've asked me.

00:55:29.599 --> 00:55:30.599
Tim Ferriss: Perfect.

00:55:30.599 --> 00:55:31.599
John Vervaeke: Okay.

00:55:31.599 --> 00:55:36.530
And so first of all, that issue that we were
talking about earlier, how pervasively does

00:55:36.530 --> 00:55:41.250
your practice transfer to multiple domains
and to multiple levels of your psyche?

00:55:41.250 --> 00:55:44.500
How much does it engage the non-propositional
kinds of knowing?

00:55:44.500 --> 00:55:50.420
How much is it engaging the ability to metacognitively
shift between perspectives, et cetera?

00:55:50.420 --> 00:55:52.990
For exactly that reason.

00:55:52.990 --> 00:55:58.660
Secondly, it's not the questions you answer,
it's practices you do at length for considerable

00:55:58.660 --> 00:55:59.730
amount of time.

00:55:59.730 --> 00:56:00.730
I practiced.

00:56:00.730 --> 00:56:05.930
First of all, I practiced it very explicitly
for two or three years, and this overlaps

00:56:05.930 --> 00:56:08.160
significantly with Stoicism, right?

00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:13.720
I practice active open-mindedness like I'm
a cognitive scientist, learn about the biases

00:56:13.720 --> 00:56:14.870
and this is how you get into it.

00:56:14.870 --> 00:56:17.530
Learn about the biases and first spot them
in other people.

00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:19.890
You'll be astonishingly good at that.

00:56:19.890 --> 00:56:23.050
Oh, confirmation bias, myside bias, blah,
blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:56:23.050 --> 00:56:24.950
You're really good at fundamental attribution
error.

00:56:24.950 --> 00:56:28.460
You're really good at it with other people
and get real and allow yourself to be sort

00:56:28.460 --> 00:56:30.040
of overconfident a bit.

00:56:30.040 --> 00:56:39.420
And then practice at the second stage, which
is try to note every day a bias in your own

00:56:39.420 --> 00:56:41.000
and actively counteract it.

00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:43.390
Oh wait, this is confirmation bias.

00:56:43.390 --> 00:56:47.839
Where would I look for evidence that could
disconfirm this?

00:56:47.839 --> 00:56:52.089
Or who could I talk to who would challenge
me on this?

00:56:52.089 --> 00:56:53.970
You have to practice it.

00:56:53.970 --> 00:56:57.059
It's not about getting a rule or a technique
of question.

00:56:57.059 --> 00:57:02.059
It's about developing a deeply ingrained habit
that helps to work.

00:57:02.059 --> 00:57:04.809
And in concert with this, and this is also
from Stoicism.

00:57:04.809 --> 00:57:13.670
Stoicism is the philosophy religion of internalizing
Socrates and practicing internalizing Socrates.

00:57:13.670 --> 00:57:14.720
Tim Ferriss: This is Antisthenes.

00:57:14.720 --> 00:57:15.960
Am I getting that pronunciation right?

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:16.960
John Vervaeke: Yes.

00:57:16.960 --> 00:57:17.960
Yeah, exactly, exactly, right.

00:57:17.960 --> 00:57:18.960
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:19.960
John Vervaeke: Now what did you learn from
Socrates?

00:57:19.960 --> 00:57:23.780
I learned how to dialogue with myself, but
he didn't mean the way we all talk to ourselves

00:57:23.780 --> 00:57:26.050
in our head all day long.

00:57:26.050 --> 00:57:27.069
And you have to practice that.

00:57:27.069 --> 00:57:28.520
You have to practice it on your own.

00:57:28.520 --> 00:57:30.760
I have to practice it with others.

00:57:30.760 --> 00:57:35.619
And I've been doing that, and I've been developing
ways of honing that.

00:57:35.619 --> 00:57:37.510
We do these weekend workshops.

00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:40.020
I do it with Guy Sengstock and Christopher
Mastropietro.

00:57:40.020 --> 00:57:45.210
We take people through a meditation practice,
a contemplation practice, then various circling

00:57:45.210 --> 00:57:47.539
practices, then various paraphrasing practices.

00:57:47.539 --> 00:57:52.200
And then we get them to do sort of a shared
Lectio Divina around a philosophical text

00:57:52.200 --> 00:57:54.069
where they learn to resonate with each other
around —

00:57:54.069 --> 00:57:55.069
Tim Ferriss: Lectio Divina.

00:57:55.069 --> 00:57:57.069
I don't know what that means, but I like the
sound of it.

00:57:57.069 --> 00:58:02.780
John Vervaeke: And then we take them into
a particular way of engaging in dialectic

00:58:02.780 --> 00:58:03.780
into dia-logos.

00:58:03.780 --> 00:58:07.160
And you have to practice this and you have
to read the dialogues and you have to internalize,

00:58:07.160 --> 00:58:09.160
you have to internalize the sage.

00:58:09.160 --> 00:58:13.520
And this is one of the — just the athlete
has to internalize the coach, just like the

00:58:13.520 --> 00:58:16.369
child has to internalize the parent.

00:58:16.369 --> 00:58:22.250
And that again is not a magic technique, it's
something you have to practice consistently

00:58:22.250 --> 00:58:27.809
as a way of life and degree to which I've
internalized Socrates and the degree to which

00:58:27.809 --> 00:58:34.450
I've cultivated habits of active open-mindedness,
the degree to which I've sought out the company

00:58:34.450 --> 00:58:39.809
of people who will challenge me in the right
ways because people can be also — criticize

00:58:39.809 --> 00:58:41.319
you in the wrong ways.

00:58:41.319 --> 00:58:44.550
So it takes discernment.

00:58:44.550 --> 00:58:46.680
That's how I'm trying to address what you've
asked me.

00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:47.930
Tim Ferriss: All right.

00:58:47.930 --> 00:58:57.990
So I want to attempt to create a bridge between
what you just said and do so for the listeners

00:58:57.990 --> 00:59:06.690
and to do so by giving some examples of the
