00:59:59.210 --> 01:00:00.210
them what's going on.

01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:01.210
Yes.

01:00:01.210 --> 01:00:02.210
John Vervaeke: So I've been talking throughout
about how you frame situations, what you make

01:00:02.210 --> 01:00:03.210
salient, what you background and what you
ignore.

01:00:03.210 --> 01:00:05.590
So think about that like the lens and the
frame of my glasses, right?

01:00:05.590 --> 01:00:10.130
And I'm not aware of my lenses or my frame
because I'm aware through them, beyond them

01:00:10.130 --> 01:00:11.440
and by means of them.

01:00:11.440 --> 01:00:13.320
So they're transparent to me.

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:17.950
Now sometimes, and what I'm doing right now,
everyone, I'm taking my glasses off because

01:00:17.950 --> 01:00:21.760
there might be something on my lenses and
now they're not transparent to me.

01:00:21.760 --> 01:00:24.450
I'm not looking through them, I'm looking
at them.

01:00:24.450 --> 01:00:25.589
That's what meditation is.

01:00:25.589 --> 01:00:29.710
It's about trying to become aware, step back
and look at your mental framing.

01:00:29.710 --> 01:00:35.270
So what you do is, for example, you try to
get people to look at something they're normally

01:00:35.270 --> 01:00:37.670
looking through, like their sensations.

01:00:37.670 --> 01:00:42.859
So for example, I teach people how to center,
but they're following their breath.

01:00:42.859 --> 01:00:47.270
And you want to follow the whole of your breath
going in, and you want to feel the sensations

01:00:47.270 --> 01:00:51.390
of the breath and of the expansion of your
abdomen, and then sensations of your abdomen

01:00:51.390 --> 01:00:53.869
contracting and the breath coming out.

01:00:53.869 --> 01:00:57.540
And what happens is, right, you're paying
attention to the sensations and very quickly

01:00:57.540 --> 01:01:00.040
your mind goes to, "No, I want to look through
things.

01:01:00.040 --> 01:01:02.390
I want to look at the world.

01:01:02.390 --> 01:01:03.930
Now I should be doing my laundry.

01:01:03.930 --> 01:01:06.070
Or when is the meditation going to be done?

01:01:06.070 --> 01:01:08.080
Or I wonder if Susan still likes me."

01:01:08.080 --> 01:01:12.160
And then what you do is you step back and
look at that distraction.

01:01:12.160 --> 01:01:14.000
You don't look at what you're thinking about.

01:01:14.000 --> 01:01:19.440
You label the process, thinking, imagining,
hoping, wondering, and then you return your

01:01:19.440 --> 01:01:21.240
attention to your breath.

01:01:21.240 --> 01:01:27.220
And so what you're doing is you're learning
to step back and look at your framing.

01:01:27.220 --> 01:01:32.390
Now think about this, even in the analogy,
if I just take my glasses off and look at

01:01:32.390 --> 01:01:37.420
them and maybe I wipe them and I wipe them
and I wipe them and I wipe them, how do I

01:01:37.420 --> 01:01:38.920
know if I've actually cleaned them?

01:01:38.920 --> 01:01:39.990
What do I need to do?

01:01:39.990 --> 01:01:40.990
Tim Ferriss: Got to put them back on.

01:01:40.990 --> 01:01:45.119
John Vervaeke: You've got to put them back
on and see if you now see more clearly and

01:01:45.119 --> 01:01:47.360
deeply than you used to before.

01:01:47.360 --> 01:01:48.829
That is contemplation.

01:01:48.829 --> 01:01:53.950
It comes from the Latin word contemplatio,
which is a translation of the Greek word theoria,

01:01:53.950 --> 01:01:59.660
which is where we get our word theory from,
which means to look deeply into reality.

01:01:59.660 --> 01:02:06.240
So Vipassana is this act of I'm stepping back
and looking at, but metta is the contemplative.

01:02:06.240 --> 01:02:12.740
I'm trying to look, I'm trying to see if — how
can I see you better than I used to?

01:02:12.740 --> 01:02:17.829
So in metta, I'm trying to open up and see
if I can see you better, or I may contemplate

01:02:17.829 --> 01:02:19.460
the impermanence of existence.

01:02:19.460 --> 01:02:23.559
I'm trying to see, I'm trying, not in — I'm
not just thinking or saying, I'm trying to

01:02:23.559 --> 01:02:25.640
realize the impermanence of things.

01:02:25.640 --> 01:02:27.220
That's a contemplative act.

01:02:27.220 --> 01:02:28.220
It's a direction outward.

01:02:28.220 --> 01:02:33.340
Tim Ferriss: And for folks who are familiar
with metta, is it fair to describe that as

01:02:33.340 --> 01:02:34.460
loving-kindness meditation?

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:36.319
John Vervaeke: Well, it depends what you mean.

01:02:36.319 --> 01:02:42.531
And this is where there's controversy, so
if you think of loving-kindness as a particular

01:02:42.531 --> 01:02:47.640
good emotional state you're going to get into,
I would argue you're confusing a method with

01:02:47.640 --> 01:02:49.609
a goal.

01:02:49.609 --> 01:02:55.640
So positive emotions, this is Fredrickson
and other work, they open you up, they make

01:02:55.640 --> 01:03:00.420
you curious, they afford wondering, they allow
you to question things.

01:03:00.420 --> 01:03:02.500
They put you into an explore mode.

01:03:02.500 --> 01:03:05.440
But it's the exploration that's important.

01:03:05.440 --> 01:03:15.710
What I'm actually trying to do in metta is
I'm trying to open myself up so that this

01:03:15.710 --> 01:03:18.500
overlaps with Stoicism too, right?

01:03:18.500 --> 01:03:22.799
So the stoics have this idea that whenever
we're going into situations, we're automatically

01:03:22.799 --> 01:03:24.829
assuming identities and assigning identities.

01:03:24.829 --> 01:03:27.260
I'm the professor, you're the student.

01:03:27.260 --> 01:03:33.420
Or I'm the scientist, and you're the famous
podcaster or whatever, right?

01:03:33.420 --> 01:03:34.420
And we're doing that.

01:03:34.420 --> 01:03:39.609
And I'm this, and this is a tool, a water
bottle, but of course it's not that in and

01:03:39.609 --> 01:03:40.609
of itself.

01:03:40.609 --> 01:03:49.570
And so that co-identification process is happening
mostly mindlessly, automatically, and reactively,

01:03:49.570 --> 01:03:53.359
and the stoics were trying to get us aware
of this.

01:03:53.359 --> 01:03:58.319
And in metta, what you're trying to do is
get aware of what identity am I assigning

01:03:58.319 --> 01:03:59.970
to you?

01:03:59.970 --> 01:04:05.610
What identity am I assuming and how is that
locking down the situation and how can I open

01:04:05.610 --> 01:04:12.710
it up in an exploratory way so that more of
your suchness, what you are beyond my assigned

01:04:12.710 --> 01:04:19.550
categories and identities can start to shape
how I see you and who I am and starts to be

01:04:19.550 --> 01:04:22.599
shaped by resonating with that.

01:04:22.599 --> 01:04:26.120
And now I'm trying to see more deeply into
you.

01:04:26.120 --> 01:04:31.599
The Buddha warns against positive emotions
just as much as he warns against negative

01:04:31.599 --> 01:04:32.599
emotions.

01:04:32.599 --> 01:04:37.140
Like the idea, I don't like the North American
interpretation of metta as "I want to really

01:04:37.140 --> 01:04:40.720
feel really good about people."

01:04:40.720 --> 01:04:46.190
I think that confuses it with Christian notions
of forgiveness and other things.

01:04:46.190 --> 01:04:53.359
What it is is "Can I come to a place where
your identity and my identity can be born

01:04:53.359 --> 01:04:56.280
afresh right here and right now?

01:04:56.280 --> 01:05:00.839
And what that can do for transforming both
you and I as a real possibility."

01:05:00.839 --> 01:05:03.099
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:05:03.099 --> 01:05:04.520
Thank you for that clarification.

01:05:04.520 --> 01:05:08.079
And it makes sense that, I mean, there would
be some conflating with what you mentioned,

01:05:08.079 --> 01:05:14.099
especially with the labeling of loving-kindness
by a lot of people in the United States, just

01:05:14.099 --> 01:05:15.170
given the history.

01:05:15.170 --> 01:05:17.160
John Vervaeke: Can I riff on that just one
second?

01:05:17.160 --> 01:05:18.160
Tim Ferriss: Sure.

01:05:18.160 --> 01:05:20.870
John Vervaeke: Because that goes towards a
fundamental misunderstanding of love.

01:05:20.870 --> 01:05:24.900
Love is not a feeling, okay, first of all,
right?

01:05:24.900 --> 01:05:26.530
But love is not even an emotion.

01:05:26.530 --> 01:05:34.079
When I love somebody, that can make me angry,
jealous, sad, happy, joyous.

01:05:34.079 --> 01:05:36.150
Love is an existential binding.

01:05:36.150 --> 01:05:38.440
It's an existential stance.

01:05:38.440 --> 01:05:44.970
It's a commitment to this co-identification
process and it being something by which each

01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:51.589
of us can bring out the good and cultivate
deeper personhood for each other.

01:05:51.589 --> 01:05:54.320
Love isn't an emotion and it isn't a feeling.

01:05:54.320 --> 01:05:59.100
So thinking that loving-kindness is cultivating
a particular emotion is not only mistaking

01:05:59.100 --> 01:06:02.740
the Buddhist idea, it's just misunderstanding
what love is.

01:06:02.740 --> 01:06:04.030
Love isn't an emotion.

01:06:04.030 --> 01:06:05.589
It isn't a feeling.

01:06:05.589 --> 01:06:10.700
It's an existential stance of commitment to
binding your identity to the identity of something

01:06:10.700 --> 01:06:12.220
or someone else.

01:06:12.220 --> 01:06:17.210
Tim Ferriss: So meditation, contemplation,
I'm not sure which came next.

01:06:17.210 --> 01:06:18.210
Paraphrasing.

01:06:18.210 --> 01:06:19.210
John Vervaeke: Circling.

01:06:19.210 --> 01:06:20.210
Tim Ferriss: Circling.

01:06:20.210 --> 01:06:21.210
What would be an example of circling?

01:06:21.210 --> 01:06:24.700
John Vervaeke: Oh, so that's where you've
got to talk to Guy Sengstock at some point.

01:06:24.700 --> 01:06:31.460
I mean, circling is this practice, and the
best way I could describe it, and it takes

01:06:31.460 --> 01:06:35.830
— there's a lot of exercises and skills
you have to build to do the practice well.

01:06:35.830 --> 01:06:39.119
So I don't want people to misunderstand.

01:06:39.119 --> 01:06:41.120
I'm just talking for simplicity, right?

01:06:41.120 --> 01:06:46.190
I'm not giving you the full secret sauce or
something like that.

01:06:46.190 --> 01:06:50.940
The best way to describe it is how can you
and I get into — do you know what stereoscopic

01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:52.059
vision is?

01:06:52.059 --> 01:06:55.310
The left and right visual fields and they
integrate so you get depth.

01:06:55.310 --> 01:06:58.940
So I want stereoscopic mindfulness, and what
does that mean?

01:06:58.940 --> 01:07:05.740
I want mindfulness into me and mindfulness
into you, and in a way that's affording you

01:07:05.740 --> 01:07:08.760
to get mindfulness into you and mindfulness
into me.

01:07:08.760 --> 01:07:10.549
And then we resonate on that.

01:07:10.549 --> 01:07:11.890
It's a way of being.

01:07:11.890 --> 01:07:15.410
Like I noticed that you leaned forward and
did this, what's happening in you right now

01:07:15.410 --> 01:07:16.650
as you're doing that?

01:07:16.650 --> 01:07:20.180
And then you tell me, and then you tell me
what you're noticing in me.

01:07:20.180 --> 01:07:28.369
And you get this kind of accelerating, mutually
disclosing mindfulness enhancement and it's

01:07:28.369 --> 01:07:31.930
very, very powerful practice.

01:07:31.930 --> 01:07:37.970
And then what you can do is you can use that
training to really strengthening the listening

01:07:37.970 --> 01:07:38.970
skill.

01:07:38.970 --> 01:07:40.910
So you practice circling with people.

01:07:40.910 --> 01:07:46.160
They start to pick up on this kind of — it's
interesting, Tim, what people report is they

01:07:46.160 --> 01:07:48.640
discover a kind of intimacy they didn't know
about.

01:07:48.640 --> 01:07:54.550
It's not sexual intimacy, it's not friendship
intimacy, but it's this other kind of intimacy.

01:07:54.550 --> 01:07:57.460
And they say paradoxical things like this
regularly.

01:07:57.460 --> 01:08:01.400
They say, "This new kind of intimacy, but
I've always been looking for it."

01:08:01.400 --> 01:08:03.301
They didn't know it, but they've always been
looking for it.

01:08:03.301 --> 01:08:04.301
It's this really — right?

01:08:04.301 --> 01:08:06.369
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like a psychedelic experience
in some ways.

01:08:06.369 --> 01:08:08.799
John Vervaeke: Well, I don't think that's
actually off the mark.

01:08:08.799 --> 01:08:10.630
I've got a hypothesis.

01:08:10.630 --> 01:08:15.650
You're doing circling with multiple people
and you're having to do this multiple switching

01:08:15.650 --> 01:08:20.560
up perspectives and inhabiting and indwelling
of other people and letting them indwell you.

01:08:20.560 --> 01:08:24.270
And it's getting areas of the brain to talk
to each other that normally don't talk to

01:08:24.270 --> 01:08:25.290
each other.

01:08:25.290 --> 01:08:27.770
Very, very similar to a psychedelic experience.

01:08:27.770 --> 01:08:34.159
I'm not going to say when I have done psilocybin,
but there was phenomenological overlap from

01:08:34.159 --> 01:08:37.109
my experience of that and my experience in
deep flow.

01:08:37.109 --> 01:08:38.199
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, absolutely.

01:08:38.199 --> 01:08:40.740
John Vervaeke: And then you move people to
paraphrasing.

01:08:40.740 --> 01:08:42.779
So they get the circling ability.

01:08:42.779 --> 01:08:43.800
They're into that.

01:08:43.800 --> 01:08:48.299
And then what you do is I'll say something
and you stop me after, and you have to paraphrase

01:08:48.299 --> 01:08:53.060
back to me with using as few of my words as
possible until they agree that you —

01:08:53.060 --> 01:08:54.400
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's an interesting constraint.

01:08:54.400 --> 01:08:55.400
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:08:55.400 --> 01:08:56.400
Right?

01:08:56.400 --> 01:08:57.400
So you can't just parrot back from memory,
right?

01:08:57.400 --> 01:08:58.400
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:08:58.400 --> 01:09:02.440
John Vervaeke: You have to understand and
convey, and the person gives you feedback.

01:09:02.440 --> 01:09:03.470
"No, you didn't get it."

01:09:03.470 --> 01:09:04.470
"Yes, you did."

01:09:04.470 --> 01:09:05.470
And you practice doing that.

01:09:05.470 --> 01:09:07.491
Tim Ferriss: What is the intent?

01:09:07.491 --> 01:09:08.800
John Vervaeke: The intent is —
Tim Ferriss: The objective.

01:09:08.800 --> 01:09:14.180
John Vervaeke: The intent is to realize that
one half of communication is listening.

01:09:14.180 --> 01:09:15.870
Tim Ferriss: Shocker.

01:09:15.870 --> 01:09:16.870
Yeah.

01:09:16.870 --> 01:09:17.870
Got it.

01:09:17.870 --> 01:09:21.589
John Vervaeke: Which is easy to say, but hard.

01:09:21.589 --> 01:09:27.350
So you slow down and you make space and you
let it impact you more deeply.

01:09:27.350 --> 01:09:28.350
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:09:28.350 --> 01:09:33.210
You really have to slow down to operate within
that constraint, not using the same language.

01:09:33.210 --> 01:09:34.330
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:09:34.330 --> 01:09:35.330
Exactly, exactly.

01:09:35.330 --> 01:09:38.449
And then you move into philosophical fellowship.

01:09:38.449 --> 01:09:44.409
It's a thing I've derived from Ran Lahav's
philosophical, contemplative companionship,

01:09:44.409 --> 01:09:47.390
or I forget what he calls it.

01:09:47.390 --> 01:09:48.390
He and I have emailed.

01:09:48.390 --> 01:09:52.770
So what I'm doing is not the same, but it
was inspired by him.

01:09:52.770 --> 01:09:55.100
So it goes something like this.

01:09:55.100 --> 01:09:58.820
You pick a philosophical text and you prime
people into this.

01:09:58.820 --> 01:10:03.449
We're not going to be reading this text in
order to get information from it.

01:10:03.449 --> 01:10:07.030
We're going to be reading this text in order
to be transformed by it.

01:10:07.030 --> 01:10:10.150
We're going use this text as a way of trying
to presence a sage.

01:10:10.150 --> 01:10:12.550
It's almost like a secular seance, right?

01:10:12.550 --> 01:10:18.310
You're going to presence a sage so that —
Remember we talked about earlier internalizing

01:10:18.310 --> 01:10:19.310
the sage?

01:10:19.310 --> 01:10:25.699
You really can't do that unless the presence
of the perspectival knowing of the sage is

01:10:25.699 --> 01:10:27.240
available to you.

01:10:27.240 --> 01:10:32.560
So what happens is, first of all, you read
the text very slowly and then the speaker

01:10:32.560 --> 01:10:40.510
will pick out a phrase that he or she thinks
conveys it, and then everybody chants it in

01:10:40.510 --> 01:10:41.610
sequence.

01:10:41.610 --> 01:10:45.739
And they chant it and they're trying to convey
as much and also resonate with what they're

01:10:45.739 --> 01:10:47.660
sensing other people are conveying.

01:10:47.660 --> 01:10:53.469
So it's like jazz, and you do this and then
you move into simple speech.

01:10:53.469 --> 01:11:00.090
Everybody is allowed to say no more than three
sentences about what is being provoked, invoked,

01:11:00.090 --> 01:11:04.590
and evoked in their interaction with the text.

01:11:04.590 --> 01:11:05.590
And they have to try it.

01:11:05.590 --> 01:11:12.270
And the task is I want you to convey as much
as you possibly can in as few words as possible.

01:11:12.270 --> 01:11:15.820
And so everybody does this, but you can't
just do it atomically.

01:11:15.820 --> 01:11:21.280
You have to pick up on what other people have
said when you do your simple speech.

01:11:21.280 --> 01:11:26.820
And you do that for several rounds and people
— and what happens is people are also asked

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:34.640
try to sense how all of these different perspectives
converge back to Spinoza or Plato or Buber

01:11:34.640 --> 01:11:35.640
or whoever it is.

01:11:35.640 --> 01:11:39.340
And then you're doing that and then you move
into extended speech.

01:11:39.340 --> 01:11:44.010
Everybody's now allowed to give three or four
sentences or a bit more and open it up, and

01:11:44.010 --> 01:11:48.580
they can even relate it to some experience
that they've had in their life.

01:11:48.580 --> 01:11:52.050
And you can see it's — and then you move
into free speech where people just talk about

01:11:52.050 --> 01:11:53.050
it.

01:11:53.050 --> 01:12:01.449
And what happens is people get a sense of
the text coming alive and Spinoza being present

01:12:01.449 --> 01:12:06.860
or Buber being present, obviously not literally,
but in this sense of there's something about

01:12:06.860 --> 01:12:13.659
the intersection in the we space that gives
them a sense of what was the mind that generated

01:12:13.659 --> 01:12:19.170
or as the origin of all of this, and you resonate
with it and you pick it up and it gives you

01:12:19.170 --> 01:12:21.130
an opportunity to internalize the sage.

01:12:21.130 --> 01:12:23.530
Tim Ferriss: I would love to try that.

01:12:23.530 --> 01:12:30.460
Have any of those experiences been so memorable
that to this day you remember a specific phrase

01:12:30.460 --> 01:12:36.980
from a text that helped to catalyze just an
extraordinary experience?

01:12:36.980 --> 01:12:41.470
Are there any that you've seen really light
things on fire in an interesting way?

01:12:41.470 --> 01:12:46.960
John Vervaeke: I think one in Spinoza was
"God is related to the world the way the mind

01:12:46.960 --> 01:12:48.290
is related to the body."

01:12:48.290 --> 01:12:50.520
Tim Ferriss: Ooh, can you say that one more
time?

01:12:50.520 --> 01:12:52.060
I'll have to marinate with that one.

01:12:52.060 --> 01:12:55.700
John Vervaeke: God is related to the world
in the same way the mind is related to the

01:12:55.700 --> 01:12:56.700
body.

01:12:56.700 --> 01:12:57.840
I forget where that's from.

01:12:57.840 --> 01:13:00.810
I think it might have been from the Emendation
of the Intellect.

01:13:00.810 --> 01:13:01.810
I'm not sure.

01:13:01.810 --> 01:13:07.140
Tim Ferriss: I would definitely need more
time to ramp up my RPMs to begin to chew on

01:13:07.140 --> 01:13:09.250
that, but what does that mean to you?

01:13:09.250 --> 01:13:13.350
John Vervaeke: That phrase means he's trying
to challenge Descartes within a Cartesian

01:13:13.350 --> 01:13:15.330
framework, which is part of the brilliance
of Spinoza.

01:13:15.330 --> 01:13:17.530
That's why his book is called Ethics.

01:13:17.530 --> 01:13:21.290
But he presents it as like Euclid's Elements.

01:13:21.290 --> 01:13:26.469
He presents it as this completely logical
argument with theorems and proofs.

01:13:26.469 --> 01:13:30.030
Tim Ferriss: So God is to the world as the
mind is to the body?

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:34.530
John Vervaeke: Yeah, because what he's trying
to say there, and by the way, he's not the

01:13:34.530 --> 01:13:39.580
only person to say — I can find similar
things in Maximus the Confessor from the Orthodox

01:13:39.580 --> 01:13:41.949
Christian tradition.

01:13:41.949 --> 01:13:47.390
But what he's trying to say is you have to
understand that the order of causes, that's

01:13:47.390 --> 01:13:52.840
your body, and the order of intelligibility
are actually — they have to fundamentally,

01:13:52.840 --> 01:13:54.430
well, be in contact.

01:13:54.430 --> 01:13:56.980
They have to conform to each other or we're
lost.

01:13:56.980 --> 01:13:59.620
No knowledge is possible for us.

01:13:59.620 --> 01:14:05.650
And so the mind, which is the tracking of
intelligibility, and the body, which is the

01:14:05.650 --> 01:14:13.670
laying out of causal patterns, have to interpenetrate
and map onto each other in this profound way,

01:14:13.670 --> 01:14:19.310
kind of like the way the software and the
hardware mesh together within a computer.

01:14:19.310 --> 01:14:23.070
And then that is how God is related to the
universe.

01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:26.000
God is related to the cosmos.

01:14:26.000 --> 01:14:28.850
That God isn't some other thing.

01:14:28.850 --> 01:14:34.010
God is the intelligibility that's non-logically
identical with the causal patterns of things.

01:14:34.010 --> 01:14:35.010
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:14:35.010 --> 01:14:36.300
John Vervaeke: I don't know if that helps.

01:14:36.300 --> 01:14:39.780
Tim Ferriss: Well, could you restate that
just for a knuckle-dragger like me?

01:14:39.780 --> 01:14:42.210
Remember, I'm from Long Island, originally.

01:14:42.210 --> 01:14:43.600
How do you relate to God now?

01:14:43.600 --> 01:14:46.730
I'd be so curious to know if you use that
word at all.

01:14:46.730 --> 01:14:52.500
If it is a word that you use, what would be
a simple way of describing to a five-year-old?

01:14:52.500 --> 01:14:57.560
Yes, I am comparing myself to a five-year-old.

01:14:57.560 --> 01:15:01.680
What that means to you.

01:15:01.680 --> 01:15:09.969
John Vervaeke: I appreciate the framing.

01:15:09.969 --> 01:15:12.780
I'm a non-theist.

01:15:12.780 --> 01:15:17.470
And this is the idea that there are a shared
set of presuppositions of modern theism.

01:15:17.470 --> 01:15:23.949
Ancient theism is a very different form of
theism, but modern theism and modern atheism

01:15:23.949 --> 01:15:25.810
share presuppositions.

01:15:25.810 --> 01:15:32.220
And they go like this, something like God
is the supreme being, the ultimate being,

01:15:32.220 --> 01:15:34.179
the super thing.

01:15:34.179 --> 01:15:39.890
And the best way to relate to God is by having
beliefs about him/her/it.

01:15:39.890 --> 01:15:45.739
And that sacredness is to have true beliefs
about this super being and to govern your

01:15:45.739 --> 01:15:47.270
life accordingly.

01:15:47.270 --> 01:15:51.360
And the theist and the atheist both agree
to that definition.

01:15:51.360 --> 01:15:53.560
And the theist says yes, and the atheist says
no.

01:15:53.560 --> 01:15:57.190
And the non-theist says, "I reject all of
those presuppositions."

01:15:57.190 --> 01:16:01.540
I don't think of ultimate reality as a thing.

01:16:01.540 --> 01:16:03.530
I don't think of it as a being.

01:16:03.530 --> 01:16:07.699
I think of it as the ground of being and being
is a no-thingness.

01:16:07.699 --> 01:16:09.520
It is not a thing.

01:16:09.520 --> 01:16:14.460
It is that in which all things come to be.

01:16:14.460 --> 01:16:17.200
Being isn't a being.

01:16:17.200 --> 01:16:21.429
This isn't something you can just get to —
Tim Ferriss: Very Daoist, also.

01:16:21.429 --> 01:16:22.429
Very Daoist.

01:16:22.429 --> 01:16:23.429
John Vervaeke: Very Daoist.

01:16:23.429 --> 01:16:28.340
The Dao that can be spoken of is not the Dao,
and the naming is the myriad way and the emptiness

01:16:28.340 --> 01:16:31.090
of the cup is what makes the cup function.

01:16:31.090 --> 01:16:35.409
All of these non-thingness metaphors, it's
like water, right?

01:16:35.409 --> 01:16:40.659
They're all trying to get you to understand
that being the ground, the really real isn't

01:16:40.659 --> 01:16:41.989
a thing.

01:16:41.989 --> 01:16:46.719
It's a no-thingness, which is not the same
thing as nothingness.

01:16:46.719 --> 01:16:52.130
And that we can enter into a profound relationship
with that no-thingness.

01:16:52.130 --> 01:16:53.620
Let me give you an analogy.

01:16:53.620 --> 01:16:55.159
I love my partner.

01:16:55.159 --> 01:16:58.480
She's an amazing woman, and I'm fortunate
to be with her.

01:16:58.480 --> 01:17:03.920
I do not think I will ever completely grasp
or understand her because there is something

01:17:03.920 --> 01:17:08.860
about her that always is transcending herself
and transcending my understanding of her.

01:17:08.860 --> 01:17:12.850
There is something in that sense properly
mysterious about her.

01:17:12.850 --> 01:17:19.320
Any frame I put around her, she shines into
it, but she also withdraws from it further

01:17:19.320 --> 01:17:21.010
into the mystery that she is.

01:17:21.010 --> 01:17:25.050
And that's why I continue to fall in love
with her, right?

01:17:25.050 --> 01:17:31.570
And so that mystery of no-thingness is something
into which we can fall deeply in love.

01:17:31.570 --> 01:17:32.719
We can.

01:17:32.719 --> 01:17:34.840
The no-thingness in me.

01:17:34.840 --> 01:17:37.620
For example, what's the no-thingness in you?

01:17:37.620 --> 01:17:41.889
Every time you step back, remember the glasses,
and look at yourself, "There I am."

01:17:41.889 --> 01:17:46.120
James makes this move, other people, William
James and other people.

01:17:46.120 --> 01:17:50.219
That's the observed self, but that's never
the observing self.

01:17:50.219 --> 01:17:53.989
The observing self can never be brought into
view.

01:17:53.989 --> 01:17:57.139
That is not any thing that can be seen by
you.

01:17:57.139 --> 01:18:01.560
It is that which makes any seeable thing seeable
to you, right?

01:18:01.560 --> 01:18:08.360
There's a no-thingness in you, and that no-thingness
in you can come into deep resonance with the

01:18:08.360 --> 01:18:12.659
no-thingness of ultimate reality such that
you fall in love with it.

01:18:12.659 --> 01:18:14.179
And this is not about belief.

01:18:14.179 --> 01:18:20.580
This is about deeply coupling so that it discloses
itself more and more to you as you are drawn

01:18:20.580 --> 01:18:22.179
beyond yourself more and more.

01:18:22.179 --> 01:18:26.080
It's like the way you fall in love with another
person.

01:18:26.080 --> 01:18:32.890
And so you can learn to fall in love with
the depths of reality.

01:18:32.890 --> 01:18:38.850
And it's like —
It is a source of intelligibility that is

01:18:38.850 --> 01:18:43.449
never exhausted, but it is not itself directly
intelligible because if it is, there would

01:18:43.449 --> 01:18:46.590
have to be a principle behind it that made
it intelligible.

01:18:46.590 --> 01:18:48.870
It is the principle of intelligibility.

01:18:48.870 --> 01:18:50.870
So it is not itself intelligible.

01:18:50.870 --> 01:18:53.949
And that is precisely what makes it sacred.

01:18:53.949 --> 01:18:56.940
I reciprocally love it, open myself up to
it.

01:18:56.940 --> 01:19:03.760
It is an inexhaustible felt of new intelligibility,
new insight, new connectedness, new meaning.

01:19:03.760 --> 01:19:09.190
And I think that's a proper understanding
of sacred that isn't primarily about believing

01:19:09.190 --> 01:19:10.190
propositions.

01:19:10.190 --> 01:19:14.510
So I'm a non-theist, and I understand God
to be a way of pointing to that.

01:19:14.510 --> 01:19:16.100
Tim Ferriss: Great.

01:19:16.100 --> 01:19:17.390
Very, very helpful.

01:19:17.390 --> 01:19:22.530
And as you're talking about the sacred, and
maybe I'm already losing track of the definition

01:19:22.530 --> 01:19:29.770
that you just provided, but I'm wondering
if Tai Chi, or let me put it in some context.

01:19:29.770 --> 01:19:36.280
If you experience the sacred, if one of the
indicators or precursors of experiencing the

01:19:36.280 --> 01:19:42.489
sacred is a sense of timelessness, and if
that's something that you seek or experience

01:19:42.489 --> 01:19:49.040
in your sort of ecology practices, including
Tai Chi, I'm wondering if that factors in

01:19:49.040 --> 01:19:50.040
somehow.

01:19:50.040 --> 01:19:51.040
John Vervaeke: Yes, it does.

01:19:51.040 --> 01:19:53.760
I mean, and many traditions sort of do this.

01:19:53.760 --> 01:19:56.949
Timelessness, and that's the proper meaning
of eternity.

01:19:56.949 --> 01:19:58.790
Eternity doesn't mean everlasting-ness.

01:19:58.790 --> 01:20:04.530
It means not bound by time and space, like
two plus two equals four or E equals mc squared

01:20:04.530 --> 01:20:05.530
for that matter.

01:20:05.530 --> 01:20:06.530
Where is it?

01:20:06.530 --> 01:20:07.530
Where is it?

01:20:07.530 --> 01:20:08.530
When is it?

01:20:08.530 --> 01:20:11.190
Those questions don't make any sense, right?

01:20:11.190 --> 01:20:18.900
Now, that is a way of bringing you into — because
eternity is timeless, spaceless, it's no-thingness.

01:20:18.900 --> 01:20:21.400
It doesn't have a space.

01:20:21.400 --> 01:20:28.180
It doesn't have a spatial temporal location
like a physical thing.

01:20:28.180 --> 01:20:32.100
Like for example, where is E equals mc squared?

01:20:32.100 --> 01:20:33.740
Right?

01:20:33.740 --> 01:20:34.740
It's everywhere and nowhere.

01:20:34.740 --> 01:20:37.179
It doesn't make any sense.

01:20:37.179 --> 01:20:44.230
So the realization of eternity, timelessness,
you aptly described it, not unending time.

01:20:44.230 --> 01:20:48.850
That is a way of opening up to how being is
distinct from beings.

01:20:48.850 --> 01:20:51.000
But you have to make the move.

01:20:51.000 --> 01:20:52.000
This is Plato.

01:20:52.000 --> 01:20:55.920
You have to ascend out of the cave, but you
also have to return back down into the cave,

01:20:55.920 --> 01:21:03.080
which is you also have to see that being also
is and is within and includes the beings.

01:21:03.080 --> 01:21:09.531
It's like the sacred is within everything
but not enclosed by it.

01:21:09.531 --> 01:21:15.980
It is beyond everything but not excluded,
if that makes any sense.

01:21:15.980 --> 01:21:21.480
So the timelessness is a move, but then you
— it's dialogical.

01:21:21.480 --> 01:21:27.480
You come out, and then you come back and you
see the world in a grain of sand and heaven

01:21:27.480 --> 01:21:29.030
in a wild flower.

01:21:29.030 --> 01:21:34.010
You hold infinity in the palm of your hand
and spend eternity in an hour.

01:21:34.010 --> 01:21:36.930
Like that, Blake, that's how you get to the
cultivation of wisdom.

01:21:36.930 --> 01:21:41.459
Tim Ferriss: Part of the reason I asked is
that, for instance, I have friends who surf.

01:21:41.459 --> 01:21:51.420
And they self-describe or describe their experiences
of flow as time stopping or not existing.

01:21:51.420 --> 01:21:53.850
And they'll also say, "The ocean is my church."

01:21:53.850 --> 01:21:59.570
I just came back from a trip where a mountain
guide said, "The mountains are my church."

01:21:59.570 --> 01:22:10.520
And so that to me brings up the question of
whether one worthwhile pursuit would be trying

01:22:10.520 --> 01:22:16.980
to find activities that provoke this sense
of timelessness to fill the void that was

01:22:16.980 --> 01:22:23.710
left when religion vacated the premises for
so many people in the modern world.

01:22:23.710 --> 01:22:25.920
I don't know if you have any thoughts on that.

01:22:25.920 --> 01:22:27.940
John Vervaeke: I have a lot of thoughts on
that.

01:22:27.940 --> 01:22:30.520
That's a big, big part of what went on in
Awakening from the Meaning Crisis.

01:22:30.520 --> 01:22:35.430
I think you want to — I mean, I think there's
a continuum from insight to flow to mystical

01:22:35.430 --> 01:22:38.580
experiences to higher states of consciousness.

01:22:38.580 --> 01:22:45.080
And I think you want to expand your cognitive
flexibility, your cognitive competence, so

01:22:45.080 --> 01:22:49.770
that you can experience the whole continuum
and you want to situate it within an ecology

01:22:49.770 --> 01:22:53.870
of practices that is guarding you against
self-deceptive bullshit and all that kind

01:22:53.870 --> 01:22:55.130
of thing.

01:22:55.130 --> 01:23:03.740
But those flow, and awe, and profound senses
of at-one-ment or a sense of non-duality.

01:23:03.740 --> 01:23:11.040
So I use the term for this sense of connectedness,
religio, which is one of the purported, possible

01:23:11.040 --> 01:23:13.639
etymological roots of the word religion.

01:23:13.639 --> 01:23:18.920
And I think what people are saying is, "I
got this profound sense of connected to myself

01:23:18.920 --> 01:23:24.120
and to the depths of reality, when I'm in
the mountains or when I'm — the ocean."

01:23:24.120 --> 01:23:25.120
Right?

01:23:25.120 --> 01:23:27.710
And Aquinas called God an ocean of being.

01:23:27.710 --> 01:23:28.710
Right?

01:23:28.710 --> 01:23:30.170
And I'm connected to this.

01:23:30.170 --> 01:23:31.170
Right?

01:23:31.170 --> 01:23:39.880
And what this does is it gives me a sense
of being deeply coupled to what is deeply

01:23:39.880 --> 01:23:41.230
real, within and without.

01:23:41.230 --> 01:23:48.510
And I think there are ecologies of practices
that can do this for people outside of an

01:23:48.510 --> 01:23:49.930
explicit religious framework.

01:23:49.930 --> 01:23:55.370
Now, I would say that dismissing the religious
traditions as if there's nothing we need to

01:23:55.370 --> 01:23:58.260
learn from them, I think that's also a mistake.

01:23:58.260 --> 01:23:59.870
I think we should respect —
Tim Ferriss: Agreed.

01:23:59.870 --> 01:24:02.530
John Vervaeke: — and dialogue with them
and learn as much as we can.

01:24:02.530 --> 01:24:04.170
And I've said this before.

01:24:04.170 --> 01:24:11.470
If people take from my work a way of how they
can return to a religious home, if they get

01:24:11.470 --> 01:24:17.030
more out of Islam, they get more out of Buddhism,
they get more of Christianity, great.

01:24:17.030 --> 01:24:18.840
I'm not in dispute with that.

01:24:18.840 --> 01:24:25.659
But I also want to help afford an ecology
of practices for the nones, the N-O-N-E-Ses,

01:24:25.659 --> 01:24:32.949
the people who say they have no official religious
affiliation, for all kinds of reasons.

01:24:32.949 --> 01:24:36.850
I'll use the metaphor your friends gave.

01:24:36.850 --> 01:24:41.699
Is there a way for them to find a church or
temple or mosque, by which they mean a set

01:24:41.699 --> 01:24:45.120
of practices that gives them that profound
religio?

01:24:45.120 --> 01:24:47.050
Yes, there is.

01:24:47.050 --> 01:24:48.060
And this is important.

01:24:48.060 --> 01:24:54.310
The solution to the meaning crisis and nihilism
is not to brow people with argument or legislation.

01:24:54.310 --> 01:24:59.679
It's to enable them to fall in love with the
depths of reality within and without and between.

01:24:59.679 --> 01:25:01.449
Again, that is what we need.

01:25:01.449 --> 01:25:02.570
Tim Ferriss: Hmm.

01:25:02.570 --> 01:25:03.699
Let me ask you.

01:25:03.699 --> 01:25:08.940
This is going to maybe seem like a non-sequitur,
but I imagine you have some thoughts.

01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:18.760
First, as bit of background, I know many religious
people all over the world, and the connective

01:25:18.760 --> 01:25:24.870
tissue, the pattern that I think I have observed
in those who seem to be content, at peace

01:25:24.870 --> 01:25:26.140
—
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:25:26.140 --> 01:25:32.520
Tim Ferriss: — is, among other things, a
strong sense of community.

01:25:32.520 --> 01:25:33.520
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:25:33.520 --> 01:25:40.330
Tim Ferriss: And the specific community I'd
like to ask you about are Sufis, because one

01:25:40.330 --> 01:25:46.130
of my most fascinating acquaintances is a
— well, I don't know if he would call himself

01:25:46.130 --> 01:25:49.630
Sufi, but he certainly studies Sufism and
is deeply immersed.

01:25:49.630 --> 01:25:51.830
He lives in the Middle East.

01:25:51.830 --> 01:25:55.290
What are your thoughts, observations, if any?

01:25:55.290 --> 01:25:57.500
John Vervaeke: Yeah, so a couple of things
from my biography.

01:25:57.500 --> 01:26:03.260
When I went to Shiatsu bootcamp, I learned
Shiatsu, as I said, and one of the people

01:26:03.260 --> 01:26:12.429
teaching was a Sufi master, and huge impact
on me.

01:26:12.429 --> 01:26:17.050
And my partner, her family, her father, his
father was within the Sufi tradition.

01:26:17.050 --> 01:26:18.050
They're Persian.

01:26:18.050 --> 01:26:21.369
And so Sufism, right, very, very profound.

01:26:21.369 --> 01:26:27.730
And a lot of the poetry that they treat — and
I mean this as a compliment, not as a insult.

01:26:27.730 --> 01:26:33.449
They treat the poetry religiously, Hafez and
Rumi and other people.

01:26:33.449 --> 01:26:34.449
Right?

01:26:34.449 --> 01:26:39.250
And a lot of it is maybe not directly Sufi,
but deeply influenced by Sufism.

01:26:39.250 --> 01:26:40.350
Tim Ferriss: Deeply influenced.

01:26:40.350 --> 01:26:45.550
John Vervaeke: And Corbin, who has influenced
me profoundly around the imaginal, also deeply

01:26:45.550 --> 01:26:46.550
influenced by Sufism.

01:26:46.550 --> 01:26:47.550
Tim Ferriss: What was that name?

01:26:47.550 --> 01:26:48.550
John Vervaeke: Corbin.

01:26:48.550 --> 01:26:51.110
Henry Corbin, C-O-R-B-I-N.

01:26:51.110 --> 01:26:57.840
He's the guy that sort of coined the difference
between the imaginary and the imaginal, and

01:26:57.840 --> 01:26:59.070
things like that.

01:26:59.070 --> 01:27:00.910
And then, I do a practice.

01:27:00.910 --> 01:27:03.989
One of my practices is what I call responsive
poiesis.

01:27:03.989 --> 01:27:08.510
Poiesis is the Greek word for poetry, but
it also means "making," the way we make new

01:27:08.510 --> 01:27:10.969
sense that we didn't have before.

01:27:10.969 --> 01:27:15.770
And what I'll do is I'll read in a Lectio
Divina fashion, this — not reading for information

01:27:15.770 --> 01:27:17.310
but reading for transformation.

01:27:17.310 --> 01:27:22.450
I'll read a Sufi poem, and then I'll try and
write a poem in response.

01:27:22.450 --> 01:27:26.570
It's not trying to parody it or parrot it
or repeat it.

01:27:26.570 --> 01:27:28.020
I'm responding to it.

01:27:28.020 --> 01:27:29.020
It's responsive poiesis.

01:27:29.020 --> 01:27:31.340
Tim Ferriss: Oh, it's like the paraphrasing.

01:27:31.340 --> 01:27:32.340
It's very interesting.

01:27:32.340 --> 01:27:33.340
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:27:33.340 --> 01:27:34.340
Yes.

01:27:34.340 --> 01:27:35.340
Very, very.

01:27:35.340 --> 01:27:36.340
But not just paraphrasing, but even more.

01:27:36.340 --> 01:27:40.750
Like, "I've really heard you, and now this
is how I can respond to what you've said."

01:27:40.750 --> 01:27:41.750
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

01:27:41.750 --> 01:27:42.750
John Vervaeke: Yeah.

01:27:42.750 --> 01:27:46.469
And so the Sufism is really important to me
in that sense.

01:27:46.469 --> 01:27:48.460
Now, you have to be really careful.

01:27:48.460 --> 01:27:50.850
You don't want to engage in inappropriate
cultural appropriation.

01:27:50.850 --> 01:27:53.260
You don't want to commodify things.

01:27:53.260 --> 01:27:56.929
You want to receive things as they're given
and in respect.

01:27:56.929 --> 01:28:04.440
But for me, Sufism is important because it
represents, well, part of a thesis I've been

01:28:04.440 --> 01:28:10.739
arguing for, which is Neoplatonism has this
tremendous capacity to enter into reciprocal

01:28:10.739 --> 01:28:15.820
reconstruction with other powerful worldviews,
cultural frameworks.

01:28:15.820 --> 01:28:17.670
It did that with Christianity.

01:28:17.670 --> 01:28:19.930
It did that with Judaism, Kabbalah.

01:28:19.930 --> 01:28:21.410
It does it with Islam and Sufism.

01:28:21.410 --> 01:28:26.890
Sufism is directly — they will even talk
about Plotinus, one of the — right?

01:28:26.890 --> 01:28:33.659
But Neoplatonism is also capable of entering
into reciprocal reconstruction with science.

01:28:33.659 --> 01:28:37.750
It did it at the late Renaissance and helped
give birth to science, by the way.

01:28:37.750 --> 01:28:43.940
Kepler and Galileo are deeply influenced by
Neoplatonism.

01:28:43.940 --> 01:28:46.510
It did it at the beginning of the 20th century.

01:28:46.510 --> 01:28:53.270
Lot of the people around the Einsteinian Revolution
are deeply influenced by Neoplatonism and

01:28:53.270 --> 01:28:57.780
things that are somewhat similar like Vedanta
and other things like that.

01:28:57.780 --> 01:28:59.340
So this is really powerful.

01:28:59.340 --> 01:29:03.290
Tim Ferriss: I should've asked this earlier,
but I was planning on coming back to it.

01:29:03.290 --> 01:29:10.639
So Neoplatonism, I — oh, I think I understand
what "neo" means, and certainly Plato, but

01:29:10.639 --> 01:29:17.280
Neoplatonism, what is that, for those of us,
including myself, who honestly could not give

01:29:17.280 --> 01:29:18.280
a definition?

01:29:18.280 --> 01:29:24.080
John Vervaeke: So it's the grand unified field
theory of ancient philosophy as a way of life.

01:29:24.080 --> 01:29:28.091
Not academic philosophy, but philosophy as
a way of life for the cultivation of wisdom

01:29:28.091 --> 01:29:31.699
and meaning and virtue, individually and collectively.

01:29:31.699 --> 01:29:45.150
What it does is, it integrates Socratic, Platonic
spirituality with Aristotelian ideas about

01:29:45.150 --> 01:29:54.510
knowledge as conformity contact with stoic
principles of how to properly cultivate virtue

01:29:54.510 --> 01:29:56.219
and metacognitive abilities.

01:29:56.219 --> 01:30:00.929
And it integrated them all together, and not
just as some LEGO blocks being put together,

01:30:00.929 --> 01:30:01.929
but organically.

01:30:01.929 --> 01:30:07.150
So they all came to, I would argue, a greater
fruition within that integration.

01:30:07.150 --> 01:30:15.970
And so it became this grand unified, powerful
living framework, so much so that when it

01:30:15.970 --> 01:30:21.679
encounters major world religions, they typically
can't ignore it or dispense with it.

01:30:21.679 --> 01:30:28.619
They typically enter into profound integration
with it, like in Christianity, like in Judaism,

01:30:28.619 --> 01:30:31.159
like in Sufism, like in science.

01:30:31.159 --> 01:30:35.949
And it looks like if Thomas Plant is right,
along the Silk Road possibly with Vedanta,

01:30:35.949 --> 01:30:37.080
possibly with Buddhism.

01:30:37.080 --> 01:30:43.840
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so we are going to continue
this thread, but I wanted to pause to return

01:30:43.840 --> 01:30:45.369
to the personal, if you wouldn't mind.

01:30:45.369 --> 01:30:46.400
John Vervaeke: I don't mind.

01:30:46.400 --> 01:30:47.719
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:30:47.719 --> 01:30:57.501
You'll have to the pardon maybe the way that
this question is couched, but I just read

01:30:57.501 --> 01:31:04.130
Lonesome Dove, which is 950 or 980 pages,
and then, now I'm watching the three-part

01:31:04.130 --> 01:31:07.560
television series with Robert Duvall and so
on, which is very good.

01:31:07.560 --> 01:31:09.449
But I'm noticing things have to get omitted.

01:31:09.449 --> 01:31:12.840
Many, many, many, many, many things have to
get omitted.

01:31:12.840 --> 01:31:18.989
And so they're trying to hit the most seminal
moments from this Pulitzer Prize-winning novel

01:31:18.989 --> 01:31:22.699
about the American West.

01:31:22.699 --> 01:31:29.199
If one were to make a film about your search
for meaning or your creation of meaning over

01:31:29.199 --> 01:31:32.280
your life, what would those seminal moments
be?

01:31:32.280 --> 01:31:35.580
What would some of those milestones be?

01:31:35.580 --> 01:31:39.640
John Vervaeke: Leaving Christianity.

01:31:39.640 --> 01:31:41.830
Encountering Socrates.

01:31:41.830 --> 01:31:48.449
Finding the Tai Chi, metta, Vipassana ecology
of practices.

01:31:48.449 --> 01:31:54.100
Reading Pierre Hadot and rediscovering ancient
philosophy not as discourse but as a way of

01:31:54.100 --> 01:31:58.510
life.

01:31:58.510 --> 01:32:05.659
Discovering 4E cognitive science that gave
me a way of talking about wisdom and self-transcendence

01:32:05.659 --> 01:32:13.119
and flow and embodiment that was consonant
with what I was learning from these two wisdom

01:32:13.119 --> 01:32:23.380
traditions, the Socratic-Neoplatonic tradition
and the sort of proto-Zen, Buddhist-Taoist.

01:32:23.380 --> 01:32:29.790
And then teaching a course at U of T called
Buddhism and Cognitive Science.

01:32:29.790 --> 01:32:34.260
A student then coming to me after taking the
course and saying, "You should put this on

01:32:34.260 --> 01:32:36.449
as a YouTube series."

01:32:36.449 --> 01:32:38.580
That became Awakening from the Meaning Crisis.

01:32:38.580 --> 01:32:42.730
Because of that, meeting people who became
very interested in these ideas about ecologies

01:32:42.730 --> 01:32:43.730
of practices.

01:32:43.730 --> 01:32:45.261
And we didn't do the final one, by the way.

01:32:45.261 --> 01:32:46.980
We didn't do dialectic Into dia-logos.

01:32:46.980 --> 01:32:48.290
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:32:48.290 --> 01:32:49.290
We should do that.

01:32:49.290 --> 01:32:50.880
John Vervaeke: Well, maybe another time.

01:32:50.880 --> 01:32:56.119
But anyways, right, and meeting people like
Guy and meeting Christopher Mastropietro.

01:32:56.119 --> 01:32:57.119
People say this.

01:32:57.119 --> 01:33:03.440
And again, asking for charity from the audience,
right, because this could sound ridiculously

01:33:03.440 --> 01:33:04.440
pretentious.

01:33:04.440 --> 01:33:09.070
But people often say if I'm Socrates, and
that's what I aspire to be at least, right,

01:33:09.070 --> 01:33:10.070
Chris is Plato.

01:33:10.070 --> 01:33:12.730
I totally agree with that.

01:33:12.730 --> 01:33:17.980
The natural lyricism, the eloquence, the gift
of reflection, the authenticity, the depth.

01:33:17.980 --> 01:33:21.730
Meeting him, getting to work with him.

01:33:21.730 --> 01:33:24.800
Meeting, of course, my current partner.

01:33:24.800 --> 01:33:27.590
That would be, I guess, the arc right now.

01:33:27.590 --> 01:33:31.820
Tim Ferriss: Could you say more about, if
I'm getting the pronunciation right, Pierre

01:33:31.820 --> 01:33:32.820
Hadot?

01:33:32.820 --> 01:33:33.820
Is that right?

01:33:33.820 --> 01:33:34.820
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:33:34.820 --> 01:33:35.820
Oh, Pierre Hadot.

01:33:35.820 --> 01:33:36.820
When I was asked — I think it was Jonathan
Rowson.

01:33:36.820 --> 01:33:37.820
Was it?

01:33:37.820 --> 01:33:40.120
No, it's Jules Evans on Rebel Wisdom, the
book that changed my life.

01:33:40.120 --> 01:33:42.170
I picked What Is Ancient Philosophy?

01:33:42.170 --> 01:33:44.389
by Pierre Hadot.

01:33:44.389 --> 01:33:47.920
That book, and philosophy as a way of life.

01:33:47.920 --> 01:33:53.130
And this is where Hadot brings out that philosophy
is not primarily about the propositional.

01:33:53.130 --> 01:33:56.200
It's not primarily about declarative discourse.

01:33:56.200 --> 01:34:02.520
Philosophy is a way of life in which you're
training skills, states of mind, perspectives,

01:34:02.520 --> 01:34:07.120
traits of characters, and those are all the
components of virtue.

01:34:07.120 --> 01:34:10.139
That's what philosophy as a way of life is
about.

01:34:10.139 --> 01:34:15.909
It's about what Hadot called spiritual exercises,
by which people entered into this process

01:34:15.909 --> 01:34:21.930
of profound aspirational transformation for
the cultivation of virtue and the deepening

01:34:21.930 --> 01:34:23.560
of the love of wisdom.

01:34:23.560 --> 01:34:30.010
And this just blew me away because it represented
what I had actually saw in ancient philosophy,

01:34:30.010 --> 01:34:33.739
but what had not been provided to me by academic
philosophy.

01:34:33.739 --> 01:34:37.200
And so that just opened up so much for me.

01:34:37.200 --> 01:34:39.119
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:34:39.119 --> 01:34:41.889
So books.

01:34:41.889 --> 01:34:45.639
You are, I think fair to say, a connoisseur
of books.

01:34:45.639 --> 01:34:46.639
You're an avid reader.

01:34:46.639 --> 01:34:49.580
You also have an extensive reading list on
your website.

01:34:49.580 --> 01:34:54.449
You have the Awakening from the Meaning Crisis
book list.

01:34:54.449 --> 01:34:59.719
If you were only allowed to choose two books
that you could personally reread from that

01:34:59.719 --> 01:35:01.040
— it doesn't have to be exactly two.

01:35:01.040 --> 01:35:02.040
It could be one.

01:35:02.040 --> 01:35:03.040
It could be three.

01:35:03.040 --> 01:35:08.520
Doesn't really matter, but you were disallowed
from ever rereading other books on the list.

01:35:08.520 --> 01:35:11.840
You only got to choose two or three.

01:35:11.840 --> 01:35:14.239
Which one, two, or three would you choose,
potentially?

01:35:14.239 --> 01:35:18.139
John Vervaeke: Plato's Dialogues and Plotinus'
Ennead.

01:35:18.139 --> 01:35:21.460
Tim Ferriss: What are the reasons for those
two?

01:35:21.460 --> 01:35:23.380
John Vervaeke: Those books are sacred to me.

01:35:23.380 --> 01:35:28.429
Remember I talked about sacredness as an inexhaustible,
fountain of intelligibility —

01:35:28.429 --> 01:35:29.429
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:35:29.429 --> 01:35:30.639
John Vervaeke: — this reciprocal opening
with the world?

01:35:30.639 --> 01:35:31.639
I read Plato.

01:35:31.639 --> 01:35:32.639
It transforms me.

01:35:32.639 --> 01:35:35.239
I go out and see things in the world I hadn't
seen.

01:35:35.239 --> 01:35:40.219
I live that way, and then that feeds back
into me and transforms me.

01:35:40.219 --> 01:35:45.389
And then I read the text as a transformed
person, and I see things I hadn't seen before.

01:35:45.389 --> 01:35:46.409
That opens me up.

01:35:46.409 --> 01:35:49.060
I now go out into — and that just keeps
happening.

01:35:49.060 --> 01:35:54.000
And it's been happening since I first read
The Republic, since I first read The Symposium.

01:35:54.000 --> 01:36:00.660
I read Plotinus' Ennead on the nature of beauty
or on the nature of One and contemplation,

01:36:00.660 --> 01:36:03.100
on dialect, any of these great Enneads.

01:36:03.100 --> 01:36:09.199
And what's astonishing about Plotinus — and
it's Neoplatonism, so you need to be educated

01:36:09.199 --> 01:36:13.150
in Platonism, Stoicism, Aristotelianism.

01:36:13.150 --> 01:36:17.160
So I don't recommend people just pick up Plotinus.

01:36:17.160 --> 01:36:20.219
Read some good secondary sources on it first.

01:36:20.219 --> 01:36:29.639
But imagine you could read a text that was
simultaneously a profound philosophical argument

01:36:29.639 --> 01:36:36.800
and a spiritual exercise that was transforming
you the way meditation and contemplation to,

01:36:36.800 --> 01:36:41.930
and they were woven together with irreplaceable
artistry.

01:36:41.930 --> 01:36:42.930
That's Plotinus.

01:36:42.930 --> 01:36:43.930
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

01:36:43.930 --> 01:36:49.989
That should be the book blurb on the back
of the cover.

01:36:49.989 --> 01:36:54.460
If you could only choose two poets to —
John Vervaeke: Ah.

01:36:54.460 --> 01:36:59.489
Tim Ferriss: — nourish your soul, for lack
of a better way to phrase it — ah, could

01:36:59.489 --> 01:37:00.989
be two to three again.

01:37:00.989 --> 01:37:05.510
We don't need to be exact about it, but who
would make the short list for you?

01:37:05.510 --> 01:37:09.560
John Vervaeke: Rilke is first and foremost
for me.

01:37:09.560 --> 01:37:17.240
I won't get it perfectly, but the poem that
goes something like, "Ah, not to be cut off,

01:37:17.240 --> 01:37:21.560
even by the slightest partition from the law
of the stars.

01:37:21.560 --> 01:37:22.560
What is it?

01:37:22.560 --> 01:37:29.130
What is it if not intensified sky, hurled
through with birds and filled with the winds

01:37:29.130 --> 01:37:30.770
of homecoming?"

01:37:30.770 --> 01:37:34.900
And it's like, "Yeah, that's what the experience
of the sacred is like."

01:37:34.900 --> 01:37:38.880
Or you read "The Archaic Bust of Apollo,"
and he does all this thing, and you're just

01:37:38.880 --> 01:37:41.659
drawn into this statue, and it's coming alive.

01:37:41.659 --> 01:37:45.909
And then he suddenly breaks it and says, "You
must change your life."

01:37:45.909 --> 01:37:48.100
That's the deep response to beauty.

01:37:48.100 --> 01:37:50.310
That's the most appropriate response to beauty.

01:37:50.310 --> 01:37:51.630
You must change your life.

01:37:51.630 --> 01:37:52.630
Right?

01:37:52.630 --> 01:37:53.630
That.

01:37:53.630 --> 01:37:54.630
So Rilke, hands down.

01:37:54.630 --> 01:37:55.630
The second.

01:37:55.630 --> 01:38:00.159
The second one is a bit of a toss-up for me.

01:38:00.159 --> 01:38:01.700
Probably Blake.

01:38:01.700 --> 01:38:07.739
I quoted Blake a few minutes ago.

01:38:07.739 --> 01:38:15.290
But there's Gerard Manley Hopkins and Yeats
also really, really important for me.

01:38:15.290 --> 01:38:22.260
Tim Ferriss: Do you think it's important to
find poetry that resonates for yourself predominantly

01:38:22.260 --> 01:38:23.969
in your native language?

01:38:23.969 --> 01:38:25.090
John Vervaeke: No.

01:38:25.090 --> 01:38:26.159
Rilke doesn't.

01:38:26.159 --> 01:38:27.159
Which is —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

01:38:27.159 --> 01:38:28.159
John Vervaeke: Because Rilke's in German.

01:38:28.159 --> 01:38:29.159
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:38:29.159 --> 01:38:32.920
John Vervaeke: And of course, I did enough
German way back when in order to get my PhD,

01:38:32.920 --> 01:38:36.460
a long time ago in a galaxy far, far away.

01:38:36.460 --> 01:38:42.060
But yeah, I can't read German now, certainly
not the way you need to be able to read it

01:38:42.060 --> 01:38:45.920
to pick up on poetry.

01:38:45.920 --> 01:38:49.780
Rilke still moves me profoundly, even in translation,
so yeah.

01:38:49.780 --> 01:38:51.120
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:38:51.120 --> 01:38:52.120
Fantastic.

01:38:52.120 --> 01:38:53.120
Thank you.

01:38:53.120 --> 01:38:58.969
And funny enough, I was listening to Coltrane
jazz before this conversation, which maybe

01:38:58.969 --> 01:39:01.889
was a good warm-up for the conversation.

01:39:01.889 --> 01:39:03.693
And I have a new collection of translations.

01:39:03.693 --> 01:39:04.693
I want to get the name of the author correct.

01:39:04.693 --> 01:39:05.693
So it is a new translation of Rumi poetry.

01:39:05.693 --> 01:39:06.693
John Vervaeke: Oh, yes.

01:39:06.693 --> 01:39:07.693
Yeah.

01:39:07.693 --> 01:39:11.160
Tim Ferriss: And it is by — I'm not going
to pronounce this correctly, but Haleh Liza

01:39:11.160 --> 01:39:12.239
Gafori.

01:39:12.239 --> 01:39:21.000
As a Persian or Persian American, I mean,
her knowledge of the language so enriches

01:39:21.000 --> 01:39:30.480
how she is able to convey passages that could
be real stumbling blocks for anyone who is

01:39:30.480 --> 01:39:34.300
basing their translation off of another translation
or who is a non-native —

01:39:34.300 --> 01:39:35.300
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:39:35.300 --> 01:39:36.300
Tim Ferriss: — speaker.

01:39:36.300 --> 01:39:38.080
It's very hard to translate, period.

01:39:38.080 --> 01:39:39.880
But it's even harder to translate poetry.

01:39:39.880 --> 01:39:41.599
It's like, do you choose the cadence?

01:39:41.599 --> 01:39:46.590
Do you choose to try to match the alliteration
or what the equivalent of alliteration would

01:39:46.590 --> 01:39:47.590
be?

01:39:47.590 --> 01:39:48.590
It's such a formidable task.

01:39:48.590 --> 01:39:50.280
I was very impressed by the book.

01:39:50.280 --> 01:39:54.290
I would be remiss if I didn't ask you, if
you're open to a few more questions —

01:39:54.290 --> 01:39:55.480
John Vervaeke: I'm fine with it.

01:39:55.480 --> 01:39:56.480
I'm enjoying this.

01:39:56.480 --> 01:39:57.480
Let's keep going.

01:39:57.480 --> 01:39:58.599
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:39:58.599 --> 01:39:59.599
Wonderful.

01:39:59.599 --> 01:40:00.599
4E.

01:40:00.599 --> 01:40:02.909
I think this is important for us to discuss.

01:40:02.909 --> 01:40:03.980
What are the four Es?

01:40:03.980 --> 01:40:07.990
John Vervaeke: So I think we should have six
Es, but I'll tell you what the standard four

01:40:07.990 --> 01:40:09.080
Es are.

01:40:09.080 --> 01:40:11.670
Tim Ferriss: And then the bonus two, I'm into
it.

01:40:11.670 --> 01:40:19.909
John Vervaeke: So this is the idea that cognition
is inherently embodied, first E. Embedded,

01:40:19.909 --> 01:40:22.349
coupled to the environment, second E. Right?

01:40:22.349 --> 01:40:24.820
Enacted, right?

01:40:24.820 --> 01:40:27.640
Cognition is something that you are doing.

01:40:27.640 --> 01:40:29.170
It's not something you have.

01:40:29.170 --> 01:40:30.170
Right?

01:40:30.170 --> 01:40:32.900
It's between you and the world, not within
you.

01:40:32.900 --> 01:40:33.900
Right?

01:40:33.900 --> 01:40:35.150
And then extended.

01:40:35.150 --> 01:40:38.520
Most of our cognition is not done inside of
us.

01:40:38.520 --> 01:40:41.920
It's done through the world.

01:40:41.920 --> 01:40:44.210
This includes other people.

01:40:44.210 --> 01:40:46.320
So for example, you and I are having a conversation
right now.

01:40:46.320 --> 01:40:51.730
We're solving a problem, and this is depending
on people building computers, setting up the

01:40:51.730 --> 01:40:55.050
Internet, setting up Zoom or Riverside.

01:40:55.050 --> 01:40:59.849
Neither one of us invented the English language,
et cetera.

01:40:59.849 --> 01:41:02.660
And all of these things, and even literacy.

01:41:02.660 --> 01:41:07.449
Literacy's not natural, but we internalize
it, but it allows us to link our cognition

01:41:07.449 --> 01:41:09.420
in profound ways.

01:41:09.420 --> 01:41:13.099
And so most of our problem-solving is done
in extended way.

01:41:13.099 --> 01:41:17.790
I guess the classic book by this is Cognition
In the Wild by Ed Hutchins, where he talks

01:41:17.790 --> 01:41:22.390
about the fact that nobody, no person, navigates
a ship.

01:41:22.390 --> 01:41:25.340
There's a whole bunch of different people
doing different roles and a whole bunch of

01:41:25.340 --> 01:41:30.820
equipment, and they form a dynamical system
that is actually responsible for navigating

01:41:30.820 --> 01:41:32.250
the ship.

01:41:32.250 --> 01:41:38.949
Myself and one of my dear friends and great
co-authors, Dan Chiappe, we published three

01:41:38.949 --> 01:41:44.530
papers on NASA scientists moving the rovers
around on Mars.

01:41:44.530 --> 01:41:45.530
Right?

01:41:45.530 --> 01:41:52.659
And how do they form this distributed cognition,
especially with that huge time gap, right,

01:41:52.659 --> 01:41:55.219
the time delay, and how do they make it work?

01:41:55.219 --> 01:41:57.440
And how does that distributed cognition work?

01:41:57.440 --> 01:42:03.150
And because they get very powerful sense of
an emergent kind of we-agency that's above

01:42:03.150 --> 01:42:05.849
and beyond just adding the part.

01:42:05.849 --> 01:42:07.780
That's the extended.

01:42:07.780 --> 01:42:10.340
So, embodied.

01:42:10.340 --> 01:42:11.730
You don't have a body.

01:42:11.730 --> 01:42:13.429
You are a body.

01:42:13.429 --> 01:42:15.969
And your mind and your body are not separate
things.

01:42:15.969 --> 01:42:18.390
They're different aspects of what you are.

01:42:18.390 --> 01:42:20.830
And people may say, "What do you mean by that?"

01:42:20.830 --> 01:42:21.870
What do I mean by that?

01:42:21.870 --> 01:42:29.119
Well, remember I said earlier, you have this
problem you're facing, which is, "What do

01:42:29.119 --> 01:42:30.270
I pay attention to?"

01:42:30.270 --> 01:42:31.270
Right?

01:42:31.270 --> 01:42:32.720
"What do I focus my attention to?

01:42:32.720 --> 01:42:33.720
What do I ignore?"

01:42:33.720 --> 01:42:35.790
And this is not cold calculation.

01:42:35.790 --> 01:42:36.790
It's also your affect.

01:42:36.790 --> 01:42:39.280
It means what do you care about, and what
do you not care about?

01:42:39.280 --> 01:42:42.199
Read Montague, the neuroscientist, put it
really well.

01:42:42.199 --> 01:42:47.869
He said, "The difference between us and computers
is we care about information, and they don't."

01:42:47.869 --> 01:42:49.449
Right?

01:42:49.449 --> 01:42:51.489
And that's our great strength and our great
weakness.

01:42:51.489 --> 01:42:54.570
Now the thing is, why do you care about information?

01:42:54.570 --> 01:42:58.909
You care about information because, and this
is the work of Evan Thompson, a colleague

01:42:58.909 --> 01:43:02.360
of mine — well, he was at U of T. I'm still
a friend.

01:43:02.360 --> 01:43:03.360
Right?

01:43:03.360 --> 01:43:06.150
It is, you are a self-making thing.

01:43:06.150 --> 01:43:07.810
That's what a living thing is.

01:43:07.810 --> 01:43:13.909
You care about information because you are,
moment by moment, taking care of yourself.

01:43:13.909 --> 01:43:16.560
Not that yourself is over here, and you're
taking care of it.

01:43:16.560 --> 01:43:19.619
You are a self-organizing, self-taking-care-of
thing.

01:43:19.619 --> 01:43:25.790
And because of that, you have to care about
this information rather than that information.

01:43:25.790 --> 01:43:32.110
And organisms with other bodies presumably
have different salience landscapes.

01:43:32.110 --> 01:43:37.670
Wittgenstein famously well-said, "Even if
a lion could speak, we would not understand

01:43:37.670 --> 01:43:38.820
him."

01:43:38.820 --> 01:43:42.480
Because even if they were using English correctly,
what they found relevant and salient would

01:43:42.480 --> 01:43:48.219
be bizarre to us because they have different
bodies that are embedded in different environments.

01:43:48.219 --> 01:43:50.440
And that is not happenstance.

01:43:50.440 --> 01:43:54.170
That is constitutive of you being a cognitive
agent.

01:43:54.170 --> 01:43:59.330
If you are not embodied, you do not have any
reason for caring about this information rather

01:43:59.330 --> 01:44:00.820
than that information.

01:44:00.820 --> 01:44:05.310
And then you have faced the fact that the
world is [inaudible] explosive in the amount

01:44:05.310 --> 01:44:07.060
of information available to you.

01:44:07.060 --> 01:44:08.470
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:44:08.470 --> 01:44:14.769
This is, very ineptly, what I was trying to
convey very early in the conversation, predominantly

01:44:14.769 --> 01:44:17.219
— and I encourage people to find this.

01:44:17.219 --> 01:44:20.739
It's not going to be immediately coming to
the tip of my tongue.

01:44:20.739 --> 01:44:27.440
But Steve Jurvetson, who's a very technical
investor, I think he did electrical engineering

01:44:27.440 --> 01:44:31.320
and mechanical engineering degree in three
years at Stanford or something like that.

01:44:31.320 --> 01:44:34.940
I'm probably getting some of the specifics
wrong, but he's been on the podcast before.

01:44:34.940 --> 01:44:44.869
And he had this very long Facebook post discussing
why it would be difficult, if not impossible,

01:44:44.869 --> 01:44:48.369
to experience anything as a human if we were
just brains in a jar.

01:44:48.369 --> 01:44:49.369
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:44:49.369 --> 01:44:50.369
Brains in a vat problem.

01:44:50.369 --> 01:44:51.369
Yes.

01:44:51.369 --> 01:44:52.369
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:44:52.369 --> 01:44:53.369
Brain in a vat problem.

01:44:53.369 --> 01:44:57.349
And I found it very compelling and sort of
embarrassing that it wasn't self-evident to

01:44:57.349 --> 01:44:59.580
me right off the bat.

01:44:59.580 --> 01:45:07.659
It's like without these appendages and this
multifaceted organism to interface with an

01:45:07.659 --> 01:45:15.590
environment, what we experience as consciousness
just doesn't seem, at least offhand to me,

01:45:15.590 --> 01:45:18.510
possible, which makes it very precious that
we have this experience.

01:45:18.510 --> 01:45:19.510
John Vervaeke: Yeah.

01:45:19.510 --> 01:45:22.489
Tim Ferriss: And in —
John Vervaeke: Being embodied is really wonderful,

01:45:22.489 --> 01:45:23.489
actually.

01:45:23.489 --> 01:45:24.489
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:45:24.489 --> 01:45:27.860
So what are the two Es that lead to six?

01:45:27.860 --> 01:45:29.970
John Vervaeke: Emotional.

01:45:29.970 --> 01:45:37.949
The idea that our knowing and our emoting
are separable things, I think is one of the

01:45:37.949 --> 01:45:45.139
great mistakes that came out of the Enlightenment
Romanticism conflict.

01:45:45.139 --> 01:45:47.780
Being rational, ratio, proper proportioning.

01:45:47.780 --> 01:45:50.920
It's about putting things into perspective,
perspectival knowing, and caring about them

01:45:50.920 --> 01:45:54.739
in the right way, and knowing within that,
when and where and how to be logical, et cetera,

01:45:54.739 --> 01:45:55.739
et cetera.

01:45:55.739 --> 01:45:58.449
We've already talked about that.

01:45:58.449 --> 01:46:03.889
And I mean, a good place to point people about
this is Damásio's Descartes' Error.

01:46:03.889 --> 01:46:04.889
Right?

01:46:04.889 --> 01:46:09.920
You have people who have a certain kind of
brain damage.

01:46:09.920 --> 01:46:14.300
The frontal cortex is working such that you
could give them a standard IQ test, and they

01:46:14.300 --> 01:46:19.500
could do all the computations quite well in
it, but you could drive them insane by saying,

01:46:19.500 --> 01:46:21.900
"Before you take the test, I want you to choose.

01:46:21.900 --> 01:46:26.580
Should you write it in red ink or black ink
or blue ink?"

01:46:26.580 --> 01:46:30.990
And then they go crazy because they have no
motivation.

01:46:30.990 --> 01:46:32.020
They have no affect.

01:46:32.020 --> 01:46:36.400
They have no reason to care about this rather
than that.

01:46:36.400 --> 01:46:38.690
Right?

01:46:38.690 --> 01:46:41.260
Emotion and cognition are interweaving and
inter-affording.

01:46:41.260 --> 01:46:45.390
So that's why emotion should be the next E.
Emotions are not irrational.

01:46:45.390 --> 01:46:46.390
Right?

01:46:46.390 --> 01:46:51.910
Like I said, there is no one faculty that
is infallibly good.

01:46:51.910 --> 01:46:52.910
Right?

01:46:52.910 --> 01:46:58.080
Yes, your emotions can lead you astray, but
try living without them and see how rational

01:46:58.080 --> 01:46:59.219
you can be.

01:46:59.219 --> 01:47:00.219
Right?

01:47:00.219 --> 01:47:01.470
Your agency will be crippled.

01:47:01.470 --> 01:47:03.340
And I mean that seriously.

01:47:03.340 --> 01:47:04.340
Right?

01:47:04.340 --> 01:47:08.719
So that would be my fifth E.
The sixth has to do with a somewhat technical

01:47:08.719 --> 01:47:12.909
term, exaptation, which is drawn from evolutionary
theory.

01:47:12.909 --> 01:47:16.429
Let me give you a clear example.

01:47:16.429 --> 01:47:19.369
So I'm using my tongue to speak, right?

01:47:19.369 --> 01:47:21.369
But tongues did not evolve for speech.

01:47:21.369 --> 01:47:25.040
If they did, all the animals with tongues
would be talking to us.

01:47:25.040 --> 01:47:26.040
Right?

01:47:26.040 --> 01:47:30.099
What you have is you have a very flexible
muscle, right, because it's for mastication,

01:47:30.099 --> 01:47:35.290
and it has a lot of very fine nerve endings
because it's for poison and sucrose detection

01:47:35.290 --> 01:47:36.570
and things like that.

01:47:36.570 --> 01:47:38.889
And it happens to be in the air passageway.

01:47:38.889 --> 01:47:43.639
So you've got something that's preadapted,
already almost perfectly built for being a

01:47:43.639 --> 01:47:45.080
speaking machine.

01:47:45.080 --> 01:47:49.719
And so exaptation is you take something, and
you repurpose it.

01:47:49.719 --> 01:47:52.199
And this is the work of Michael Anderson.

01:47:52.199 --> 01:47:57.060
Like the way that cerebellum has been repurposed
from being a sensory motor balance thing to

01:47:57.060 --> 01:48:02.780
being how we balance and move around in abstract
space.

01:48:02.780 --> 01:48:10.300
And so that exaptation, the idea of Michael
Anderson, more recent work, especially in

01:48:10.300 --> 01:48:13.730
[inaudible] and others, is the brain is constantly
doing that.

01:48:13.730 --> 01:48:20.820
The brain is constantly exapting things, like
machines, that have been built for doing one

01:48:20.820 --> 01:48:22.420
thing, and then they get repurposed.

01:48:22.420 --> 01:48:26.880
Or they also use the language of circuit reuse,
like from computer science, right, that has

01:48:26.880 --> 01:48:27.880
been repurposed.

01:48:27.880 --> 01:48:34.659
Tim Ferriss: I like the six Es.

01:48:34.659 --> 01:48:41.671
I'm being very careful with my language here,
so I'll try not to be, because it'll end up

01:48:41.671 --> 01:48:45.100
being too clunky if I self-edit too intensely.

01:48:45.100 --> 01:48:56.710
Do you have any heretical beliefs about consciousness
that people in the space would strongly disagree

01:48:56.710 --> 01:49:01.540
with or think are unfounded, yet you have
some degree of conviction in?

01:49:01.540 --> 01:49:03.420
John Vervaeke: Yes.

01:49:03.420 --> 01:49:09.199
And if people are interested in this, I have
a more long form presentation of a talk I

01:49:09.199 --> 01:49:14.369
gave in Thunder Bay at the Conference on Consciousness
and Conscience.

01:49:14.369 --> 01:49:20.140
And I talk about the three questions of consciousness.

01:49:20.140 --> 01:49:24.050
And so the first question is the question
everybody's really fixated on, which is the

01:49:24.050 --> 01:49:28.469
nature question, which is, something that
seems so non-physical, how does it possibly

01:49:28.469 --> 01:49:32.909
exist in the otherwise physical universe?

01:49:32.909 --> 01:49:34.090
And that's the nature question.

01:49:34.090 --> 01:49:38.949
And of course, you have Chalmers' version
of the hard problem and qualia and things

01:49:38.949 --> 01:49:39.949
like that.

01:49:39.949 --> 01:49:41.440
And I'll come back to qualia in a second.

01:49:41.440 --> 01:49:47.030
Now there's another question which is as equally
important, and people don't give it enough

01:49:47.030 --> 01:49:50.030
attention, and they should.

01:49:50.030 --> 01:49:54.010
And this is the function question, which is,
what is consciousness for?

01:49:54.010 --> 01:49:55.550
What does it do?

01:49:55.550 --> 01:50:00.210
And before you give me an answer, let me try
and make this more problematic to you.

01:50:00.210 --> 01:50:06.200
You do a lot of very, very sophisticated stuff
without consciousness.

01:50:06.200 --> 01:50:10.520
You are taking the noises coming out of my
face hole and translating them into ideas

01:50:10.520 --> 01:50:11.520
in your mind.

01:50:11.520 --> 01:50:14.010
And you have no idea how that process works.

01:50:14.010 --> 01:50:17.810
You have no introspective access to it, right?

01:50:17.810 --> 01:50:18.960
I make noises.

01:50:18.960 --> 01:50:20.070
You have ideas.

01:50:20.070 --> 01:50:21.219
What's happening in between?

01:50:21.219 --> 01:50:23.869
Don't know.

01:50:23.869 --> 01:50:30.060
Or think about the complex, sophisticated
action you can do, how little attention you

01:50:30.060 --> 01:50:35.150
have to bring to walking or even, and you've
had this, I have no doubt, probably, highway

01:50:35.150 --> 01:50:41.099
hypnotism, where you've been driving and you
realize "For the past 15, I haven't been paying

01:50:41.099 --> 01:50:43.350
attention to the road at all.

01:50:43.350 --> 01:50:50.320
And yet this sort of zombie inside me has
been managing to keep me on the road.

01:50:50.320 --> 01:50:53.640
And I wouldn't rely on it or anything," but
you did.

01:50:53.640 --> 01:51:00.980
So the question is, our unconscious seems
to be so powerful.

01:51:00.980 --> 01:51:04.329
Why do we have consciousness?

01:51:04.329 --> 01:51:05.329
What's it do?

01:51:05.329 --> 01:51:07.050
And why do we love it so much?

01:51:07.050 --> 01:51:10.410
And then the third question is the meta question.

01:51:10.410 --> 01:51:13.090
What's the relationship between these questions?

01:51:13.090 --> 01:51:15.710
So here's my first heresy.

01:51:15.710 --> 01:51:19.840
Unlike many people, I think these two questions
have to be answered in a completely integrated

01:51:19.840 --> 01:51:20.840
fashion.

01:51:20.840 --> 01:51:23.780
I think trying to answer the nature question
without answering the function question is

01:51:23.780 --> 01:51:24.820
doomed to fail.

01:51:24.820 --> 01:51:27.940
And I think trying to answer the function
question without trying to answer the nature

01:51:27.940 --> 01:51:29.250
question is doomed to fail.

01:51:29.250 --> 01:51:33.770
So there's the first Vervaeken heresy, and
I don't know who's going to be Devil's advocate

01:51:33.770 --> 01:51:34.770
for me.

01:51:34.770 --> 01:51:36.190
But anyways, oh no, that'd be the opposite.

01:51:36.190 --> 01:51:39.380
That would be my person putting me on trial.

01:51:39.380 --> 01:51:40.380
So that's the first.

01:51:40.380 --> 01:51:41.380
Here's the second.

01:51:41.380 --> 01:51:45.860
The second is, we can do this.

01:51:45.860 --> 01:51:53.949
We can find a way of answering this if we
pay attention to a convergence that is occurring

01:51:53.949 --> 01:51:56.630
about what is the function of consciousness?

01:51:56.630 --> 01:52:03.780
And then I think this gives us a lot of the
machinery for explaining a lot of the qualia

01:52:03.780 --> 01:52:06.170
that are so central to the nature of consciousness.

01:52:06.170 --> 01:52:07.170
Tim Ferriss: Qualia being?

01:52:07.170 --> 01:52:08.170
John Vervaeke: What is that?

01:52:08.170 --> 01:52:09.170
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

01:52:09.170 --> 01:52:15.389
John Vervaeke: Qualia means the subjective
feel, the redness of red to you.

01:52:15.389 --> 01:52:18.659
You're not just unconsciously reacting to
wavelengths.

01:52:18.659 --> 01:52:20.889
You're having this experience of redness.

01:52:20.889 --> 01:52:22.760
That's a qualia.

01:52:22.760 --> 01:52:28.080
And that seems to be something in consciousness,
and it's subjective in nature.

01:52:28.080 --> 01:52:31.119
And we don't even know if your qualia and
my qualia are the same.

01:52:31.119 --> 01:52:33.060
Tim Ferriss: Well, very often they're not.

01:52:33.060 --> 01:52:38.110
I mean, Japan is a good example of that.

01:52:38.110 --> 01:52:46.159
Their green and blue are not the same green
and blue that we see, depending on the crosswalk

01:52:46.159 --> 01:52:47.409
go signals and so on.

01:52:47.409 --> 01:52:50.340
John Vervaeke: And even a person who's color
blind has different qualia.

01:52:50.340 --> 01:52:54.940
Or as Thomas Nagel famously argued, bats who
use echolocation have qualia that we don't

01:52:54.940 --> 01:52:55.940
have.

01:52:55.940 --> 01:52:56.940
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:52:56.940 --> 01:52:57.940
Right.

01:52:57.940 --> 01:52:58.940
John Vervaeke: Okay.

01:52:58.940 --> 01:52:59.940
So that's what that means.

01:52:59.940 --> 01:53:03.290
And that's the hard problem, because there
doesn't seem to be any way of explaining their

01:53:03.290 --> 01:53:04.640
nature.

01:53:04.640 --> 01:53:09.350
And this is work I'm doing with a whole bunch
of other people, so I don't want to take sole

01:53:09.350 --> 01:53:10.350
credit.

01:53:10.350 --> 01:53:16.040
But I think if you make use of functionality
and you make use of the four kinds of knowing

01:53:16.040 --> 01:53:20.070
and things like this, I think you can give
an integrated answer.

01:53:20.070 --> 01:53:25.400
There is a consensus emerging, and I've published
on this and talked about this, around what

01:53:25.400 --> 01:53:27.530
consciousness does, what's it for.

01:53:27.530 --> 01:53:33.699
And then I think once you get that and you
open up that functionality, you actually get

01:53:33.699 --> 01:53:38.830
a phenomenology, you get an explanation of
the experience out of that.

01:53:38.830 --> 01:53:44.639
So the converging thing is that, what consciousness
is for and it overlaps with attention and

01:53:44.639 --> 01:53:47.690
working memory and fluid intelligence.

01:53:47.690 --> 01:53:52.530
Think about when you're driving on the highway,
when do you come back to it?

01:53:52.530 --> 01:53:59.230
If something unexpected happens, or there's
a really complex situation suddenly emerging,

01:53:59.230 --> 01:54:04.870
or there's an ill-defined problem that you're
not quite sure how to formulate and frame,

01:54:04.870 --> 01:54:06.810
that's what you need consciousness for.

01:54:06.810 --> 01:54:09.120
So what is consciousness?

01:54:09.120 --> 01:54:11.860
It's this higher order relevance realization.

01:54:11.860 --> 01:54:19.790
It's about re-realizing what's relevant and
important so that you can deal with the added

01:54:19.790 --> 01:54:26.369
challenge of zeroing in on relevant information
that is given to you by novelty, complexity,

01:54:26.369 --> 01:54:27.369
and ill-defined-ness.

01:54:27.369 --> 01:54:29.440
That's what consciousness is for.

01:54:29.440 --> 01:54:31.310
It's enhanced relevance realization.

01:54:31.310 --> 01:54:34.409
And that's why it overlaps with working memory
and things like that.

01:54:34.409 --> 01:54:40.850
Tim Ferriss: Now, that does sound like a consciousness
that would extend to animals other than humans.

01:54:40.850 --> 01:54:42.619
John Vervaeke: Totally.

01:54:42.619 --> 01:54:45.900
It helps to explain our intuition about this.

01:54:45.900 --> 01:54:50.900
Generally, when people are willing to extend
the attribution of consciousness, they actually

01:54:50.900 --> 01:54:57.329
do it in a way, without realizing, that tracks
how much fluid intelligence do they think

01:54:57.329 --> 01:54:58.940
that organism has?

01:54:58.940 --> 01:55:03.079
Because fluid intelligence, working memory,
attention, all of these things are about relevance

01:55:03.079 --> 01:55:04.691
realization and enhancing it.

01:55:04.691 --> 01:55:07.190
And so you've got this consensus.

01:55:07.190 --> 01:55:14.310
Okay, now let's say we get relevance realization,
and that consciousness of higher order version.

01:55:14.310 --> 01:55:15.310
What about qualia?

01:55:15.310 --> 01:55:21.869
Well, first of all, let's divide qualia, and
this is the second Vervaeke heresy.

01:55:21.869 --> 01:55:25.310
Let's divide qualia into two kinds of qualia.

01:55:25.310 --> 01:55:29.500
There's the typical qualia that philosophers
love to talk about, the adjectival qualia,

01:55:29.500 --> 01:55:33.840
like blueness and greenness and sweetness.

01:55:33.840 --> 01:55:40.579
But there are adverbial qualia, there are
qualias of here-ness, now-ness, togetherness

01:55:40.579 --> 01:55:41.579
of my experience.

01:55:41.579 --> 01:55:45.079
I'm here, I'm now, my experience is together.

01:55:45.079 --> 01:55:50.870
There's a unity and a here now presencing
of consciousness.

01:55:50.870 --> 01:55:53.329
The adverbial.

01:55:53.329 --> 01:56:03.060
Now here's the thing, talk to people who have
engaged in long-term meditative practice.

01:56:03.060 --> 01:56:08.550
And this is well documented, read Foreman
for the documentation, I've been here in this

01:56:08.550 --> 01:56:09.550
state.

01:56:09.550 --> 01:56:12.070
And you get into a state called the pure consciousness
event.

01:56:12.070 --> 01:56:13.070
You're not conscious of anything.

01:56:13.070 --> 01:56:16.380
You're definitely not conscious of any qualia,
you're not conscious of any thought, you're

01:56:16.380 --> 01:56:17.890
not even conscious of consciousness.

01:56:17.890 --> 01:56:20.510
You're just conscious.

01:56:20.510 --> 01:56:21.510
You don't black out.

01:56:21.510 --> 01:56:23.510
You're just conscious.

01:56:23.510 --> 01:56:28.960
All you have is a pure perspectival, participatory
knowing of consciousness.

01:56:28.960 --> 01:56:32.510
You're conscious by being conscious, and there's
nothing else going on.

01:56:32.510 --> 01:56:36.840
Now what's interesting about that is you don't
black out because you can remember being there

01:56:36.840 --> 01:56:38.500
in that state.

01:56:38.500 --> 01:56:43.640
There's no adjectival qualia, but consciousness
is still present.

01:56:43.640 --> 01:56:45.570
So think about that for a second.

01:56:45.570 --> 01:56:47.780
But what doesn't go away?

01:56:47.780 --> 01:56:52.500
You don't lose the here-ness because people
say, in the pure consciousness event, there's

01:56:52.500 --> 01:56:54.930
full presence.

01:56:54.930 --> 01:56:55.930
You don't lose now-ness.

01:56:55.930 --> 01:56:57.719
They talk about, "I'm in eternity."

01:56:57.719 --> 01:56:59.350
This pure now-ness.

01:56:59.350 --> 01:57:01.180
And they don't lose togetherness.

01:57:01.180 --> 01:57:04.710
They talk about pure oneness and unity.

01:57:04.710 --> 01:57:09.331
The adverbial qualia are on steroids and the
adjectival qualia have gone away.

01:57:09.331 --> 01:57:10.331
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's interesting.

01:57:10.331 --> 01:57:12.010
It's a good way to put it.

01:57:12.010 --> 01:57:15.500
John Vervaeke: Which means the adjectival
qualia are not necessary.

01:57:15.500 --> 01:57:16.780
And there's other arguments.

01:57:16.780 --> 01:57:19.389
They're not sufficient for consciousness.

01:57:19.389 --> 01:57:22.300
Consciousness is primarily about adverbial
qualia.

01:57:22.300 --> 01:57:26.120
And adverbial qualia can be explained by relevance
realization.

01:57:26.120 --> 01:57:29.320
It's how are things relevant to you?

01:57:29.320 --> 01:57:33.199
That gives you the centeredness to consciousness.

01:57:33.199 --> 01:57:36.719
The timing of things is an important part
of relevance.

01:57:36.719 --> 01:57:40.590
How things belong together, fit together.

01:57:40.590 --> 01:57:43.659
Relevance realization gives you the adverbial
qualia.

01:57:43.659 --> 01:57:52.869
So if you understand the functionality of
consciousness as heightening relevance realization,

01:57:52.869 --> 01:57:56.929
it will be present in all of those adverbial
qualia.

01:57:56.929 --> 01:58:01.849
And there will be a sense of those even being
heightened when you're in a pure consciousness

01:58:01.849 --> 01:58:02.849
state.

01:58:02.849 --> 01:58:05.849
That's the third and last Vervaeke heresy.

01:58:05.849 --> 01:58:09.050
So it's a completely naturalistic explanation
of consciousness.

01:58:09.050 --> 01:58:10.489
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:58:10.489 --> 01:58:13.310
I've got a number of follow-up questions here.

01:58:13.310 --> 01:58:15.040
I need to set the table a little bit.

01:58:15.040 --> 01:58:25.199
So I was overseas, and ended up in conversation
with two very smart Russian emigres who had

01:58:25.199 --> 01:58:29.390
a lot of biophysics background and exposure.

01:58:29.390 --> 01:58:32.869
And we were having a lot of coffee, or I was
having a lot of coffee.

01:58:32.869 --> 01:58:36.199
Well, at least one of them were only drinking
tea.

01:58:36.199 --> 01:58:40.369
Nonetheless, ended up talking about, and by
talking, I mean me listening to them talk,

01:58:40.369 --> 01:58:44.909
about quantum effects in the brain.

01:58:44.909 --> 01:58:50.800
It was well above my pay grade, but it led
me to really want to have someone on the podcast,

01:58:50.800 --> 01:58:55.329
at some point, who can credibly speak, because
there are a lot of whack-a-doodles into all

01:58:55.329 --> 01:59:00.300
sorts of new age stuff who would love to try
to talk about this, but someone who has credible,

01:59:00.300 --> 01:59:04.619
scientific bonafides that would allow them
to talk about this.

01:59:04.619 --> 01:59:09.260
And this leads to my question, which hopefully
doesn't get me too many heckles from the audience.

01:59:09.260 --> 01:59:12.060
It probably will get a few.

01:59:12.060 --> 01:59:15.570
Do you have any thoughts on panpsychism?

01:59:15.570 --> 01:59:18.850
Is it just nonsense, in your view?

01:59:18.850 --> 01:59:21.849
Is there plausibly something to it?

01:59:21.849 --> 01:59:27.880
There seem to be very strong feelings on many
sides related to this.

01:59:27.880 --> 01:59:30.739
Do you have any thoughts or opinions on the
matter?

01:59:30.739 --> 01:59:32.389
John Vervaeke: Yeah.

01:59:32.389 --> 01:59:37.500
The quantum thing, the best people, I think,
would be Penrose or Hameroff, people like

01:59:37.500 --> 01:59:38.500
that.

01:59:38.500 --> 01:59:41.300
I think it's undeniable.

01:59:41.300 --> 01:59:45.580
We have empirical evidence that there's quantum
stuff going on in smell.

01:59:45.580 --> 01:59:47.360
I think that's undeniable.

01:59:47.360 --> 01:59:51.250
Is there quantum stuff going on in the kind
of consciousness that is overlapping with

01:59:51.250 --> 01:59:54.449
attention and working memory and intelligence?

01:59:54.449 --> 01:59:55.520
No.

01:59:55.520 --> 02:00:01.619
Because you show all kinds of limitations
that are clear evidence that you are operating

02:00:01.619 --> 02:00:05.800
like a classical machine, like all the biases
I talked about and all the ways you ignore

02:00:05.800 --> 02:00:08.340
all kinds of information, blah, blah, blah,
blah.

02:00:08.340 --> 02:00:12.190
And you also have problems about temperature.

02:00:12.190 --> 02:00:14.969
I think those people give the best representation.

02:00:14.969 --> 02:00:16.800
I'm giving you why I'm not convinced by that.

02:00:16.800 --> 02:00:22.429
I think the evidence against it is significantly
greater than any evidence for it.

02:00:22.429 --> 02:00:27.850
And like you, I don't like the argument that
goes consciousness is weird, quantum is weird,

02:00:27.850 --> 02:00:29.989
therefore consciousness is quantum.

02:00:29.989 --> 02:00:31.530
That's just ridiculous.

02:00:31.530 --> 02:00:33.869
Now, panpsychism is a different thing.

02:00:33.869 --> 02:00:36.840
And you don't have to be convinced about quantum
stuff to be a panpsychist.

02:00:36.840 --> 02:00:41.889
Tim Ferriss: And I should have defined my
terms, but would you mind just giving people

02:00:41.889 --> 02:00:45.130
an overview of what panpsychism is?

02:00:45.130 --> 02:00:50.450
John Vervaeke: So panpsychism is a version
of what's called, well, I would argue, it's

02:00:50.450 --> 02:00:53.860
a version of what's called elemental property
dualism.

02:00:53.860 --> 02:01:03.409
And so the idea here is, we have fundamental
building blocks of our ontology, space, time,

02:01:03.409 --> 02:01:05.790
force, matter, energy.

02:01:05.790 --> 02:01:09.429
And we may move those around, but that that's
not important right now.

02:01:09.429 --> 02:01:12.500
And we don't try and explain them.

02:01:12.500 --> 02:01:16.670
We use them to explain other things because
they're fundamental.

02:01:16.670 --> 02:01:21.520
And like I say, physics, some people are arguing,
we may reject time and space as fundamental

02:01:21.520 --> 02:01:22.890
and move to something deeper.

02:01:22.890 --> 02:01:23.890
I don't know.

02:01:23.890 --> 02:01:26.290
But that's not relevant to the core of the
argument.

02:01:26.290 --> 02:01:30.330
The core of the argument is that consciousness
is like that.

02:01:30.330 --> 02:01:35.060
Consciousness is a fundamental, ontological
building block, and therefore the attempt

02:01:35.060 --> 02:01:40.219
to explain it with any of the other fundamental
building blocks is impossible.

02:01:40.219 --> 02:01:45.480
You can't do it because they are at the same
ontological, ontology, meaning the structure

02:01:45.480 --> 02:01:46.480
of reality.

02:01:46.480 --> 02:01:47.980
They're at the same ontological level.

02:01:47.980 --> 02:01:51.770
They're the basement level in terms of which
you explain everything else.

02:01:51.770 --> 02:01:58.860
So the attempt to try and explain them is
a misframing of the kind of thing they are.

02:01:58.860 --> 02:02:05.730
And so that means if consciousness is such
a fundamental ontological building block like

02:02:05.730 --> 02:02:14.560
space, time, energy, force, and energy and
matter are convertible, Einstein, there is

02:02:14.560 --> 02:02:20.920
no part of reality, even empty space has got
all of these fundamental ontological building

02:02:20.920 --> 02:02:22.590
blocks.

02:02:22.590 --> 02:02:28.429
And if consciousness is such a fundamental
building block, it is also showing up in everything.

02:02:28.429 --> 02:02:35.960
So my table has consciousness, not my full-blown
consciousness, but it has what it's like to

02:02:35.960 --> 02:02:37.380
be a table.

02:02:37.380 --> 02:02:41.820
Like, my consciousness is about perspectival
knowing.

02:02:41.820 --> 02:02:48.679
And so, it has what's like to be a table,
it has an inner subjective experiential aspect.

02:02:48.679 --> 02:02:51.050
Now not full-blown like mine.

02:02:51.050 --> 02:02:55.810
Nobody's ridiculous in claiming there's some
sort of consciousness like mine somehow trapped

02:02:55.810 --> 02:02:57.920
in this table, unable to speak or something.

02:02:57.920 --> 02:02:59.800
We have no evidence for that.

02:02:59.800 --> 02:03:07.239
But the idea is that there are aspects of
what it is to be a table that are what it's

02:03:07.239 --> 02:03:11.540
like to be a table for the table and not for
me or for you.

02:03:11.540 --> 02:03:14.500
And that would be what the claim of panpsychism
is.

02:03:14.500 --> 02:03:21.110
The person who best makes it plausible, to
my mind, well, not necessarily agreeing, is

02:03:21.110 --> 02:03:24.530
a colleague of mine, Bill Seager.

02:03:24.530 --> 02:03:29.190
He's made a very strong case for the plausibility
of panpsychism.

02:03:29.190 --> 02:03:35.520
Now, I reject panpsychism, although I accept
things that come close to it in some important

02:03:35.520 --> 02:03:36.520
ways.

02:03:36.520 --> 02:03:38.810
Tim Ferriss: What do you reject and what do
you accept?

02:03:38.810 --> 02:03:44.369
John Vervaeke: I don't think that there's
anything like perspectival knowing going on

02:03:44.369 --> 02:03:45.880
at the level of the table.

02:03:45.880 --> 02:03:56.869
But I'm confident enough about my ignorance
of consciousness and reality to not say I'm

02:03:56.869 --> 02:03:59.760
sure and slam on the table.

02:03:59.760 --> 02:04:04.349
I do think, and this is work of Evan Thompson
and others in 4E cogsci, and Michael Levin,

02:04:04.349 --> 02:04:06.430
I think there's a deep continuity.

02:04:06.430 --> 02:04:13.120
Meaning, the principles of consciousness have
a deep continuity with the principles of cognition,

02:04:13.120 --> 02:04:16.950
which have a deep continuity with the principles
of life, which have a deep continuity with

02:04:16.950 --> 02:04:21.239
the principles of self-organization, which
have a deep continuity with some of the fundamental

02:04:21.239 --> 02:04:24.510
ways in which reality unfolds for us.

02:04:24.510 --> 02:04:26.370
And so, they're not identical.

02:04:26.370 --> 02:04:30.659
You can't simply reduce the upper level to
the lower levels, but there are shared principles

02:04:30.659 --> 02:04:32.329
all the way through.

02:04:32.329 --> 02:04:38.360
And I think the evidence for a deep continuity
hypothesis is very good, and the arguments

02:04:38.360 --> 02:04:39.620
are very important.

02:04:39.620 --> 02:04:47.510
So I do think, for example, it's proper to
say that there's a rudimentary cognition for

02:04:47.510 --> 02:04:48.510
a perimysium.

02:04:48.510 --> 02:04:57.369
It's a cognitive agent, it's detecting the
sugar molecule as food, and this other molecule

02:04:57.369 --> 02:05:00.050
as poison.

02:05:00.050 --> 02:05:04.409
It's a little bit of an aspect, a little bit
of a perspective, and it allows it to care

02:05:04.409 --> 02:05:05.860
about this rather than that.

02:05:05.860 --> 02:05:08.340
Now, is that what you and I have?

02:05:08.340 --> 02:05:09.840
No, something like that.

02:05:09.840 --> 02:05:12.550
Does that extend below living things?

02:05:12.550 --> 02:05:17.440
I don't think so, because self-organizing
things like the fire don't seem to direct

02:05:17.440 --> 02:05:22.090
their behavior to preserve their own existence
the way living things do.

02:05:22.090 --> 02:05:26.469
And I think that's a fundamental difference
in how things are organized and how they function.

02:05:26.469 --> 02:05:32.150
So I don't think consciousness and cognition
or agency drop below the level of living things.

02:05:32.150 --> 02:05:35.900
Tim Ferriss: This is not directly related,
but I'm curious.

02:05:35.900 --> 02:05:44.530
You were mentioning the intimacy, the, let's
call it atypical intimacy that nonetheless

02:05:44.530 --> 02:05:49.929
seems, I'm paraphrasing, familiar to those
who are doing circling.

02:05:49.929 --> 02:05:58.500
And I'm wondering what are some of the most
unusual modes of cognition or relating or

02:05:58.500 --> 02:06:03.040
perceiving that you have noticed in those
types of exercises?

02:06:03.040 --> 02:06:06.989
John Vervaeke: In circling and philosophical
fellowship and dialectic into dia-logos?

02:06:06.989 --> 02:06:07.989
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

02:06:07.989 --> 02:06:09.040
John Vervaeke: All of these.

02:06:09.040 --> 02:06:12.750
First of all, especially when you take people
through this and they start doing a lot of

02:06:12.750 --> 02:06:18.650
that whole ecology into dialectic into dia-logos,
they will discover a nestings of intimacies.

02:06:18.650 --> 02:06:24.860
They will discover first interpersonal intimacy
between you and I. And then they'll discover

02:06:24.860 --> 02:06:31.040
intimacy between all of us and what is called
the we space, this emergent sort of dynamical

02:06:31.040 --> 02:06:37.370
system that is sort of linking us and coupling
us all together.

02:06:37.370 --> 02:06:42.619
People will start to get a sense of intimacy
to the we space, Chris and I call it the logos,

02:06:42.619 --> 02:06:43.619
or a German word, the geist.

02:06:43.619 --> 02:06:51.230
And then there's a third intimacy that people
sometimes get to, which is, they start to

02:06:51.230 --> 02:06:57.170
get an intimacy through the interpersonal
and through the intimacy with the we space

02:06:57.170 --> 02:07:02.179
to sort of like the ground, the origin, the
fount of intelligibility.

02:07:02.179 --> 02:07:07.620
They get a sense of intimacy that we talked
about earlier as a sense of the sacred.

02:07:07.620 --> 02:07:14.010
And what's really interesting about this is,
people from all kinds of backgrounds, resolutely

02:07:14.010 --> 02:07:20.389
secular, will start to use spiritual and religious
language to talk about these kinds of intimacies.

02:07:20.389 --> 02:07:26.640
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's a curious, I think,
indicator.

02:07:26.640 --> 02:07:30.340
It's like, when secular language fails, where
do you go?

02:07:30.340 --> 02:07:31.340
Uh-oh.

02:07:31.340 --> 02:07:34.920
What are you going to do?

02:07:34.920 --> 02:07:41.389
You, as I understand it, had an office, I
believe, directly across the hall or close

02:07:41.389 --> 02:07:45.460
to Dr. Jordan Peterson.

02:07:45.460 --> 02:07:55.380
And my question is, with respect to the finding
or creation of meaning, or meaning, broadly

02:07:55.380 --> 02:08:00.690
speaking, where would you say the two of you
most strongly agree, and where do you most

02:08:00.690 --> 02:08:01.719
strongly disagree?

02:08:01.719 --> 02:08:08.400
John Vervaeke: Way before Jordan became a
god, Jordan and I, we used to regularly show

02:08:08.400 --> 02:08:09.550
up at conferences.

02:08:09.550 --> 02:08:12.809
We shared students a lot.

02:08:12.809 --> 02:08:18.489
We had some public debates, meaning the proper,
good sense.

02:08:18.489 --> 02:08:24.300
And so Jordan shares with me this idea of
relevance realization as a core problem to

02:08:24.300 --> 02:08:26.470
be explained and understood.

02:08:26.470 --> 02:08:31.369
And the relevance realization gives us that
sense of religio, that sense of connectedness

02:08:31.369 --> 02:08:34.730
because it's how we're coupled non-propositionally
to the world.

02:08:34.730 --> 02:08:36.639
We've already talked about this.

02:08:36.639 --> 02:08:38.489
Jordan and I share that a lot.

02:08:38.489 --> 02:08:42.730
And he's very, very interested, and often
supportive of the work I do.

02:08:42.730 --> 02:08:50.370
I'm relevance realization, intelligence, consciousness,
meaning, and the cultivation of wisdom.

02:08:50.370 --> 02:08:54.630
And so that's where we have some very significant
agreements.

02:08:54.630 --> 02:08:58.070
He places more emphasis on the narrative.

02:08:58.070 --> 02:08:59.080
On narrative than I do.

02:08:59.080 --> 02:09:02.619
I think narrative is very important.

02:09:02.619 --> 02:09:04.340
I want to make that very clear.

02:09:04.340 --> 02:09:08.719
I don't think we become temporally extended
moral agents without narrative.

02:09:08.719 --> 02:09:15.989
But I think narrative depends on pre-narrative
dialogical capacities, and we can move post

02:09:15.989 --> 02:09:19.579
narrative, because this is what many people
in wisdom traditions, mystical traditions

02:09:19.579 --> 02:09:21.559
report, they move post narrative.

02:09:21.559 --> 02:09:25.219
And you see techniques for getting people
to go post narrative.

02:09:25.219 --> 02:09:30.469
Think about the Zen koan, it's designed to
get you into a narrative and then break narrative

02:09:30.469 --> 02:09:31.469
apart.

02:09:31.469 --> 02:09:36.050
Or Jesus of Nazareth's parables, they sound
like stories and there's a narrative.

02:09:36.050 --> 02:09:39.869
And you read the prodigal son, and then, who
am I supposed to identify with?

02:09:39.869 --> 02:09:40.909
Who's right?

02:09:40.909 --> 02:09:42.469
Is it the father?

02:09:42.469 --> 02:09:43.989
Is it the prodigal son?

02:09:43.989 --> 02:09:47.179
And you realize, if you settle on any one
of those, you haven't got it.

02:09:47.179 --> 02:09:49.510
And it blows the narrative apart.

02:09:49.510 --> 02:09:52.750
And sorry if this sounds pretentious, I mean
it respectfully.

02:09:52.750 --> 02:09:59.750
Jesus is a master of saying these things that
sort of blow people apart, blow the narrative

02:09:59.750 --> 02:10:01.390
apart.

02:10:01.390 --> 02:10:06.619
And we have done this publicly, he and I had
a discussion with Jonathan Pageau, and I was

02:10:06.619 --> 02:10:11.380
challenging Jonathan and Jordan about the
emphasis on the narrative.

02:10:11.380 --> 02:10:16.820
And that, of course, comes from Jordan, from
his Jungian background, and from Jonathan,

02:10:16.820 --> 02:10:18.309
from his Eastern Orthodox.

02:10:18.309 --> 02:10:23.329
Although Jonathan, to give him due credit,
came around later and he released a video

02:10:23.329 --> 02:10:25.659
where he said, "John's right.

02:10:25.659 --> 02:10:28.300
The nomological is not reducible to the narrative.

02:10:28.300 --> 02:10:31.909
There's ways in which we connect to reality
that are non-narrative and are important."

02:10:31.909 --> 02:10:33.270
And I thought that was really good of him.

02:10:33.270 --> 02:10:35.150
So that'd be one area where Jordan and I disagree.

02:10:35.150 --> 02:10:39.010
Tim Ferriss: Do you have to get the narrative
before you can go post narrative?

02:10:39.010 --> 02:10:40.980
John Vervaeke: I think so.

02:10:40.980 --> 02:10:43.300
And this is where I think the difference is.

02:10:43.300 --> 02:10:49.730
I think that narrative is indispensable, but
I do not think that means it's metaphysically

02:10:49.730 --> 02:10:51.170
necessary.

02:10:51.170 --> 02:10:53.150
Let me give you an analogy.

02:10:53.150 --> 02:10:55.739
My only language that I'm fluent in is English.

02:10:55.739 --> 02:11:00.760
English is indispensable to me being a cognitive
agent and communicating, but I wouldn't claim

02:11:00.760 --> 02:11:05.409
it is metaphysically necessary that every
cognitive agent speak in English and only

02:11:05.409 --> 02:11:08.150
in English or has to know English.

02:11:08.150 --> 02:11:09.690
That's ridiculous.

02:11:09.690 --> 02:11:13.079
So you have to make a distinction between
indispensability.

02:11:13.079 --> 02:11:19.980
And I think narrative is developmentally indispensable,
but I don't think it's metaphysically necessary

02:11:19.980 --> 02:11:21.559
to the full development of our cognition.

02:11:21.559 --> 02:11:23.989
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so you have that disagreement.

02:11:23.989 --> 02:11:31.770
You have different weighting of narrative
and not the alternative, but then the term

02:11:31.770 --> 02:11:37.489
that you mentioned, your colleague used —
John Vervaeke: The nomological, having to

02:11:37.489 --> 02:11:40.390
do with the lawlike structures for reality.

02:11:40.390 --> 02:11:41.469
Tim Ferriss: There we go.

02:11:41.469 --> 02:11:46.150
John Vervaeke: Laws and theories are powerful
and important in a way that's not reducible

02:11:46.150 --> 02:11:48.059
to narratives.

02:11:48.059 --> 02:11:49.340
And that's one area.

02:11:49.340 --> 02:11:56.230
And then another area is, I put a lot more
emphasis on practices and theorizing and helping

02:11:56.230 --> 02:12:00.489
to engineer practices and ecology of practices
than Jordan does.

02:12:00.489 --> 02:12:01.489
That's another major difference.

02:12:01.489 --> 02:12:02.780
Tim Ferriss: Why do you think that is?

02:12:02.780 --> 02:12:06.840
What would his response to that be, would
you imagine?

02:12:06.840 --> 02:12:09.929
John Vervaeke: Part of it, I think, in fairness
to him, is background.

02:12:09.929 --> 02:12:15.630
And part of it has to do, I think, with, Jordan
is a very complex person.

02:12:15.630 --> 02:12:20.199
I respect Jordan, I consider him a colleague
and I consider him a friend.

02:12:20.199 --> 02:12:24.730
And what I want to say very clearly, and I
do this to his face, by the way, and because

02:12:24.730 --> 02:12:27.980
I do it respectfully and dialogically, Jordan
is fine with this.

02:12:27.980 --> 02:12:29.869
We disagree about things.

02:12:29.869 --> 02:12:36.220
For example, I think postmodernism and Derrida
and Foucault, there's a lot to be learned

02:12:36.220 --> 02:12:37.220
there.

02:12:37.220 --> 02:12:40.940
I have criticisms of them, but there are important
arguments that need to be engaged with.

02:12:40.940 --> 02:12:44.410
I have argued this with Jordan to his face,
he disagrees.

02:12:44.410 --> 02:12:48.780
He thinks that postmodernism is largely crypto-Marxism,
et cetera, et cetera.

02:12:48.780 --> 02:12:51.660
And he rejects it sort of to core.

02:12:51.660 --> 02:13:00.010
I'm not denying that postmodernism has been
taken by people in this way, but I would also

02:13:00.010 --> 02:13:02.550
say that many people invoke Derrida and Foucault.

02:13:02.550 --> 02:13:06.320
They've not actually read Derrida and Foucault.

02:13:06.320 --> 02:13:10.969
Derrida is very interested in Neoplatonism.

02:13:10.969 --> 02:13:16.559
And Foucault was very interested in Hadot
and Stoicism towards the end of his career.

02:13:16.559 --> 02:13:17.690
And there's reasons for that.

02:13:17.690 --> 02:13:21.619
So I think you have to have a much more nuanced
understanding of postmodernism.

02:13:21.619 --> 02:13:26.159
Now, I've made that argument to Jordan and
we disagree about it.

02:13:26.159 --> 02:13:30.659
But because I respect him, and we enter into
genuine dialogue, that's fine.

02:13:30.659 --> 02:13:36.079
Now, the problem is, Jordan also has this
other side where, and he doesn't do this with

02:13:36.079 --> 02:13:44.020
me, where he's very confrontational, and he
admits this, and he gets very polemical, and

02:13:44.020 --> 02:13:46.159
it's usually in the political domain.

02:13:46.159 --> 02:13:51.059
And that's an area when he and I don't agree
very much.

02:13:51.059 --> 02:14:02.199
But, to his credit, Jordan respects that he
and I can have significant different political

02:14:02.199 --> 02:14:07.329
commitments and nevertheless get into deep
and important discussions of philosophical

02:14:07.329 --> 02:14:10.200
and scientific merit.

02:14:10.200 --> 02:14:15.079
I have to say that because I'm really tired
of people saying that because I talk to Jordan

02:14:15.079 --> 02:14:20.159
and because I maintain the relationship that
pre-existed his godhood that I am somehow

02:14:20.159 --> 02:14:27.469
advising or advocating for everything he says.

02:14:27.469 --> 02:14:29.090
That's ridiculous.

02:14:29.090 --> 02:14:32.900
I directly criticize him about a lot of things.

02:14:32.900 --> 02:14:38.500
It's just I think it should be done in the
way that we do it and we've always done it.

02:14:38.500 --> 02:14:41.070
And so that's a hard thing.

02:14:41.070 --> 02:14:44.659
My relationship to him is a challenge for
me.

02:14:44.659 --> 02:14:46.170
I've considered both options.

02:14:46.170 --> 02:14:50.270
I've considered sort of just sort of throwing
in, and I'm part of the Petersonian camp.

02:14:50.270 --> 02:14:51.840
And it's like, no, I can't do that.

02:14:51.840 --> 02:14:53.179
I can't do that, in all honesty.

02:14:53.179 --> 02:14:57.740
I disagree with Jordan about politics as the
main area.

02:14:57.740 --> 02:15:02.320
I disagree for philosophical reasons with
Jordan about politics being the main area

02:15:02.320 --> 02:15:04.739
in which we can solve the meaning crisis.

02:15:04.739 --> 02:15:09.820
Politics is at the propositional level, and
is at the adversarial level.

02:15:09.820 --> 02:15:12.179
We need the non-propositional.

02:15:12.179 --> 02:15:15.820
That's the main place where the meaning crisis
is going to be addressed, I would argue.

02:15:15.820 --> 02:15:17.659
I disagree with him about that.

02:15:17.659 --> 02:15:22.989
I disagreed with him about particular philosophical
stances, like his stance on postmodernism,

02:15:22.989 --> 02:15:25.090
et cetera.

02:15:25.090 --> 02:15:28.349
And so, completely identifying him would be
inauthentic.

02:15:28.349 --> 02:15:30.719
Completely rejecting him would be inauthentic.

02:15:30.719 --> 02:15:32.650
We share a lot of concerns.

02:15:32.650 --> 02:15:35.469
I admire many of his ideas.

02:15:35.469 --> 02:15:38.920
I think he's done good work, published work.

02:15:38.920 --> 02:15:40.699
He continues to make good arguments.

02:15:40.699 --> 02:15:42.389
He's an insightful person.

02:15:42.389 --> 02:15:44.010
And I have an ongoing history with him.

02:15:44.010 --> 02:15:47.079
And he talks to people that I talk to.

02:15:47.079 --> 02:15:50.369
So I'm damned if I do, damned if I don't.

02:15:50.369 --> 02:15:57.070
So I sort of just say, well, okay, what I'm
going to do is I'm just going to show up and

02:15:57.070 --> 02:16:02.679
interact with him as philosophically and scientifically
honestly as I can.

02:16:02.679 --> 02:16:10.960
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I had Jordan on this podcast,
and it was incredible to see how strongly

02:16:10.960 --> 02:16:13.949
binary, not just the responses —
John Vervaeke: He's a polarizing guy.

02:16:13.949 --> 02:16:17.550
Tim Ferriss: But also the expectations of
me were all in.

02:16:17.550 --> 02:16:19.690
Are you all in or are you all out?

02:16:19.690 --> 02:16:28.720
And there was part of me that wanted to say,
this is the kind of identity politics morass

02:16:28.720 --> 02:16:31.800
that we have as such an issue.

02:16:31.800 --> 02:16:39.149
To have any kind of reconciliation and civil
debate, it can't be, well, I'm making a strong

02:16:39.149 --> 02:16:42.530
statement, but it shouldn't have to be that
black or white.

02:16:42.530 --> 02:16:45.590
I think there are many areas —
John Vervaeke: I want to strengthen your argument

02:16:45.590 --> 02:16:46.590
for you.

02:16:46.590 --> 02:16:47.590
I want to strengthen your argument for you.

02:16:47.590 --> 02:16:49.469
Tim Ferriss: Please, I need someone to do
this with me more regularly.

02:16:49.469 --> 02:16:51.490
John Vervaeke: This has to do with adaptivity.

02:16:51.490 --> 02:16:53.170
This has to do with intelligence.

02:16:53.170 --> 02:16:58.240
Let's just give you an example of relevance
realization right here right now you have

02:16:58.240 --> 02:17:02.910
two attentional systems, probably more, but
at least these two.

02:17:02.910 --> 02:17:07.569
You have the task focus that is trying to
keep you focused on, what is John saying with

02:17:07.569 --> 02:17:10.570
his multisyllabic complex sentences?

02:17:10.570 --> 02:17:13.700
And then there's a part of you, the default
network that's mind wandering.

02:17:13.700 --> 02:17:15.030
And I'm not accusing you of anything.

02:17:15.030 --> 02:17:20.030
It's drifting away, it's thinking about other
things, and they're locked together.

02:17:20.030 --> 02:17:23.769
Like evolution, the mind wandering introduces
variation.

02:17:23.769 --> 02:17:30.479
The task network kills most of it off, but
some of it survives and helps reproduce the

02:17:30.479 --> 02:17:32.809
conversation and keep it going.

02:17:32.809 --> 02:17:39.700
Opponent processing, your autonomic nervous
system about arousal is biased to arousing

02:17:39.700 --> 02:17:40.700
you.

02:17:40.700 --> 02:17:44.700
Parasympathetic is biased to getting you to
calm down, and they're locked together in

02:17:44.700 --> 02:17:46.240
opponent processing.

02:17:46.240 --> 02:17:49.349
You find this all through adaptive systems.

02:17:49.349 --> 02:17:50.889
Opponent processing.

02:17:50.889 --> 02:17:52.439
Democracy.

02:17:52.439 --> 02:17:58.679
I argued this when I gave a talk at the international
symposium on democracy in Prague in September.

02:17:58.679 --> 02:18:02.360
Democracy should be opponent processing.

02:18:02.360 --> 02:18:08.479
The best way for me to correct my perspectival
bias is to have you be in opponent processing

02:18:08.479 --> 02:18:09.479
with me.

02:18:09.479 --> 02:18:15.250
You take a different bias, but we agree that
we are each other's best means of self-correction.

02:18:15.250 --> 02:18:17.030
That's how democracy should work.

02:18:17.030 --> 02:18:24.210
And we have degenerated it into adversarial,
the other side is evil and must be destroyed

02:18:24.210 --> 02:18:30.530
because we are perfectly right and correct
with a sinful, and I use that word advisedly,

02:18:30.530 --> 02:18:33.870
a sinful self-righteousness on both sides.

02:18:33.870 --> 02:18:38.380
A pox on both of your houses, I would say
to that, because you're undermining the very

02:18:38.380 --> 02:18:46.340
opponent processing that is necessary for
democracy to be a good means for us to use

02:18:46.340 --> 02:18:50.309
extended cognition to solve our complex problems.

02:18:50.309 --> 02:18:51.610
So that's how I'm strengthening your argument.

02:18:51.610 --> 02:18:53.370
Tim Ferriss: That's what I meant to say.

02:18:53.370 --> 02:18:55.030
So thank you.

02:18:55.030 --> 02:19:02.710
John Vervaeke: It goes something I said earlier,
stop trying to demonize or deify any one of

02:19:02.710 --> 02:19:04.110
your faculties.

02:19:04.110 --> 02:19:08.060
Stop trying to demonize or deify Jordan.

02:19:08.060 --> 02:19:10.670
Confront his arguments, argument by argument.

02:19:10.670 --> 02:19:12.769
Some of them are good, some of them are bad.

02:19:12.769 --> 02:19:16.590
Even some of his bad arguments need to be
taken seriously because they're well-made

02:19:16.590 --> 02:19:18.330
and they contain insight.

02:19:18.330 --> 02:19:24.090
Some of the things you reject, but reject
it because of the content of the argument,

02:19:24.090 --> 02:19:26.000
not because of the side you're on.

02:19:26.000 --> 02:19:28.240
What is that accomplishing?

02:19:28.240 --> 02:19:33.230
That is not accomplishing any overcoming of
your or my self-deception.

02:19:33.230 --> 02:19:38.990
All it's doing is reinforcing whatever confirmation
bias we are already enslaved to.

02:19:38.990 --> 02:19:43.729
Tim Ferriss: Talking about opponent processing
makes me think about some of my close friendships

02:19:43.729 --> 02:19:52.370
and how I choose to develop friendships with
people who can help with the checks and balances

02:19:52.370 --> 02:19:59.030
and the constructive pushback when helpful.

02:19:59.030 --> 02:20:05.851
And that leads me to want to ask you, and
this is very self-interested of me, but it's

02:20:05.851 --> 02:20:11.590
something that's on my mind a lot because
most of my friends are non-religious, but

02:20:11.590 --> 02:20:15.140
looking to create meaning or find meaning,
as we all are.

02:20:15.140 --> 02:20:23.819
How would you advise, say, a group of middle-aged
friends to support one another in having more

02:20:23.819 --> 02:20:25.970
meaningful lives or creating meaning?

02:20:25.970 --> 02:20:29.400
What can people do in a small cohort?

02:20:29.400 --> 02:20:32.470
Would you have any recommendations?

02:20:32.470 --> 02:20:35.100
John Vervaeke: Don't just swap beliefs.

02:20:35.100 --> 02:20:36.859
That's insufficient.

02:20:36.859 --> 02:20:41.271
That's just the propositional.

02:20:41.271 --> 02:20:47.090
Commit to taking up together a living ecology
of practices.

02:20:47.090 --> 02:20:52.590
Commit to doing that together and bring that
opponent processing into the non-propositional

02:20:52.590 --> 02:20:56.970
aspects of your cognition, into the extended
aspects of your cognition.

02:20:56.970 --> 02:21:02.200
Learn how to do some of these practices I've
been talking about.

02:21:02.200 --> 02:21:08.240
Take them up together and take up ones that
involve you practicing together.

02:21:08.240 --> 02:21:14.149
Because a big part of what the church and
the temple and the mosque did was exactly

02:21:14.149 --> 02:21:15.149
that.

02:21:15.149 --> 02:21:23.120
They provided a home, a tradition in which
ecologies of practices were properly homed

02:21:23.120 --> 02:21:29.740
and supported so that people could come together
and not just communicate, but commune together

02:21:29.740 --> 02:21:34.620
by sharing participation, I'm emphasizing
all these words because of all the ways we've

02:21:34.620 --> 02:21:41.320
talked about them, in shared ecologies of
practices that they mutually conform to and

02:21:41.320 --> 02:21:48.220
transform themselves to, and thereby, mutually
set up a space where they can afford each

02:21:48.220 --> 02:21:54.649
other's transformations, where you can cultivate
together enhanced religio, enhanced sense

02:21:54.649 --> 02:21:59.730
of sacredness, enhanced sense of the depths
within you, calling to the depths of others

02:21:59.730 --> 02:22:02.560
and calling to the depths of reality.

02:22:02.560 --> 02:22:05.260
Don't tell me what you believe, tell me what
you practice.

02:22:05.260 --> 02:22:10.410
Tell me how you're practicing together and
how you're acting as checks and balances and

02:22:10.410 --> 02:22:18.350
opponent processing on the creation and the
running of these ecologies of practices.

02:22:18.350 --> 02:22:22.399
And commit to it the way you commit to a friendship.

02:22:22.399 --> 02:22:25.050
Your friend is a complex, dynamical system.

02:22:25.050 --> 02:22:28.050
An ecology of practice is a complex dynamical
system.

02:22:28.050 --> 02:22:33.310
It takes a time and effort and commitment
and a willingness to transform and fail and

02:22:33.310 --> 02:22:36.040
learn from mistakes in order to make this
work.

02:22:36.040 --> 02:22:38.760
Get into an ecology of practice.

02:22:38.760 --> 02:22:44.820
Try to make sure that that ecology of practice
is in ongoing dialogue and discussion with

02:22:44.820 --> 02:22:50.310
the best cognitive science out there right
now, so you don't go off in weird rabbit holes

02:22:50.310 --> 02:22:55.610
that can happen when people do this, so that
you stay tied to the best science we have

02:22:55.610 --> 02:22:59.950
about how cognition and consciousness and
intelligence and distributed cognition and

02:22:59.950 --> 02:23:01.710
flow, et cetera, work.

02:23:01.710 --> 02:23:05.180
That is the single most important thing.

02:23:05.180 --> 02:23:07.050
Now I've offered lots.

02:23:07.050 --> 02:23:11.660
I have a full meditation course and contemplation
course, cultivation of wisdom course.

02:23:11.660 --> 02:23:13.630
I have the whole After Socrates.

02:23:13.630 --> 02:23:15.430
There's lots out there.

02:23:15.430 --> 02:23:17.010
And you don't have to just pay attention to
me.

02:23:17.010 --> 02:23:22.851
You can pay attention to the ecologies of
practices like Rafe Kelley.

02:23:22.851 --> 02:23:26.580
I went to Return to the Source last summer,
blew me apart.

02:23:26.580 --> 02:23:28.689
Just an amazing ecology of practices.

02:23:28.689 --> 02:23:33.270
I've been doing a lot of participant observation
and experiment in other people's ecologies

02:23:33.270 --> 02:23:35.330
of practices.

02:23:35.330 --> 02:23:40.520
You have to become a connoisseur and a creator
of ecologies of practices.

02:23:40.520 --> 02:23:46.510
There's no other way to enhance the capacity
for participating in meaning.

02:23:46.510 --> 02:23:49.660
That would be the most central advice I would
give somebody.

02:23:49.660 --> 02:23:53.090
Tim Ferriss: I was meaning, and I will now
ask about After Socrates.

02:23:53.090 --> 02:23:58.160
So you have this very popular YouTube series,
Awakening from the Meaning Crisis.

02:23:58.160 --> 02:24:00.120
Why create After Socrates?

02:24:00.120 --> 02:24:03.529
John Vervaeke: I listened to the criticism
of other people.

02:24:03.529 --> 02:24:08.800
Awakening from the Meaning Crisis has a couple
of main criticisms that people made.

02:24:08.800 --> 02:24:12.390
One was, a lot of people asked at the end,
"But what do I do now?

02:24:12.390 --> 02:24:14.210
What do I do now?

02:24:14.210 --> 02:24:18.640
You've convinced me about the meaning crisis
and this problem, and blah blah.

02:24:18.640 --> 02:24:19.680
But what do I do now?"

02:24:19.680 --> 02:24:26.460
And then secondly, "You did this totally individualistically,
but how does this work with other people?"

02:24:26.460 --> 02:24:31.920
So After Socrates is not extensive, it's intensive,
it's trying to trace the entire tradition

02:24:31.920 --> 02:24:38.810
from Socrates, and spending a lot of time
there.

02:24:38.810 --> 02:24:39.810
Reverse engineer.

02:24:39.810 --> 02:24:42.560
I'm not claiming this is exactly what Socrates
did, who could claim that?

02:24:42.560 --> 02:24:47.710
I'm trying to reverse engineer as best I can
this dialectic into dia-logos, these kinds

02:24:47.710 --> 02:24:54.460
of practices we've been talking about, so
that people can undertake a pedagogical program.

02:24:54.460 --> 02:25:00.110
So Tim, every episode I give an argument,
and they build on each other, like Awakening

02:25:00.110 --> 02:25:01.550
from the Meaning Crisis.

02:25:01.550 --> 02:25:05.870
But I also give points of reflection that
are designed to be taken up in discussion

02:25:05.870 --> 02:25:07.240
by groups.

02:25:07.240 --> 02:25:12.150
And then I give instruction in a particular
practice that resonates with the content of

02:25:12.150 --> 02:25:13.150
the lecture.

02:25:13.150 --> 02:25:17.920
So as the lecture builds the argument, intergroup
discussion is being built and a pedagogical

02:25:17.920 --> 02:25:24.690
program is being built so that you can come
to take up the Socratic way of life in community

02:25:24.690 --> 02:25:31.120
with other people who are taking it up and
cultivating an extended and developed ecology

02:25:31.120 --> 02:25:32.189
of practices.

02:25:32.189 --> 02:25:33.720
That's what After Socrates is all about.

02:25:33.720 --> 02:25:35.960
Tim Ferriss: Where can people find After Socrates?

02:25:35.960 --> 02:25:37.621
Where's the best place for people if they
want to learn more?

02:25:37.621 --> 02:25:39.880
John Vervaeke: Just go on my YouTube channel.

02:25:39.880 --> 02:25:41.360
It's right there for free.

02:25:41.360 --> 02:25:47.000
Tim Ferriss: All right, we will certainly
link to the YouTube channel, the website,

02:25:47.000 --> 02:25:48.000
JohnVervaeke.com.

02:25:48.000 --> 02:25:49.320
On Twitter, @vervaeke_john.

02:25:49.320 --> 02:25:51.890
John, this has been such a pleasure.

02:25:51.890 --> 02:25:53.580
I've really enjoyed this conversation.

02:25:53.580 --> 02:25:58.470
Thank you for taking so much time and being
so patient with my questions.

02:25:58.470 --> 02:26:00.290
Hopefully it wasn't too torturous.

02:26:00.290 --> 02:26:01.290
I do appreciate it.

02:26:01.290 --> 02:26:02.290
John Vervaeke: You had it all.

02:26:02.290 --> 02:26:05.010
I really enjoyed it so much so that I'll answer
your question.

02:26:05.010 --> 02:26:07.380
If you want me ever to come back, I'd be very
happy to do so.

02:26:07.380 --> 02:26:08.421
Tim Ferriss: I would love to do it?

02:26:08.421 --> 02:26:09.421
I would love to do a round two.

02:26:09.421 --> 02:26:12.720
Is there anything else you would like to say?

02:26:12.720 --> 02:26:17.279
Any closing comments, any requests you'd like
to make of my audience?

02:26:17.279 --> 02:26:20.601
Anything at all that you'd like to add before
we wind to a close?

02:26:20.601 --> 02:26:22.279
John Vervaeke: I don't think so.

02:26:22.279 --> 02:26:29.329
I would ask people to consider the two series,
Awakening from the Meaning Crisis, After Socrates.

02:26:29.329 --> 02:26:33.210
Take a look at some of the conversations I'm
having with other people.

02:26:33.210 --> 02:26:38.530
Try to give me the benefit of the doubt when
I'm talking to people you might not like.

02:26:38.530 --> 02:26:39.910
Tim Ferriss: Good advice.

02:26:39.910 --> 02:26:43.240
John Vervaeke: I guess that's all I would
have to say right now.

02:26:43.240 --> 02:26:49.910
And again, I was a little intimidated, and
I'm not usually thinking about talking to

02:26:49.910 --> 02:26:50.910
you.

02:26:50.910 --> 02:26:55.570
You have quite a powerful, like I said before
we turned the recording on, reputation.

02:26:55.570 --> 02:27:00.410
And yet you were so welcoming and accommodating.

02:27:00.410 --> 02:27:04.950
You and I, I think, got into a mutual flow
state quite a few times.

02:27:04.950 --> 02:27:08.040
I think it was really wonderful.

02:27:08.040 --> 02:27:09.040
So thank you very much.

02:27:09.040 --> 02:27:10.220
Tim Ferriss: Oh, thank you, John.

02:27:10.220 --> 02:27:15.479
Thank you for being such a great jazz player
on the stage.

02:27:15.479 --> 02:27:17.720
And I really, really enjoyed it.

02:27:17.720 --> 02:27:25.120
I took tons of notes, so I have quite a lot
