02:27:49.410 --> 02:27:52.700
And remember, it's not just what you believe.

02:27:52.700 --> 02:27:54.080
It is what you practice.

02:27:54.080 --> 02:27:56.280
So get to practicing, folks.

02:27:56.280 --> 02:27:58.350
And as always, thanks for tuning in.

02:27:58.350 --> 02:27:59.730
John Vervaeke: Thank you, everyone.

02:27:59.730 --> 02:28:02.729
[sound effect]
Tim Ferriss: I thought we would start with

02:28:02.729 --> 02:28:09.819
a sandbox that I like to play in, and I think
you are a black belt where I am still a middling

02:28:09.819 --> 02:28:14.690
white belt, and that's a discussion of language
and words.

02:28:14.690 --> 02:28:19.280
And specifically, I was hoping you could perhaps
give us some examples of words that we use

02:28:19.280 --> 02:28:27.780
commonly that frame the way we think about
life or relate to life in the West, if that

02:28:27.780 --> 02:28:31.530
is perhaps a container that works for the
question.

02:28:31.530 --> 02:28:35.080
John Vervaeke: Yeah, I think it is a good
container.

02:28:35.080 --> 02:28:42.530
I think there are words that are signposts,
benchmarks for a kind of cultural cognitive

02:28:42.530 --> 02:28:47.320
grammar, giving us some sort of our basic
conceptual vocabulary.

02:28:47.320 --> 02:28:54.150
Two that come to mind very readily are these
two terms, subjective and objective, and people

02:28:54.150 --> 02:28:58.870
of course forget the original meanings of
these words, but what we do is we have taken

02:28:58.870 --> 02:29:04.690
them to be an exhaustive division of reality.

02:29:04.690 --> 02:29:09.700
There's the interior subjective mind stuff
and there's the exterior objective physical

02:29:09.700 --> 02:29:16.300
stuff, and we take it that that's just the
way it is and that's how we've always thought.

02:29:16.300 --> 02:29:21.830
And that's how everybody has always thought
about reality, and that's not true.

02:29:21.830 --> 02:29:25.930
The Greeks didn't have that division, and
of course, many Eastern philosophies don't

02:29:25.930 --> 02:29:27.279
have that division.

02:29:27.279 --> 02:29:33.560
And there's been critiques of that framework
which we got from Descartes, the Cartesian

02:29:33.560 --> 02:29:37.660
framework for both the last 200 years, even
in the history of Western philosophy.

02:29:37.660 --> 02:29:42.240
The fact that we have these terms, we bandy
them about, we think that that's all there

02:29:42.240 --> 02:29:43.240
is.

02:29:43.240 --> 02:29:46.600
It's complete and exhaustive and they're divided
and they're really radically incommensurable

02:29:46.600 --> 02:29:48.200
with each other.

02:29:48.200 --> 02:29:53.910
It just shows how well this has insinuated
itself into the very way we see ourselves,

02:29:53.910 --> 02:29:57.980
see the world, experience ourselves.

02:29:57.980 --> 02:30:00.189
It's very powerful and very interesting.

02:30:00.189 --> 02:30:04.840
Part of my work is to try to undermine that
dichotomy and try and get us to think more

02:30:04.840 --> 02:30:10.240
profoundly about that there must be something
deeper than the subjective and objective that

02:30:10.240 --> 02:30:14.090
fundamentally makes it possible for them to
be related together.

02:30:14.090 --> 02:30:18.931
Because if not, we are really doomed in a
kind of radical skepticism and solipsism.

02:30:18.931 --> 02:30:22.760
Solipsism is the idea that the only thing
that's really real is your own mind kind of

02:30:22.760 --> 02:30:24.189
thing.

02:30:24.189 --> 02:30:26.810
So that would be one clear example.

02:30:26.810 --> 02:30:31.970
Another one along those lines that we haven't
paid attention to how much it's changed is

02:30:31.970 --> 02:30:33.300
the word matter.

02:30:33.300 --> 02:30:40.870
We now take matter as actual stuff, but if
you go before the scientific revolution, matter

02:30:40.870 --> 02:30:42.600
in the Aristotelian framework means exactly
the opposite.

02:30:42.600 --> 02:30:49.660
It means pure potentiality, that which is
awaiting to be informed and actualized.

02:30:49.660 --> 02:30:57.340
Aristotle's notion is a piece of wood is potentially
a chair or a table, and what makes it act,

02:30:57.340 --> 02:31:03.020
actuality, what makes it act like a table
is its form, its structure and functional

02:31:03.020 --> 02:31:04.189
organization.

02:31:04.189 --> 02:31:09.370
What makes it act like a chair is its form,
but before that it's just a potential chair

02:31:09.370 --> 02:31:10.910
or a potential table.

02:31:10.910 --> 02:31:12.210
And that's what matter was.

02:31:12.210 --> 02:31:14.050
It was sort of this pure potential.

02:31:14.050 --> 02:31:17.490
And so the word has actually flipped in meaning
radically.

02:31:17.490 --> 02:31:22.370
Then we of course think that it's always been
this way and we forgot that there's a dramatically

02:31:22.370 --> 02:31:25.110
opposite possibility even within our own history.

02:31:25.110 --> 02:31:27.190
So that would be too easy.

02:31:27.190 --> 02:31:34.130
Well, not easy to reflect upon, but easy to
bring up examples of words that are very powerful

02:31:34.130 --> 02:31:39.950
in our culture, but so much so that we see
so much by means of them that they're almost

02:31:39.950 --> 02:31:41.720
completely transparent to us.

02:31:41.720 --> 02:31:48.390
Tim Ferriss: How has your relating to, and
I apologize if the wording on this is clumsy,

02:31:48.390 --> 02:31:55.569
but your relating to your experienced reality
changed as you've studied cognitive science

02:31:55.569 --> 02:32:03.270
and philosophy and these different labels
and frameworks that despite our perhaps underlying

02:32:03.270 --> 02:32:07.000
subconscious belief that it has always been
this way.

02:32:07.000 --> 02:32:15.870
In the case of say, subjective, objective,
how has your own relating to life's experience

02:32:15.870 --> 02:32:16.870
changed?

02:32:16.870 --> 02:32:22.990
John Vervaeke: I coined a term “the transjective”
to mean that which binds the two together,

02:32:22.990 --> 02:32:27.080
that which binds the inner life of the organism
to the outer world of the environment kind

02:32:27.080 --> 02:32:28.080
of thing.

02:32:28.080 --> 02:32:33.170
There I was influenced by a teacher of mine,
John Kennedy, who is a protege of J.J.

02:32:33.170 --> 02:32:34.170
Gibson.

02:32:34.170 --> 02:32:35.170
And so let me give you an example.

02:32:35.170 --> 02:32:36.870
I've become aware of things.

02:32:36.870 --> 02:32:40.220
So Gibson had a notion of an affordance.

02:32:40.220 --> 02:32:43.000
Like so, this water bottle is graspable.

02:32:43.000 --> 02:32:47.580
Now, the graspability isn't in my hand, I
can't grasp anything.

02:32:47.580 --> 02:32:51.390
I can't grasp Africa or something like that.

02:32:51.390 --> 02:32:57.109
The graspability isn't in the bottle because
like an ant can't grasp it and et cetera.

02:32:57.109 --> 02:33:04.050
It's actual, a real relation of fittedness
between my hand and this, and the floor is

02:33:04.050 --> 02:33:06.700
walkable by me.

02:33:06.700 --> 02:33:15.000
And so this way of experiencing this connectedness
as a more profound way of experiencing my

02:33:15.000 --> 02:33:21.260
mental life has now become increasingly prominent
and salient for me.

02:33:21.260 --> 02:33:27.070
It goes towards the main work I do on scientific
work, about relevance realization and things

02:33:27.070 --> 02:33:28.130
like that.

02:33:28.130 --> 02:33:31.620
But it also overlaps.

02:33:31.620 --> 02:33:32.860
I do Daoist practices.

02:33:32.860 --> 02:33:35.070
I do Tai Chi Chuan and Qigong and things.

02:33:35.070 --> 02:33:42.240
And that sense of connectedness that is talked
about, that shift helps me get into that sense

02:33:42.240 --> 02:33:46.530
of connectedness in a more profound and transformative
way.

02:33:46.530 --> 02:33:48.479
So, nobody's ever asked me that question.

02:33:48.479 --> 02:33:50.460
That's a really good question.

02:33:50.460 --> 02:33:56.580
It has really changed the configurations of
my experience.

02:33:56.580 --> 02:33:58.189
What is foregrounded?

02:33:58.189 --> 02:33:59.530
What becomes salient to me?

02:33:59.530 --> 02:34:05.180
What such that I can actually taste it more
in my experience and be transformed more by

02:34:05.180 --> 02:34:06.180
it?

02:34:06.180 --> 02:34:07.180
Yes, very much so.

02:34:07.180 --> 02:34:08.240
That's a very clear example.

02:34:08.240 --> 02:34:12.109
And that, sorry, I'll stop in a sec, but the
way that this —

02:34:12.109 --> 02:34:14.100
Tim Ferriss: — you can go as long as you
like.

02:34:14.100 --> 02:34:19.020
John Vervaeke: Well, that translates in that
really — how do I say this without sounding

02:34:19.020 --> 02:34:20.630
like a Hallmark card?

02:34:20.630 --> 02:34:25.811
But you move to an understanding of love as
this connectedness, but not as the idea of

02:34:25.811 --> 02:34:31.960
connectedness, but as the real reciprocal
opening between you and another person.

02:34:31.960 --> 02:34:35.960
They disclose to you in a way that allows
you to disclose to them, and you're mutually

02:34:35.960 --> 02:34:36.960
affording that.

02:34:36.960 --> 02:34:41.820
And that goes from being something that you
just think to something that you found yourself

02:34:41.820 --> 02:34:44.930
deeply being shaped by as you participate
in it.

02:34:44.930 --> 02:34:50.380
Tim Ferriss: I'm glad I asked, and I hope
to ask — Well, I am going to ask many more.

02:34:50.380 --> 02:34:51.380
We've got plenty of time.

02:34:51.380 --> 02:34:52.970
This is fun.

02:34:52.970 --> 02:34:57.630
I wanted to come back to something you mentioned,
and that is, I'm going to paraphrase here

02:34:57.630 --> 02:35:01.460
because I can't recall the exact wording that
you used.

02:35:01.460 --> 02:35:13.660
But effectively, some interwoven fabric that
is more fundamental than the subjective-objective

02:35:13.660 --> 02:35:16.540
dichotomy that we use.

02:35:16.540 --> 02:35:24.310
I'm curious if that is an older way of knowing
if that is — it's a revisiting of an older

02:35:24.310 --> 02:35:34.670
way of knowing or relating to reality, or
if that is a newer way of thinking about something

02:35:34.670 --> 02:35:41.170
that could connect and underlie those types
of concepts.

02:35:41.170 --> 02:35:45.439
I'm asking in part because I had a conversation
a few weeks ago with Wade Davis, who's an

02:35:45.439 --> 02:35:47.430
ethnographer, and —
John Vervaeke: Yeah, I've read one of his

02:35:47.430 --> 02:35:48.430
books.

02:35:48.430 --> 02:35:53.930
Tim Ferriss: And he's written quite a bit
about different ways of knowing, which I certainly

02:35:53.930 --> 02:35:57.370
want to dive into with you as well.

02:35:57.370 --> 02:36:01.100
Is what you referred to — something old,
is it something new?

02:36:01.100 --> 02:36:05.260
Is it something that has no temporal fixed
point?

02:36:05.260 --> 02:36:08.790
John Vervaeke: Well, I mean, the answer is
both.

02:36:08.790 --> 02:36:11.640
That's not as a cop-out Canadian answer.

02:36:11.640 --> 02:36:15.569
What I mean by that is there's definitely
important providence, and I'll talk about

02:36:15.569 --> 02:36:16.569
it in a sec.

02:36:16.569 --> 02:36:20.920
But I'm not recommending that we simply think
we can return to that in some sort of a historical

02:36:20.920 --> 02:36:21.920
manner.

02:36:21.920 --> 02:36:24.431
So that's what I mean when I'm sort of caveating.

02:36:24.431 --> 02:36:32.460
This notion that our relationship to reality
is more about this connectedness, this is

02:36:32.460 --> 02:36:45.189
obviously in the whole Neoplatonic tradition,
this idea of participation that — one way

02:36:45.189 --> 02:36:49.279
the ancients thought about it is, well, this
has a structural functional organization to

02:36:49.279 --> 02:36:52.660
it that allows it to act the way it does.

02:36:52.660 --> 02:36:54.359
Who best knows this?

02:36:54.359 --> 02:36:55.359
Somebody who could describe it?

02:36:55.359 --> 02:36:57.320
Or somebody —
Tim Ferriss: For people who have audio only,

02:36:57.320 --> 02:36:59.069
this, you're pointing at the water bottle.

02:36:59.069 --> 02:37:01.950
John Vervaeke: Oh, sorry.

02:37:01.950 --> 02:37:04.109
Yeah, sorry, everybody.

02:37:04.109 --> 02:37:06.270
Thanks for that.

02:37:06.270 --> 02:37:08.200
Now, who actually knows this better?

02:37:08.200 --> 02:37:10.319
Somebody who could describe it or somebody
who could make it?

02:37:10.319 --> 02:37:13.630
Well, generally people say, well, somebody
who could actually make it, who actually bring

02:37:13.630 --> 02:37:16.359
about the structural functional organization.

02:37:16.359 --> 02:37:17.779
What does the maker have in their head?

02:37:17.779 --> 02:37:21.569
Well, they have the structural functional
organization and they just have to put it

02:37:21.569 --> 02:37:22.710
into that.

02:37:22.710 --> 02:37:28.960
The thing that makes this act the way it does
is identical to something in your mind, and

02:37:28.960 --> 02:37:30.740
you're both sharing in that.

02:37:30.740 --> 02:37:33.100
This is called a contact epistemology.

02:37:33.100 --> 02:37:36.820
You're actually in — that you're mutually
participating.

02:37:36.820 --> 02:37:39.830
And so the relatedness and the connectedness
is more primordial.

02:37:39.830 --> 02:37:44.939
That's sort of an idea running through the
whole Neoplatonic tradition, which I would

02:37:44.939 --> 02:37:51.680
argue following Arthur Versluis is really
the spiritual grammar of the west is the Neoplatonic

02:37:51.680 --> 02:37:52.680
tradition.

02:37:52.680 --> 02:37:53.680
In that sense, it's old.

02:37:53.680 --> 02:38:00.939
In another sense, it's very new because what's
coming out of 4E cognitive science is exactly

02:38:00.939 --> 02:38:02.439
this kind of thing.

02:38:02.439 --> 02:38:04.430
This has to do with ways of knowing.

02:38:04.430 --> 02:38:09.870
I talk about, because of the work I do in
4E cogsci, four kinds of knowing, and maybe

02:38:09.870 --> 02:38:11.260
we can get into that.

02:38:11.260 --> 02:38:15.850
But the point is that three of those four
kinds of knowing are about this kind of connectedness

02:38:15.850 --> 02:38:18.050
and mutual participation.

02:38:18.050 --> 02:38:24.360
And so I find this very interesting, the sort
of cutting edge cogsci is arguing that this

02:38:24.360 --> 02:38:29.319
is a better way of — even to use the metaphor,
your mind is not in your head.

02:38:29.319 --> 02:38:32.090
It's between your embodied brain and the world.

02:38:32.090 --> 02:38:36.820
That's where the mind is, and that's how you
should think of the mind.

02:38:36.820 --> 02:38:41.270
That's really cutting edge and important,
and there's a lot of important work being

02:38:41.270 --> 02:38:42.270
done about it.

02:38:42.270 --> 02:38:46.640
But in a lot of ways it hearkens back to that
ancient Neoplatonic tradition.

02:38:46.640 --> 02:38:51.560
It's both old and new in really, really persuasive
ways, I would say.

